      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>BOVRIL,</head>
        <p>BOVRIL,</p>
        <p>Which are a most important item in the week's menu of every household, are made most piquant and tasty by the addition of</p>
        <p>Which is the guaranteed product</p>
        <p>RIME OX BEEF and which imparts the flavour of Fresh Meat to even the most tasteless dish.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>IT is hard to realise that time flies in a dentist's chair.</p>
        <p>"Do you laugh when you write a joke?" inquired his friend. "No," replied the humourist ; "but I smile when I sell one."</p>
        <p>"IF there are holes in your clothes, why don't you complain to your wife 2" "Least said soonest mended.</p>
        <p>She: " Have you ever loved another?" He: "Yes, of course. Did you think I'd practise on a nice girl like you?" CUSTOMER (looking over the stock: "I can't see a useful thing in all your stock !" Silversmith: "Of course you can't! These are all wedding presents."</p>
        <p>ALGIE "I wonder if Miss Rippingal has learned ' to ride a bicycle yet. Have you seen her lately ! " Cla ud : " No ; I believe no one is permitted to see he but the doctor." Too TAME.—Manager: "Going out, sir? What's the trouble? Don't you like Wild West' • show?" Brooklyn Man: "No ; I expected to see thrilling scenes of danger, and hair-breadth escapes, but they're nothing to what I go through every day crossing the street opposite the Town Hall!" CHANGE OF HEART. —Deacon Skinnem : "I can't tell you how blessed lam in, my son now. You know I always had a trouble getting him to go to church, but of late he has been going willingly, not only on Sundays, but on week-days. He never misses a service." Neighbour "Your son, is in love with _Miss De Poore, who sings in the choir."</p>
        <p>Deacon Skinnera : " What ! Doe's he intend to marry that poverty-stricken minx? If he goes near that church again, I'll disinherit him !”</p>
        <p>DEsPorism IN BLIJE.—A newly-created rural policeman, puffed with a sense of his authority, was pompously strutting round his beat when he saw two men standing at the street-corner. Marching up to them, he said—" Move on, there and don't be gathering a crowd." "Two is not a evowd," said one of the men. "Yes, it is," snapped the man of law; " and, remember this, one's a -crowd if /say so!" THE BOY HAD AN IDEA.—The kid had smashed his father's shaving mug and done sundry .other damage wben his mother discovered him. "Oh, Freddy?" she exclaimed, horrified; "what will your papa say when he comes home and sees what you have done ? " "Well, mamma," he replied, with a half smile, "I don't think I would like to repeat it before you." "How is your wife?" "Urn! Her head has been troubling her a good deal this year." "Ner- vous headache?" "Not exactly. She keeps on wanting a new bonnet every few weeks." " VELLY WELL."—Julian Ralph, when he went to China, prepared himself very carefully in pigeon English, which he had been told he would find useful, and on discovering a Chinaman in his bed- room at a hotel in Shanghai, remarked: " Hello ! What ting ? What fashion man you belong? , 'What side you come ?" To which the Chinaman replied: "This is Mr. Ralph, I presume. We have mutual friends who suggested my calling on you. I spent eight years at school in Norwich, Connecticut." "Ah !" said Mr. Ralph, partially recovering his presence of mind; velly well, velly well."</p>
        <p>HEARD NEAR THE SILVERY TAY. —Those who Itre acquainted with the filthy state of the River Clyde will best appreciate the following dialogue— Man Donald, they tell me that's the finest salmon river in Scotland," said a worthy who hailed from Glasgow, and was on a visit to his friend in Perth. "It's a' that," replied Donald, as they both walked leisurely along the banks of the river Tay. " Weel, dae ye ken, Donald, a' the time I've been here, I've never seen a single fish'iump oot the water, and in the Clyde, above the harbour, -yell see them jumping every ither meenit." " Nae doot," answered Donald, "for I'm shair they'll be glad tae get a mouthful o' fresh air."</p>
        <p>"I have come to ask for your daughter's hand. "Oh—well, you can't have it. i'm not doling out my daughter on the instalment plan. When you feel that you can support the whole girl you may call again."</p>
        <p>A Bons DIPLOMAT. —Charley was caught nap- ping on the porch of the summer resort. • A pair of soft little hands covered his eyes, and a sweet voice commanded : "Guess who it is." Nothing very dreadful for Charley in this, you think; but then you don't know that Charley was engaged to two girls, and, for the life of him, couldn't decide which voice it was, which made it a very embarras- sing situation for Chancy. A wrong guess would lead to complications awful to think of. But a happy thought inspired Charley, and he announced: "It's the dearest, sweetest • little girl in all the world." "Oh, you lovely boy V whispered the satisfied one, as she removed her hands.</p>
        <p>AFTER DARK. —lt was a silvery voice that sounded through the darkness: "Oh, Jack, don't let me tip over! Oh, it's turning! Merciful heavens! Remember, Jack, I weigh nearly one hundred and thirty pounds, and if I fall"— "Don't he afraid," came the floating assurance; "I will hang on till death ! " Suddenly a scream and a scramble rent the air, as two bicycles cracked together. "Jack, are you hurt?" No answer. "Jack, did the wheels hurt your head—are you stunned?" Still deep and utter silence. Then she gave a pathetic little wail. "Oh, Jack, Jack, did I hurt you—did I fall on you?" And out of the dusk came a faint but forcible, "By Jove, you did !"</p>
        <p>NOT SURPRISED. "My dear sir," said the doctor, as he left his patient to speak to her hus- band, "I am afraid your wire's mind is gone." Husband (in no way startled) : "I am not sur- prised, for she has been giving me a piece of it almost every day since we were married, and that's fifteen years ago."</p>
        <p>WlLY.—Gipsy fortune-teller: "I am sorry to tell the young lady that she will pass through a serious illness in her twentieth year." Lady, forty, if a day) : "Good gracious !" Sighs and gives the gipsy half-a-crown).</p>
        <p>BADE THEM PAUSE. —A rather ludicrous CC/11- tretemps took place the othe,r Sunday in a certain village church in the north. The beadle, who is accustomed to sit immediately below the pulpit, is known locally as the owner of the biggest mouth in the district, VIA the poor man is hardly to blame for that. On the Sunday in question the beadle unfortunately felt sleepy, and yawned at a very critical and impressive part of the minister's ser- mon. "Pause, my brethren, before it is too late," exclaimed the preacher, "or you may be dragged into the abyss which is even now yawning before you." The official felt deeply hurt, and the con- gregation think that perqonties from the pulpit should be avoided if :2.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Critehley's " STAIUM tiLo6B Is uneqaalled for imparting, a beautiful finish to starched ,men. /cc., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr.—. Corn- wall. says:—" My wit) will not fihish our linen without your "Starch Gloss.'' Frem Mrs —, Bradford :—Dear sir,— lam pleased to inform you that I i.ave tried many so-calle4 Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find one equal to yours." It should be übed in ever:. house. Sold at id., 3d.. and 6d., by mast starch sellers. If not in stook will procure it. r.l. CRITOBLEI, Blackburn.. Proprietor.—Advt.</p>
