      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MT DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>An extremely stylish and becoming hat is of white felt bound and trimmed with velvet, either bright green, violet, petunia, or black; and with six black tips, four at the left side, two under the brim at the back, where they are secured with a knot of velvet or a cluster of violets and leaves.</p>
        <p>A lovely little toque is in cerise mirror velvet, edged with chenille and sequin insertion, and trimmed with black ostrich tips and paradise ospreys. The tips are cleverly arranged to droop on to the hair at the back.</p>
        <p>It seems an accepted fact that bodices of different materials (and in many cases, colour), from the skirt with which they are worn, are to be more fashionable than ever during the approaching winter, and therefore the blouse is not by any means likely to suffer even a temporary eclipse, although during the cold weather it will not be so much in favour as the heavier, closer-fitting, and more elaborate bodice.</p>
        <p>A stylish blouse made in the new pine pattern velveteen or flannels is with full fronts, and a sort of cascade frill of the velveteen lined with plain- coloured satin on either side of a wide vest of closely pleated soft-brocaded satin or silk, while a third model has a vandyked yoke of braided and embroidered face-cloth, the centre point of which is carried right down the front. Tulle is still in vogue for evening gowns, and is to be had in every possible shade of colour. The newest has bright silk stripes, with little lace flowers in between; this makes a very charmirg dance frock mounted over bright-coloured silk.</p>
        <p>A really pretty dressing-gown is of rose-coloured cashmere. The deep tabbed collar and the sleeves are trimmed with inch-wide black satin ribbon. Dinner and theatre gowns have the bodices made with high necks and long sleeves, a . remarkably sensible fashion and a generally becoming one too. I must tell you about a very fascinating design I saw last week, especially as, although they were marvel of daintiness, it was not intricate in con- struction, and could be managed by an intelligent country dressmaker, or by a clever amateur. It was of white chiffon over white silk, the silk under- bodice close-fitting, the chiffon set full on it in plain gathers at neck and waist, and over it was a rounded zouave pieces of white kid, which is quite the latest novelty. It is cut in an openwork design, much resembling Byzantine lace, all the edges outlined with the finest gold twisted thread, and the surface sewn with "jewels" of palest green and pink. The kid openwork is laid over a web of lace stitches, and the whole thing was most elegant. The long sleeves are of rippled chiffon over close-fitting ones of white silk, with big double-looped " bow " puffs at the top, caught with a 'cut steel ornament that flashed with " diamante" sparks. There is a soft folded neck- band of chiffon, and above this a pretty turreted tucker of chiffon, narrower at the front and sides, and rising into a high fan-iike pleating at the back. The waistbelt is of rather wide white atin ribbon, tied in a 'dainty bow at the left side. The artificial flowers this season are particularly beautiful, especially the velvet violets and pansies in lovely shades of mauve and purple, and, the velvet geraniums in scarlet and Danish red.' Heliotrope is another favourit flower, and ohry- santhamums are made in all sorts of rich autumn shades. A rich wine or claret colour, and shades ot petunia, or reddish heliotrope, are the most popular in velvet, and these are .trimmed with chinchilla or mink. Costumes for outdoor, wear are exceed- ingly simple, and the skirts are much narrower than they were last autumn, while velvet gowns, intended for receptions and other occasions, where a handsome indoor toilette is needed, are richly emblVdered with sequins and gold thread, and in some cases glistening paste dewdrops are added with good effect. A model underskirt in pale pink satin is trimmed with creamy Valenciennes lace, run with white satin ribbons, and the corsets should be to match. The. true Parisian always likes all her lingerie to match, and some of these sets are made in delicate coloured satins and brocades. Anothei' gown of prepossessing appearance is of bright green tulle over satin in the same shade_, the upper part of white satin embroidered with gold wire and jewels, and with a jewelled fringe. This was something like a yoke; but it was laid over the tulle, and left loose at the front and back, where the fringe fell, but joined in at the shoulders and armholes, the full tulle falling from under it and slightly overhanging the waistbelt of folded green satin. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
