      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>ONE MOMENT PLEASE!:</head>
        <p>ONE MOMENT PLEASE!:</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gr vel. Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back Dropsy. Wind and Water Complaint. Diseas.s Kidneys t.ladder Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a pr eitive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and it not satisfied your money will be returned. Prices Is. In all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps. from HoLnuovn's medical Hall Checkheateu, Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-st.. werringtca. Don't be put off, if you cannot get them write tothe Proprietor and ta box will be sent tacit post- - • vt</p>
        <p>OBACCONIBT9 ounmeneing ; write for Illustrated Guide „no Catalogue (259 pages) 3d. TOBACCONISTS' ()InFirrixe 188, Huston-road, London. Manager, HENRY MYER&amp; A dvt</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Her Majesty, according to present arrangements, will leave Windsor Castle for Osborne on the 18th inst., where she will receive a family party includh3g the Duke and Duchess of Connaught. the Princess Louise and Marquis of Lorne, and the Duchess of Albany and her children. There are, however,to be no festivities of any description during the winter residence of the Court at the Isle of Wight, owing to the deep mourning of Princess Henry of Battenberg. The Queen is now a great-grandmother twenty times told, the latest arrival being the little son of Prince and Princess Henry of Prussia. Prencess Henry is the second daughter of our beloved Princess Alice (Grand Duchess of Hesse) and sister to the Czarina. She married in 1888 her cousin, Prince Henry of Prussia, only brother of the German Emperor. Until the birth of this son the Prince and Princess had only oue child, who is terribly afflicted, being deaf and dumb. It has been stated in some of our London papers re- cently that the trophies which the Queen wins at agri- cultural shows and fat stock exhibitions are melted down and gradually absorbed in more artistic pieces of plate, but this is not so. The cups are largely used for table and sideboard decoration, especially at Osborne and Balmoral, whither it is inconvenient to carry aliny of the treasures which are permanently kept at Windsor Castle.</p>
        <p>By the way, the royal borough of Windsor would appear to be a perfect haven of rest and peaco,and alto- gether a.most desirable place to live in, now a few re- gulations by sanction of the Secretary of State have been made. Anyone playing musical instruments, sing- ing, or shouting continually can be prosecuted, nurses and others may not wheel mail carts or perambulators abreast of each other on the footpath, and mlreover no one may beat mats or rugs,wash their windows or shop fronts after 9 a.m. ! _</p>
        <p>_ Speaking of cleaning windows reminds me of that useful but much to-be-dreaded implement, the hose. There is,of course,nothing like it either for quickness or for thoroughness. but it requires to be used with discre- tion, and with a proper regard for public convenience. Cleanliness and godliness are apt to be dis-assoniated when the unwary pedestrian is played upon,and trade?- men who employ this method of washing their windows ancipavementslshould instruct the operators to remember that human beings do not need to be watered. One morning,early last week,having sallied forth earlier than is my wont. I inadvertently came within range of a par- ticularly large sized hose, in the charge of a playful youth,and the result was such that I there and then made up my mind to refrain from emulating the early bird for the future.unless far away from the region of shopland. It is however, of quite common occurrence for accidents of this sort to happen, and it would appear about time some of the careless operators who appar- ently have a notion that the early morning ,pedestrian is not worthy of consideration were taught to think differ- ently upon this particular subject. The Princess of Wales kept her birthday at Sandring- ham, surrAundet by a thoroughly family party, and as her Royal Highness is happily once more in the enj.y- meat of excellent health and spirits, the occasion was a particularly festive one. The scene within doors at Sandringham,is one of supreme cosiness. The furniture is most luxurious, and wherever you may go, upstairs, downstairs, and in my lady's chamber, there is a profu- sion of the most beautiful palms and flowers. Their Royal Highnesses are simply adored round about their Norfolk home, and deservedly, for they make thicir peo- ple's troubles their own, and never forget a kindly face, however humble. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Christmas present giving is indulged in to a consider- a'Ae.extent by our Royal Family, and already some of her Majesty's daughters and granddaughters have been seen making their purchases at those establishments in Louder, :ionoured by their patronage. And trulT, never was " present hunting" made mere delightful than it is this year, not only to the upper ten thc.‘yeand but to foik.on a lower rung of the social ladder. \ Several of the large drapery establishments have a cosy little tea room, Where the fragrant beverage and dainty cakes may be enjoyed at a small charge,and for those who re- quire it.a handy little restaurant,where a li,ht luncheon mac be partaken nf is to be foond. There never was too, such a thlieitful year for the little people. A few days back, accompanied by two small connections, I visited three or four of our largest West End shops, with a view tl securing treasures des- tined to adorn a forthcoming Christmal-tree, and cer- tainly the afternoon was a most enjoyable one, from every point of view. At one establishMent we were treated, free of charge, to an exhibition of the Cer.ema- toscope, and at stother, to a wonderfully interesting exhibition of mechanical toys. A ballet dancer pir- ()netted on the point of one toe, and performed the most admirable and graceful sweeps and turns,a big bear held a mirror in one Paw whilst with the other he brushed his hair carefully. and two new women "in " rationale" puffed cigarettes, put up their eyeglasses, and went through all the actions of a languid conversation. There were also cats that jumped, elephants that swung their trunks, cows that moved, lions that roared, and last,but by no means least, a splendid assortment of games and books.</p>
        <p>--iii;.r.opos of children's books, it may interest some of my readers t: know that Hans Anderson, that friend of little people who delight in fairy tales, lies buried in the cemetery at Copenhagen. Two fine yew trees stand guard over the tomb,and a few roses wander caressingly over it. On the headstone is cut in Danish some words ,from one of his own works. "It is the soul which God has created in his own image, and which cannot die ; this, the seed of eternity, liven with us through lift ; as our body alone dies, so our soul alone lives." Tailor made gowns of box cloth, or covert coating, are donned by all the best dressed women for walking and day wear, and really if girls with a limited dress a lowance would only screw up the courage to save,and invest in a well-built specimen, they would appear 'dressed to perfection upon less than is usually expended in odds and ends to "do up" various costumes which prove anything but suitable for outdoor wear. When choosing cloth for winter gowns, it is advisable to un- ravel a corner, and pull out a few threads. Cotton can be detected at once, it is so even and stiff. A good cloth is made entirely of wool, an inferior one has cott._ n in- terwoven with it, or is made of old used woolen goods Ithat have been pulled 'o pieces and cleaned, dyed and respun. Old wont breaks very easily and has no resist- ant power when the threads are suddenly stretched. It wauld appear as though tailor made frocks are a very ancient institution indeed, for according to a dress- maker's bill, which has lately been found in an ancient temple of Chaldea, relating to a great lady of the land, some 92 dresses and tunics perfumed with myrrh, aloese and cassia, were delivered to her by an individual of the masculine persuasion, somewhere about 2,500 B C. Thf bill is a particularly heavy one, the gowns of" cloth o gold "or " Samite" evidently costing more, far more than the most expensive modern gowns. Although it is no longer permissable to indulge in the well-loved ribbon bow at the back of the neck, we may yet arrange to have it in front, and thereby give a dainty finish to perchance a middle-aged frock that has lost most of the charms it possessed "once upon a time." Most of these neck stocks are built upon buekram, and fasten at the back of the neck. This is a far better arrangement than tyinr the ribbon how each time, a mode of procedure which means speedy ruination to the ribbon.</p>
        <p>An excellent sandwich, for delicate persons who do not care for fat in any other form, can be made of thin slices of the best bacon, broiled or cooked until crisp, and well-browned. Lay the slices while hot, between thin slices of white or brown bread. lhese sandwiches will he found very appetising. Hard boiled eggs grated and mixed with a little olive oil, salt and pepper, are. also wholesome and excellent if spread on thin sli,e9 of bread. :" MADGE. 7</p>
      </div>
