      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>-CANTAL" CIIAT.</head>
        <p>-CANTAL" CIIAT.</p>
        <p>From a conversation I have had with an English- man who has just returned from the Transvaal I Rather that the anti-Downing-street sentiment in South Africa is much stronger than Mr. Cnamber- lain in his recent answer to a correspondent is ap- ,Parently willing to admit. My informant, an Englishman engaged in business, and a trained ob- server. tells me that in Johannesburg itself there is no fear that President Kruger will attempt to repudiate the Convention or will take any step in the least calculated to bring about a racial war be- tween British and Dutch in South Africa. All the 'alarming reports pointing to this circulated from time to time are merely intended to influence the :share market, and do not in any way represent the real state of affairs in the Trsnsvaal. The real danger to the Nmpire in South 4frica is not Presi- dent Kruger or the Transvaal Government, but the ever-deepening sentiment among men of British 'blood throughout South Africa that things will not mend so brig as Downing-street remains the pivot 'of government. The alternative of a South African Republic•or a Federation on Republic lines is freely discussed, and my informant, who claims to be a patriotic subject of the Queen, says that from his 'experience of South Africa he cannot blame those who look to this solution of the difficulties beset- ting South Africa as the only one which offers any hope of a permanent settlement. There remains, of course, the great question as to whether British or Dutch influence is to predominate, and the solu- tion of that question is admittedly full of difficulty. 'These are the views of an intelligent business man who has, so far as I am aware, no reason to distort or misrepresent facts, and they are at least worthy of being put on record, if only because they are in such striking contrast to the optimism of Mr. -Chamberlain's recently expressed views.</p>
        <p>The complaints made by many of the clerks of the Post-office of the slowness of promotion have. led the Pastmaster General to look into the sub- ject,with the result that some of the senior officials will have to retire. The Duke of Norfolk has is- sued an order that retirement at the age of sixty will be compulsory,except in cases where the maxi- mum superannuation would not be obtained. To meet this, complu.-ion will not be used until forty years have been completed in the Department. pro- vided the men concerned are efficient. Where it seems desirable to retain an official after he has reached sixty, the permission of the Postmaster General must be obtained. All who are now be- tween the ages of sixty and sixty-five have re- ceived notice that in twelve months they must re- tire.</p>
        <p>The London municipal band have become im- mensely popular' and it is necessary to devote larger sums each year to meet the demands for free music. When the scheme was first started In 1892 over £3,000 was spent in providing music for three months of the year. This last summer £7,021 was spent, although rather more was allotted. Part of the surplus will be devoted to adding to the musical library which has been started. Next year it is proposed to spend T 7,800 on the bands, the performances commenc- ing in May and going on till the end of August. It is interesting to note that ever £2OO has been forthcoming from the sale of programmes and £4OO from the letting of chairs at the perform- ances.</p>
        <p>Lord Farrer sent the following pungent criticism of the" Landlords Relief Bill " to some of the Lon- don newspapers :—"We ought, I suppose, to be thankful for small mercies, even though bestowed on us in our own despite, and I therefore wish to tender my hearty thanks to Mr. Chaplin for the relief he has given me by the Rating Bill of last Session, which I did my best to oppose. On look- ing through the list of agricultural ratepayers in my parish, I find that I am the ratepayer who re- ceives the largest relief; that the next is the manager of a growing and apparently prosperous industry; that the largest farmers in my parish re- ceive less than a fourth of what I receive, whilst to many of the poorer ratepayers the relief becomes almost imperceptible. The sum I receive myself, which is about a fourth of the annual pay of a weekly labourer, is scarcely enough to induce me to take more land into cultivation, or to abstain from reducing my expenses, 'as lam trying to do Still, as a poor agriculturist who carries on a very un- profitable business for his own amusement, I ara grateful for very small mercies.and am not without bopes that a further development of the policy of ~ Mr. Chaplin's Bill may relieve me from all rates on -, my house and gardens. At, all events, it is a com- fort to me to think that the relief given to me,such . as it is, is given at the expense of my neighbours 41/4 in London. who, to judge by the amount which they are raising by rates for improvements of all kinds, must have a great deal to spare for us poor villa owners,"</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, London celebrated the birthday of tha Princess of Wales. Bands played, the church bells rang marry peals,flags were hoisted, and guns fired. The Princess is undoubtedly the most popu- lar member of the Royal Family. She has always been a favourite, and attempts which are now and again made to disparage her only increase her popularity.</p>
        <p>I hear that the great difficulty which the doctors !lave in the treatment of Dr. Jameson is his inabil- ity to take food. For a month before the operation he lived entirely on milk. The adoption of this re- stricted diet xv ias due to the fact that he suffered greatly from indigestion. Again, the evil in this i respect s rather aggravated than otherwise by the necessity which has arisen for procuring him sleep by means of a nardotic.This insomnia has also been persistent for sometime, and is largely attri- buted by the doctors to the compulsoiy confinement and want of active exercise acting upon one who had been accustomed to a vigorous outdoor exist- ence. Dr. Jameson, I understand, has repeatedly expressed to his dlctors that he does not desire to be released on the ground of his health. He is anxious to complete the whole term of his ininrison- meat. 06 the other hand, the opinion of Mr. Her- bert Allingbam, the surgeon. and of Dr. Scott, for- tified by that of Sir William Broadbent, was to the effect that his removal from Holloway Gaol is abso- lutely necessary..</p>
        <p>Are we to have "Gas and Water" Home Rule from the Unionist Government? The leading articles in the " Times " on Tuesday calling strongly for a reform of the present system of pri- vate legislation points to something of the kind. It has excited a good deal of comment and surmise. The old hardship imposed on remote municipalities in Ireland when they have to come all the way to Westminster to promote their little local bills is an old grievance,and wrluld not excite much sympathy even in a Government determined to kill Home Rule by kindness. But the small instalments of political reform in municipal matters which even the Pre- sent House of Commons has managed to insert in these private bills has aroused considerable feeling in Unionist quarters. The Tory members of the House of Lords, with the Lord Chancellor at the head, complained loudly when, after rejecting a public bill for municipal reform, they found then- selves being circumvented bit by bit by clauses inserted into private bills which they dared not re- ject, and they would be prepared to swallow some far-reaching reforms which would put a stop to this system.</p>
        <p>The progressive party in Methodism are deter- mined to take time by the forelock in the proposed •change in the order of the Wesleyan Conference sessions, and notably that the President should be nominated by the Representative Session. With a view to ascertaining definitely the views of the great body of Wesleyan ministers on this, which not a few regard as a most revolutionary change, Mr. H. P. Hughes has, by private circular,addressed his brethren, and in response has received up to the present replies from about eight hundred ministers. The results are not yet made public, but I am in a position to say that, roughly speaking, a hundred are in favour of the election ot president taking 'place in the Representative Session, 200 are stoutly opposed to this propcoal, and about 100 other replies may be classed as " doubtful." It is probable that the remaining SOO who are in full circuit work and the more than 300 sup.4rnunierary ministers may go in largely for the status quo.</p>
        <p>ROWLANDS' MACASSAR OIL is the onlyl reliable and genuine preserver and beautifier of the hair; it prevents it falling off or t irning grey, and eradicates Scurf and Dandriff is also sold in a Golden Colour for fair haired ladies and children. Bottles. 3s, 6d., 78. ; 10s. 6d. equal to four small.</p>
        <p>Ro'.v ANDS' ODONTO is the best and safest dentifrice which can be used, as it contlins no acid, mineral or gritty ngredients ;it whitens tbe teeth, prevents and arrests decay and sweetens the breath. 2s. 9d. per box, Sold by Chemists, Perfumets and Stores; can be sent post free by A. ROWLAND &amp; Sorts. 20. Hatton Garden, Loudon, on receipt of advertised prices in postal order.</p>
        <p>WA.ERINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. In the absence of the Mayor, the deputy Mayor (Alderman Greenall) presided. There were also present Aldermen Sutton, Wright, Bolton, Councillors Crosfield, Shaw Green, Plumpton, Smet- hurst, Bennett, Longton, Evans, Ellison, Tinnionjolley, Baird, Shillcock, Grace, Monks, Garner, Fleming, Lee, Heaton, Rlberts, Cannel!, Rogers; Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr. Longdin (!nrough surveyor), and Mr. Haddock (gas engineer).</p>
        <p>THE LATE LADY GREENALL AND MAJOR PICK:MERE. The following letter was received from Sir Gilbert Greenall in regard to the vote of condolence passed by the Council on the death of Lady Greenall :—" Kindly convey to the Town Council our thanks for the very kind resolution passed at their meeting on November 9th.— Believe me, yours truly, GILBERT GREENALL."—Mrs. Pickmere wrote as follows :—" Dear Mr. Mayor,—l have delayed writing, scarcely knowing how I could suffi- ciently thank you for your kindness. I wish, on behalf of myself and my sons, to express our' gratitude, and will you please convey our thanks to the members of the Town Council for the vote of condolence aad sympathy so beautifully expressed and copied, which was sent to me OR the death of my dear husband. It will ever be a great consolation to me to remember the universal kind- ness that has beea shown to me in my sorrow by all friends and the public generally. We all fully appreci- ate the kind words which came from the Mayor and Corporation of Warrington, his native town, being from friends with whom he had been so long and intimately associated. We were all deeply touched by it.—With our united kind regards, I remain, Mr. Mayor, yours truly, MARGARET Plekmaai." On the motion of the DEPUTY-MAYOR, seconded by Alderman SUTTON, it was decided that these two letters be recorded on the minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WATCH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WATCH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee it was resolved that the following members be appointed a sub-commit- tee to assist the Chief Constable in carrying out the duties of the police under the Licensing Acts :—Coun- cillors Dr. Gannett, Heaton, and Monks. The minutes were confirmed, on the motion of the DEPUTY-MAYOR, seconded by Alderman SUTTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>STRTET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>STRTET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that the following members be appoi Ited the Buildings Sub-Committee: The-Chairma,n, the Deputy-Chairman. Aldermen Bolton and Wright, Councillors Furness and Longton. It also recommended that the plans of the following I,buildings, &amp;e., be approved :-11 houses and stable in Hale-st.,for Mr. John Appleton; iron shed at 82 Longford, for Mrs. Mary Bloomfield ; "Old. Crow" Inn. Fennel-st., re- building! • new boiler house at Bank Quay, for Messrs. J Crosflecd and Sous, L;mited. The minutes were confirmed on the motion of Alder- min SUTTON, seconded by Alderman WRIGHT. Councillor BENNETT said it would be interesting to point out in reference to these minutes the large number of streets which had been named in accordance with the suggestion of the late Dr. Kendrick—that was.that they bore the names of Warrington worthies. Dr. Kendrick's idea was that by using these names for the streets they would bs perpetuating them, and doing honour to those who by their services to the town deserved some recognition. (Rear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>HEALTIT COMMITTEE. -THE SMALLPDX HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>HEALTIT COMMITTEE. -THE SMALLPDX HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>This committee, at a meeting held on the 25th Nov., resolved that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to advertise for tenders for the erection of a smallpox hospital according to the plans then submitted. The minutes were confirmed, on the motion of Alder- man SIITTON, seconded by Councillor BAIRD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE.-THE PAIL SYSTEM</head>
        <p>SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE.-THE PAIL SYSTEM</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 20th November it wis re- solved that the Town Clerk serve notices upon the owners of property in the area recently added to the borough requiring them to convert all closets on the midden system to the pail system. The tender 4f Messrs. William Coates and Son to supply slops for the night soil men at 83. 2d. each was accepted. The minutes were confirmed, on the motion of Coun- cillor MONKS, seconded by Councillor EVANS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TSB COST OF STREET MAKING</head>
        <p>TSB COST OF STREET MAKING</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee related principally to the 'final apportionments of the cost of ferming certain streets and passages. The confirmation of the minutes was proposed by Alderman WRIGHT, seconded by Councillor BELTOR. Councillor BENNETT raid he had received complaints that in the paving of streets the streets were not taken in rotation. He thought that the oldest streets should be paved first. Alderman WRIGHT replied that as a rule the commit- tee took the oldest streets first,and then dealt with those that most needed attending to. Those that were made very decent were allowed to remain longer than those that were knee-deep in sludge. (Hear, hear.) He thought the committee might be trusted not to force the property owners unduly, but the action they took was forced upon them by other committees, and they took every precaution to deal withl those cases first which most needed attention. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The minutes were confirmed. salon ATTENDANCE COMIITTEE.-.EFFECT OF EXTENSION</p>
        <p>OF THE EOUNDARIES.</p>
        <p>This committee reported that the average attendance at the public elementary schools d 'rimy. the mouth of October. 1896, was 9.224 ; corresponding period last year, 8,728 ; increase, 496. Councillor SHAW GREEN movel the confirmation of the minutes. He remarked that now the extension of the boundary was complete the committee had had to undertake a canvass of those parts of the town that hitherto had not been in the borough, and that they had already discovered a considerable number of children who were not atteiading school at all. There were from 350 to 400 more children on the school books, and Mr. Clark, one of the echoolmasters. stated that a great im- prevemenk, had already taken phce at his school at Great Sankey. There were 342 children at the examina- tion for labour certificates on IVlanday, mailing 600 alto- gether who had sat for labour certificates, which proved the necessity of having a second. examination. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SHILLCOCIK having seconded the motion, the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>MUSEUM, LIBRARY, ARTS AND TECHNICAL INTRUCTIMq COMMITTEE.-THE CONTINUATION SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The Minutes of the above committee were of a lcnethy and interesting character. The following sub-commit- tees have been appointed, the chairman to be ex-officio member of each :—Museum Snb.Committee : Mr. Greening' (convener), Messrs F. W. Monks, Peers and Roberts ; Art Gallery Sub-Committee : Messrs. Roberts, Owen, Woads, Dutton, Bennett, Crosfield ; In- titnte Sub• Committee : Messrs. F. W. Monks, Roberts, Shaw Green, Furness. Plumpton, Woods, Ellison, Owen. Peers, and Jolley. Gifts and loans wore reporzed, and a list of books purchased submitted. The following ex-</p>
        <p>tract from H.M. Inspector's report on the continuation schools received from the Edacation Department, was read :—" The school has been taught with gratifying sticoesg. With WPII directed efforts the instruction has been made practial and thorough, and secured the in- terest of a large number of pupils, who by their quiet demeanour and evident earnestness have contributed in great measure to the very satisfactory standard of pro- ficiency attained." It was reported that the number of students present in each department of the School of Art, &amp;c„ was as follows ;—Art 28, science and technical 46, commercial 24. and continuation 137. total 285. 3UneillOr ROBERTS, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, observed that the report of Her Majesty's inspector with regard to the Continuation Schools josti- fied the work of the committee, an 4 it would be as gratifying to the vaembers of the Council and the inhab- itants of the borough as it was to the committee itself. (Annlause.) Councillor MONKS seconded, and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>From the report of the Registrar and the Cemetery Committee it appeared that, the interments for the past four weeks were—Adults,39 ; minors, 79 ; stillborn and rorematurp,l2 Corresponding period last year—Adults, 33; minors,76 ; stillborn and premature,B. The receipts for the past four weeks were £95 is. ; corresponding period last year, £9l Bs. 4,1. The minutes were confirmed on the motion of Coun- cillor EVENS, seconded by Councillor SHILLCOCIC.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL, PARK AND BATHS COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the above committee it was resolved that the tender of Mr. R. Johnson to supply the neer.... sary office furniture required at Bank House at £127 2s. 7d. be accepted. Alderman BOLTON moved the confirmation of the min- utes,and alluded ta the Gardens Sub-Committee. The name of Conneillor Smethurst had been accidentally left off the list,and the committee now consisted of Aldermen Bolton and Francomb and Councillors Dr. Cannel! and Heaton.</p>
        <p>Councillor HEATON seconded, and the minutes were agreed to.</p>
        <p>MARKETS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting. of—th;--c-n-m-mltt—e-e--it—Was reported that the total incline from all sources for the previous month was £345 129. 43., corresponding period last year £346 6s. 7d. The minutes were approve&amp; on the motion of the DEPUTY-MAYOR, seconded by Councillor FLEMING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>GAS COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>GAS COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>The minutes were of a routine character, end, on the motion of Councillor HEATON, seconded by Alderman BOLTON, they were approved.</p>
        <p>THROAT IRRITATION AND COUGH.—Soreness and dryness, tickling and irritation, inducing cough and affecting the voice. For these symptoms use Hpps's Glycerine Jujubes. In contact with the glands at the moment they are excited by the act of sucking, the Glycerine in these agreee,le confec- tions Ircomes actively healing. Sold only in boxes, 10.. tins Is. Id., lapelled "JS EPPS 8c Co., Ltd., Hommo- pathic Chemists, London." Dr. Moore, in his work on " Nose and Throat Diseases." says: "The Glycerine jujubes prepared by James Epps and Co., are of undoubte -I service as a curative or palliative agent," while Dr. Gordon Holmes Senior Physician to the Municipal Throat and Her Infirmary writes "After an extended trial, I have found your Gly- cerine Jujubes of considerable benefit in almost all,iformslof throat disease." (No. 1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>RIVER q COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>RIVER q COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>This committee's minutes the appointnient of Councillor Roberts as chairmansand Councillor Pierpoint as deputy-chairman of the committee.—The minutes were passed withsut clulment on the motion of Council- lor Roberts seconded by Alderman Bolton. FIRE ENGINE dOMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee reported that Mr. Alderman Hutchin- son was appbinted chairman, and Mr. Councillor Smet- hurst the deputy-chairman of this committee for the en- suing year. The Superintendent's report that a fire had occurred at Throstle Nest Farm, Orford, the premises of Mt. W. Smith.—The minutes were confirmed on the motion of Cuuncillor Smethurst, seconded by Councillor Heaton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The following gentlemen have been appointed a Man- aging Sub-committee :—The Chairman. the Deputy- Chairman,'Councillors Evans, Fleming, Garner, and Shaw Green. . . _ .</p>
        <p>The minutes were agreed to, the mover being Council- lor Luz, and the seconder C)uncillor SHAW GREEN. THE GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>Mr. THOMAS MOR.RIS, F.G.S. has beca appointed chair- man of the Gymnasium Committee, and Councillor Tinninn deputy-chairman. The sub-aommittee consists of the Chairman, the Deputy-Chairman, Messrs. Daniel Jones and Hugh Ferguson, and the Scretary. It has been resolved that the use of the Gymnasium be granted to the members of the police force tor two afternoons in each week during such hours as the instructor may au." Point ; that the reading room be supplied with news- papers and books : that the best thanks of the meating be given to Mr. D Jones for his gift of a clock for the institution ; that the subscription to the ladies' class be red aced to 10a. for the season ; and that boys under 17 years of age be admitted every Wednesday and Saturday evening at a charge of a I.d. each night. Councillor TINUON moved the confirmation of the minutes, and observed that this was the first, time the minutes had had to be brought before the Council for confirmation. Reference would be found to the granting , of permission to the police force to make use of the Gymnasium two afternoons in each week. That, he contended, was a very wise departure, because gymnas- tics were necessary for almost everybody, especially for men who had the quiet occupation usually carried on by the police. (Laughter.) The Gymnasium would be especially useful to the recruits of the force who, if they had a training !there, would be more fitted for their ' duties. The committee were not charging any- thing to the Watch Committee for the use of the gymnasium and the apparatus, and in this respect he thought they were setting other committees an example that might be followed with advantage by not claiming "the pound of flesh "other committees seemed to claim (Laughter.) Councillor Tinnion also alluded to the resolution with respect to boys and the use of the build- ing. The Gymnasium under the old management was not able, owing to want of funds, to do is much good in the town as was desirable, and the expenditure of £lO or £2O a year coming from the funds of the borough would not b 3 very much felt. The committee would be glad for the members of the Council, in a generous mood, to popularise the institution,and have books and tickets to distribute amongst boys with that object in view. The thanks of the committee and the Council were due to Mr. D. Jones for his gift of a clock to the institution. (Hear, hear.) Alderman WRIGHT seconded. Councillor SMETHURST hoped the representatives of the press would note the fact that the subscription to the ladies' class had been reduced from 15s. to 105. for the season. The minutes were then confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>COUNCILLOR LONGTON RISES TOO_ LATE</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR LONGTON RISES TOO_ LATE</p>
        <p>Councillor LoNGroT rose to ask a question in reference to the minutes of the Health Committee, but was ruled out of order, the Deputy-Mayor observing that, Coun- cillor Langton should have been in his place.</p>
        <p>THE MISUSE OF OPEN SPA.CES.-A LIVELY DEBATE.</p>
        <p>The General PurposPs Cimmittoe recommended that the following members be appointed a sub-commmittee with full powor to put in forco the powers and provisions of the Local Gavernment B'ard's Provisional Orders Confirmation (No. 2) Act, 1895, relating to tie widening of Bridge-street. reporting to the C'unoil from time to time thereon : The Mayor, Alderman 131ackhurst.Bolton, Wright, Councillors Shaw Green, Heaton, and Roberts. It was further decided that a special committee consist- ing of the following members of the Council, be ap- . .</p>
        <p>pointed for the purpose of considering the propriety of applyinc, to the Board of Trz.,de in pursuance of the Electric Lighting Acts, 1882 and 1888, for a Provisional Order to sunpl7 electrical energy for public or private purpoAes within the borough, viz :—The Mayor, Alder- men Bolton, Sutton C.mncillors Bennet', Crosfield, Evans, Heaton, Monks, Pierpoint, Roberts, Smethurst, and Tinnion. The DEPUTY MAYOR moved, and Alderman BovroN seconded, the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor BENNETT said he P7ar, sorry to interrupt the smooth flow of the proceedings.but he was rather afraid that they were that evening proceeding with greater celerity than ability. nnd l w%.s sorry Councillor Long ton had been Moved ot 17 of order. Councillor LoWnots again rose to speak. The DEPT/TT-MAYOR: You ought to have been in time. Your are out of order. .</p>
        <p>- Councillor BENNETT ; It i 3 the result of rushing the business. The DEPUTY-MIYOR : I object to that. It has not</p>
        <p>been rushed through. Councillor ROBERTS: I also wish to take exception to that remark. There is a proper and wrong way of do- ing business, and if members ot the Corporation are not aware of it, it is quite time they were. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LO*TOTON : Allow me to explain, Mr. Deputy-Mayor. iam sure that if you heard me you would not rule me out of order. The DEPUTE-MAYOR : I cannot allow the discussion. You are out of order.</p>
        <p>_ _ Alderman BOLTON: Bring the matter up at the next meeting. Councillor BENNETT explained that he did not use the word " rushing" as a reflection on the Chairman, but the whole tendency of the meeting was to gallop over the business. Councillor HEATON • Withdraw. I don't think you ught to charge us with rushing the business as long as it is done in the proper way. Councillor BENNIirk proceeded to refer to the appoint- ment of the sub-committee of the General eurposes Committee`to look after open spaces,and said that strong remonstrances had been made to him with reference to Rowley Fair, and to the other representatives of the w. rd. Councillor FLEMING said the matter was being dealt with by the Markets Committee. Councillor BENNETT said he now undei stood the mat- ter was under the control of the Markets Committee,and ' that they intended to deal with it. He should like to make a suggestion—as he had done before—that real music should be provided instead of the hideous din of the hurdy-gurdy whicn made night and day deplorable at present. (Hear, hear.) Occasionally they might have some b4nds on the open spaces to contribute to the plea- sure of the inhabitants of the locality. Then with re- gard to the widening of BriAge-street, he asked if the sub-committee of the Street Improvement Committee should not have been deputed to look after that matter instead of a sub-committee of the General Purposes Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Councillor HELTON : I think you are wandering away from the minutes. I don't see anything referring to the matter.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT I think if you would observe the rules you are so ready to accuse others of breaking it would conduce to the despatch of business. Councillor HEATON : I think the committee is quite capable of looking after the matter. Councillor BENNETT : You are out of order. I cannot</p>
        <p>see the logic of the matter being under the control of the General Purposes Committee. lam pleased a sug- gestion of mine has been carried out and that a special</p>
        <p>committee has been appointed to look after the qu'estion of electric liehtins.7.. Alderman BOLTON said the committee which had been appointed to look atter the widening of Bridge-street was a capable committee. The General Purposes Clip- mittee was supposed to be composed of the brains of the Council and old and experienced men. (Laughter.)</p>
