      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 4, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, DECEMBER 4, 1896.</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>THIS AHHOUNCEMENT ENTMES YOU TO A DAINTY SAMPLE TIN OF</p>
        <p>Councillor GRACE said in Orford ward the people had been afflicted with the very nuisance Councillor Bennett had complained about. The Corporation bought an open space for the purpose of a public recreation ground. It had never been used for that purpose, and lately there had been held there a cattle fair and a pleasure fair with tnat engine-driven music. (Laughter.) Such a nuisance ought not to be allowed in any respectable community, and the sooner it was done away with the better, and the open space devoted to thd benefit of the public. (Hear, hear.) Councillor FLEatING said the subject came before the Markets Cornmi'tee on Monday. The committee were trying to deal with it, and they would be able to report to the Council at its next meetine what they intended to do in the fu ure. Alderman SUTTON, referring to the remark of Council- lor Bennett as to the appointment of a sub-committee for the widening of Bridge-street, said if he would notice the names on the sub-nommittee already apoointed he would see that the Street Improvement Committee was v,ry well represents d, four being members of that com- mittee. In addition there was Councillor Shaw Green, ' the chairman of the Finance Committee, and at the present juncture it was more a question of finance than anything else. It was more a matter for the Finance Committee to deal with than the Street Improvement or any other committee. At the same time he thought it was the intention that the sub-committem should consist of representatives of the various committees who were interested in the widening of the streets, and in order that it might he carried out according to the Act of Parliament. He did not think there was anything to complain about as far as the Street Improvement Com- mittee were concerned. He, as chairman, was very well satisfied with the procedure, and he thought all would come out well. Everything that was done would be brought at the proper time before the Council for con- sideration. • The minutes were confirmed. The Council theta rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>MAYORA.T. "AT HOME."</head>
        <p>THE MAYORA.T. "AT HOME."</p>
        <p>Of the msny successful mayoral " at home's "in the history of the Warrington Corporation, perhaps none have been so well attended as that which was held in the Town Hall on Thursday afternoon last, when the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Fairclough) received visitors from 3 o'clock until 5.30. As on previous occasions the entrance hall,the reception and adjoining rooms were tastefully deco- rated and presented a bright and attractive appear- ance. The work had been placed in the hands of Messrs. Hesketh nnd Dutton, and they are to be congratulated on the way the decorations were carried out. The plants and thwers had been selected from the greenhouses of Bank Park by the head gardener, NIT% Parker, and some tine specimens of chrysanthemums were on view. Music was dis- coursed at intervals and the instrumentalists were accommodated on a raised platform in the vesti- bule. Mr. Pearson supplied an interesting pro- gramme of classical and other music tor 'cello, piano, and violin, the performers being : 'cello,Mr. Ter Poorten ; piano, Mr. W. H. Payton; violin, Mr. R. H. Pearson. The refreshment buffet was in the charge of Mr. Powis, the caretaker of the Town Hall. Appended will be found a list of the ladies and gentlemen who either called or left cards :</p>
