      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>A.STONISHINti AFFAIR NEAR WA RRINGTON, CULCHETTI GARDENER'S ESCAPE. SPECIAL INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>A.STONISHINti AFFAIR NEAR WA RRINGTON, CULCHETTI GARDENER'S ESCAPE. SPECIAL INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>Culcheth is by no means an easy place to g Lo,but a I eporter of the "Warrington and Mid-Cheshire Ex- aminer"having been informed of the astonishing story of , Mr. James Parker,Fligh Lodge,Culcheth, set out the other day to hear from his own lips an account of the affair. After devious journevings the " Examiner " representa- tive arrived at High Lodge, and enquired for Mr. James Parker who, he learned, was at his work and would not be back till night. There was nothing for it then but to go after Mr. Parker,and the reporter eventually found him at work among his greenhouses. Mr. Parker, a genial and highly intelligent man, on learning what was required of him, immediately ex- pressed his willingness to let the press and the public know the full particulars of his escape from the jaws of death. He briskly walked back to the lodge, and on ar- rival explained the object of the reporter's visit to his wife.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Parker proved equally ready to oblige and helped her husband to give the following narrative: " It is," began Mr. Parker, "about seven years since I first began to suffer frcm spinal disease. For five years I was unable to work, and three years of that time I was treated at one of our leading hospitals. I was so badly paralysed that I could not mo4e a foot,and got worse instead of better at the hospital. I came home, believing as I remarked to my wife, that "I had come home to die." I had all the usual symptoms of paralysis—loss of power, inability to stand in the datk, and great pain in the limbs and back. The pain was in- tolerable at times, and medicine appeared to do me no good. While in bed a friend sent rrul a Northampton paper containing particulars of a case somewhat similar to my own. This patif nt was known to my friend, who could vouch for the truth of his remarkable recovery through the use of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I decided to take the pills as a last resort, though one doctor at the hospital had told me I was in- curable and should never walk agaih. Well! I procured a box of Dr. Williams' pills from Warrington. I took three daily and afterwards increased the dose to six a day, taking two aftet each meal. When the first box was finished I was able to move my toes, which I had been unable to do for months. When I commenced, it would be about August 1893, and by March of the fol- lowing year I was walking nicely, to the surprise of everyone.F,r the last eighteen months I have not needed any and I can now walk miles. A) Jam 45 years old I am past the age at whicl . sufferers from spinal disease usually re 'over."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Parker interposed with the remark " It has been a blessing for us that we ever tried them," and her hus- band concluded by saying "I cannot speak too highly of the Pills. But for them I should have been in bed yet."</p>
        <p>All the above facts were given without the slightest hesitation whatever, and the reporter left High Lodge firmly convinced that Mr. and Mrs. Parker had the soundest of reasons for their unswerving bPlief that the wonderful recovery of the former was entirely due to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cure not only cases like the above, but all the many dis- orders which arise from an impoverished state of the blood and nerves, such as anremia, pale and sallow com- plexions, green sickness, general muscular weakness. de- pression of spirits, loss of appetite, palpitation of the heart, shortness of breath, pains in the back, nervous headache, loss of memory, early decay, all forms of fethale weakness, hysteria, paralysis, locomotor:ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; also of all diseases arising from vitiated humours in the blood, which cause scrofula, rickets, hip-joint diseases, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs. They invigorate the blood and system when brolpsn down by overwork, worry, diseases, excesses and indiscretions of living. These pills are not a purgative medicine. They contain nothing that could injure the most delicate. They act directly on the blood. In this way the blood, becoming " built up," and being sup- plied with its lacking constituents, becomes rich and red and nourishes the various organs, stimulating them to activity in the performance of their functions, and thus eliminating diseases from the system. These Pills are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by Chemists, end by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, Lon- don, RC., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 93. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are never sold in bulk, or from glass jars and the like, and any dealer who offers substitutes in this way is trying to defraud. and should be avoided like any other dishonest person.</p>
        <p>For the past quarter of a century there as been the contiroinus flow of letters bearing testimony to the truly wonderful corPs effected by CLARKE'S WORLD-FAMED BLOOD MIXTTTRIC, "the finest Blood Purifier that science and medical skill have brought to light." Sufferers from Scrofula. Scurvy, Eczeme, Bad I egs, Skin and Blond Diseases Pimples, and Sores of env kind, are solicited to give it a trial to test its value. Sold everywhere. at 2s. 9d per bottle Beware of worthless imitations and substitutes.—Advt. (6)</p>
        <p>Mrs PONSONEIVR HAIR RESTORKR, is great dlscoery, a Most Refreshing Wash, Marvellous Results, Innumetable Testimonials, Patronised by Royalty. In great demsnd by Professionals, makes the 1-fair Grow, prevAnts -Bald- ness, Invigorating, restores hair to its naturalcolour, gives healthy and youthful appearance. Mostastonish- ing results ; 28 6d Bottle, post free.—A deb s;ssMrs. Pox- SONBY, 25, Lime-st., Liverpool—Advt.</p>
