      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HUMAN SCOURGE</head>
        <p>THE HUMAN SCOURGE</p>
        <p>• Weak and disordered kidneys are the cause of over 60 per cent, of the deaths annually reported. This is a startling fact. Kidney diseases are decidedly on the increase. Most of the attacks of Influenza of recent years have left weak and affected kidneys. What can be done to stop the progress of these diseases ?</p>
        <p>Remember that kidney disease neglected leads. to Bright's disease, a very serious and deplorable mal- ady. Tbe world never had a true specific for kidney disease until the discovery of Warner's Safe Cure, and to-day it is the standard remedy for kidney diseases in England, Germany, France, and in fact, all over the wcrld.</p>
        <p>Don't trifle with health. Don't neglect the first symp- toms, but treat yourself at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WATIMNOTON CRICKET LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WATIMNOTON CRICKET LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FIRST ANNUAL MEErING.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening last the first annual meeting of the above league was held in the Lecture Hall, of the Y•M.C.A. Mr. T. Morris president, occupied the chair, and Mr. E. A. Sanders (general secretary) was also pre- sent. There was a fair attendance of members of th. clubs affiliated with the league. Altogether there are about 250 cricketers associated with the different clubs. so that the league is by no means an uninfluential body</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>REPORT OF THE PAST YEAR</head>
        <p>REPORT OF THE PAST YEAR</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY read his r,.p )rt, giving a review of the season. be could safely say that both in the carryir.g out of the season's working and from a cricketing point of view, and last, but not least, from a financial point of view, they had come through the ordeal in a most satisfactory manner. (Applause.) This success had been mainly brought about by the energetic members of the- committe, and by the deep and kindly interest the president and vice-presidents had taken in the league during the past year. (Applause.) After referring to the sportsmanlike feeling which had pre- vailed through all the matches, Mr. Sanders went on to say that out of 40 matches arranged only one had to be cancelled, and that through bad weather. (Applause.) The result of the matches was that the Sinkev Wire Mills Cricket Club were proclaimed the champions of Warrington. (Applause). Out of a possible 16 points the winners had obtained 14, the remaining two being lost owing to the cancelled fixture referred to. Their min.,- dangerous opponents had been the Cheshire Lines C.C., with 10 points; St. Elphins came next, then the Y.M.C,A., and bringing up the rear were the Sankey Wesleyans Club. The Warrington Cricket Club were in favour of the League, and the committee were willing to grant them the use of the ground for special matches. (Applause.) Mr. Sanders was also thankful to say that after all the expenditure had been reckoned up, and it was rather heavy, they could start another year with a balance in hand of £1 12s. 4d. (Applause.) He con- cluded by wishing that all would work harmoniously together and further successes for the league would be assured. (Applause)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Ms following officers were unanim•usly elected for the ensuing year :—President, Mr. T. Morris, F.G.S. - vice-president 3, Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P., Councillor Wil, ham Pierpoint,, J P., Mr. A. Browne' Mr. Walter Brough and Mr. George Walker ; treasurer, Mr. Hill ; secretary, Mr. E. A. Sanders ; committee, the secretary of each of the 6 League clubs. _ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DLETRIBUTION OF MEDALS.</head>
        <p>DLETRIBUTION OF MEDALS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman then distributed the medals which had been won by the Sankey Wire Mills Club. Each of the following players received one :—Messrs. W. Woodcock, F. Williams.W. 13ailey,J. Moss,A. Marshall, S. Hobson,J, Evans, J. Helsby, T. Simpson, W. Crosskill, and J. W. Warburton. The PRESIDENT said the question had been often of late crossing his mind as to whether the young men ot to-day were not paying too much attention to football and cricket. It behoved all to see to the education of the mind aes well as the body if Englishmen were going to be ,L1)le to bold their own ag,tin,, foroign norap7ti- tors. (Bear, hear, and applause.) Mr. Morris added at the close that if any young man would show that he.was unable to pay the entrance fees at the technical school, and was anxious to join, he would be pleased to pay them himself. (Loud applause.) Mr. F. WILLIAMS proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman for presenting the medals, and for his kind words,and to the umpires for the fair way in Which they had endeavoured to do their duties.—The vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR ENTRANCP</p>
        <p>An application to enter th-;1-eaguf_‘ for next season was read from the Firth Mattress Co:, who were forming' p club to be called the Firth Cricket Club.—On the motion of Mr. Williams, seconded by Mr. Crampton, the club were unanimously elected members of the league. The Sankey Wesleyans Cricket Club having,as wooden spoonists, had to retire from the league, were re-elected without dissent. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR PU DELICATE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>PURIFIED PETROLEUM FOR PU DELICATE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>IT is usual nowadays when children are fail and do not thrive on their ordinary food to feed them on some one of the almost innumerable artificial foods. There are many cases, however, where a medicine is needed as much or more than a food. For such cases (in fact for all delicate children) Angier's Petroleum Emulsion, the food medicine, is a remedy superior to any other. Infants are especially liable to intestinal disturbance. These the Emulsion corrects, while at the same time it supplies the food value of an oil and the bone-making properties of lime and soda, Its effect upon delicate children is oftentimes marvellous. Their food will at once agree with them better and be perfectly digested, they eat better, digest better, sleep better, and they gain in weight, colour, and general health. Give it to the children when they cough, are scrofulous or losing flash, when their food does not digest, or when their bowels are out of order. Children like to take it and it always does them glod. It is extensively prescribed by the medical profession for all kinds of wasting diseases of children. Sold by all leading chemists, 2s. 9i. and 4s. 6d. A sample bottle sent free on receipt of 3d. to cover postage. Descriptive pamphlet post free. Angier Chemical Co., 32, Snow Rill, London, E. C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH MERTING.—A. meeting of the Parish Council was held on Friday night last. There were present Messrs. John Whitlow (chairman), Pemberton. Roughsedge, Ashton Hayes, Pearson, Broady, H. Eyes, W. T. Davenport, and Mr. Ellison (clerk)—It was decided to appoint the Chairman and the overseers of the parish as trustees of Johnson's charity.—The Clerk reada letter from the Clerk to the Runcorn District Council with reference to the application of the Parish Council that the District Council should repair the footpath near the London Bridge Inn, stating that the Parish Council were empowered to undertake the repair and maintenance of footpaths, and he did not see sufficient reason to take the matter out of the hands of the Parish Council.— Mr. Pearson said the matter had been referred to the District Council because there was a ditch there which carried away the surface water from the highroad. He moved that the Clerk write to the Courey Council point- ing out the unsatisfactory i. condition of the ditch, and how by its encroachment it made the footpath danger- ous, and asking them to mprove the drainage and repair the footpath ; and further that he write to Mr. Geo. Warburton (Mr. Egerton-Warburton's agent) asking him to leok at the ditch, and he might contribute to- wards the repair of it. The cost would be about £15— Mr. Pemberton seconded. He objected to the Parish Council spending any money on the repair of the path. Its present condition. was not caused by wear and tear, but by the water from the drain.—Mr. Ashton Hayes was of opinion that if they applied in a straight for- ward manner to Mr Wirburton he would do the necess- ary work. The 7 need not apply to the County Coun- cil.—Mr. Pemberton : It is all very well for Mr. Warburtou to do this, hut will he always keep the path in repair ?—lt was eventually decided to write to Mr. Warbuton only, an( f to ask him to meet a Aeputatien of the Council. consisting of the Chairman, Messrs. Daven- port, Pearson, and Warburton.</p>
