      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>Two or three causes have combined Dr.RJealemaesesr. s lately to again draw attention to</p>
        <p>South African affairs. There is, for instance, the release of Dr. JAMESON. It will be remembered that the now famous ex-adminis- trator of Rhodesia was sentenced in July last to fifteen months' imprisonment for the part he took in the Transvaal raid. About four of these months have expired and he is now at liberty. We have not heard or read any adverse criticism of the action of the HOME SECRETARY in remitting the remainder of the doctor's sentence. Nor is there likely to be any such criticism. Dr. JAMESON'S health appears to have completely broken down during his not very prolongsd sojourn in Holloway Gaol, and Sir MATTHEW WHITE RIDLEY in open- ing the prison doors has only acted upon the advice of the eminamt medical men who were asked to report upon the case: When the HOME SECRE- TARY released certain" dynamiters " on the plea of ill-health his action was generally approved by the Liberal party—though it was not so well received by some of his own party—and nobody wanted him to deal more severely with Dr. JAMESON than he did with the dynamiters. On the whole it can be said that all the would-be " raiders " of the Transvaal have been treated with exceptional lenity considering the gravity of their offence. This is no doubt largely due to the fact that the Opinion is almost unanimously held that the real contrivers or instigators of the raid have not been brought to justice at all. When the news of the expedition was first received in this country at the beginning of the year there were many people who shrewdly suspected that the reasons which were put forward to justify such an impolitic and unlawful enterprise formed by no means the real or trustworthy explanation. Almost everything that has been made public since then, and especi- ally the notorious cypher telegrams, has helped to confirm these suspicions. It has long since been proved that the protection of the women and children of Johannesburg was not the pre- dominant idea of the originators of the invasion. Nor was the wish to establish or secure British ascendancy their inspiring motive. Some of them, it turned out later on, were not even British subjects, but alien, or, at all events, cos- mopolitan financiers, who were evidently deter- mined by fair means or foul to seize a controlling power over the gold mines of the Transvaal. If anybody calculated upon raising British prestige in South Africa by means of this ill-contrived raid they must now feel sadly disappointed, for it seems to have had the extremely opposite effect. Mr. DIAMOND, formerly a member of the House of Commons, who is now travelling in South Africa, is extremely severe in his comments upon the stupidity and mis-government of the Boers. hut at the same time he sees very clearly that the JAMESON raid has had the effect ot strengthq'.Pg what may be termed the Boer element not on]..; in the Transvaal ut throughout the whole of South</p>
      </div>
