        <p>Africa. Long before the raid took place there was a strong reform movement in Johannesburg which Ivas supported even by some of the more enlight- ened Boers. There were men in that movement Who were prepared to go to the extent of a revolu- tion in order to secure what they considered to be their rights as citizens of the Transvaal /tepublic, but who directly they heard of the incursion by the armed forces of the Chartered Company offered their services to President XitUGER'S government in order to repel the inva- ders. Some of these men are of British blood, but they preferred the rule of the Boers,defective and almost intolerable as it might be in some tespects, to that of a chartered company of specu- lators. Certain other results of the invasion of Which Dr. JAMESON was the leader, have been pro- ttinently mentioned this week. President KRUGER hail definitely stated that a claim for indemnity is to be made against the Chartered Company for the damage that was caused by their troops. This indemnity, he is good enough to say, will not be "too large," but for all that it will probably amount to a very substantial sum. The Chartered Company have no option but to pay. Then,in addition to that the Company will have to undergo the ordeal of an inquiry by the committee appointed by the House if Commons. It has been intimated in a very vague form that there is to be submitted to this committee some extraordinary evidence which Will show that certain leading politicians or diplo- Izatists had a prior knowledge of the invasion. It will probably turn out that this is only another attempt to whitewash the Company at the expense of others.</p>
        <p>THE fight on behalf of voluntary A Revolt of School Managers. schools has taken a rather serious turn in a populous part of the East end of London. A body of managers and trustees of voluntary schools have there combined together to defy the law—that is to say, they have refused to pay the rates which are due or overdue upon their schools. This is surely a singu- lar position for members of the party of law and order to take up. The schools in question, some of which owe between £lOO and £2OO in rates, have been very leniently dealt with in the past. Until quite recently they have almost entirely escaped the payment of rates. The overseers have always shrunk from proceeding to the last resort in such cases—that of distraint. But their hands have at length been forced in a rather Peculiar yet effective fashion. Some time ago co East-end vestrymen—who are now described as two "Parish Hampdens "—announced that they Would refuse to pay their rates so long as the managers of the schools were allowed to escape scot free. The spirit of lawlessnesss was evidently spreading, but the overseers have proved equal to the occasion. They summoned the two "Parish Hampdens " and they summoned the school managers at the same time. This week there has, therefore, been quite a formidable array of parsons before the magistrates who deal with these rate cases. The general excuse for non-pay- ment was" poverty," but magistrates who deal With rate eases are pretty well hardened against this plea and in every instance the order went forth that the rates must be paid in seven days, or, in case of default, there would be a visit from the broker. The plight of the school managers, Who have no doubt done the best they could under the difficult circumstances in which they were placed, again shows the chaotic condition into Which the education system is getting in some Parts of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BANKS AND THEIR CUSTOMERS.</head>
        <p>BANKS AND THEIR CUSTOMERS.</p>
        <p>HANKINSON v. PARR'S BANK, LIMITED. At Manchester ASSiZOB on Tuesdsy, before Mr. Justice was called. The kennedv and a special jury, this case Plaintiff .Thomas Hankinson,producemerchant and Com. Mission agent,Manchester,sued Parr 's Banking Company for damages for breach of duty as his bankers. Mr. Wharton and Mr. Hibbert Ware appeared for the plain_ tiff; Air. C. A. Russell, Q.C., and Mr. Mansfield for the defendants. The plaintiff's case was that in December, 1895,he made an arrangement with the manager of the defendants' bank at Hyde's Crogs that he should have an overdraft to the extent of £lO above the surrender value of certain policies of insurance which bedeposited. On the 10th of October, 1896, the surrender value of the Policies was £205, and the plaintiff's account was over- drawn to the extent of £2Ol. On that day the plaintiff drew a cheque for £3 in favour of a customer, and the Cheque when presented was dishonoured by the bank. Mr. ganhinson said the customer in question had not done any business with him since. Up to that time he had been a good customer. The defence was that the bank never made the ar- rangement alleged by the plaintiff. Their contention Was the plaintiff was to ha an overdraft, first of £l2O and then of £l7O. On the 7th of October the plaintiff'. account was overdrawn to the extent of £2Ol, and th, Manager on that date told ie plaintiff that no furthe, Cheque would be honoured which would increase the amount of the overdraft. The hearing of the case was not finished when the Court adjourned till Wednesday morning at half-past ten. The hearing of this action was resumed on Wednesda, and the jury found for the plaintiff, and awarded hin, e2O damages.</p>
        <p>THE WEST CHESHIRE BREWERY COMPA NY, LIMITED,</p>
        <p>This is the title of anew company to be forme —full particulars of which will be found in nu advertising columns—for the purpose of acquirim and extending the business of Messrs. Gatehous and Son. Limited, brewers, Birkenhead. The issu is of £130,000 four per cent, irredeemable fir, Mortgage debenture stock, and 10,000 five per cent cumulative preference shame of £lO each. Th, breweryiis situated at Trantnere, near Birkenheac and the business has been estabtished for man Years, which it is stated is a profitable and stead') • Progressive one. The present company is incor Dorated for the purpose bf developing and enlar. inQ the business, and to provide iunds for th Llcquisition of additional properties. The COM bally will acquire free from eicumbranct t'he freehold brewery and land, together wit ,lixed plant, &amp;c., 50 freehold licensed houses, 1( 'Assehold licensed houses,one on annual tenancy,anr freehold shops and houses. The company eo i'loys no managers in any of its licensed housesith whole being let to bona-fide tenants. The price be paid to the vendors for the business and all tI r 4 connected with it is .£310,000 payable as to -80.000 in ordinary shares, £20,000 in preference ',hares, rind the balance in cash. The report of th, ?aditori shows the company to ,have been doing glerative business. The subscription list is no, and will close at or before 4 p.m. to-da_ r-riday).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WWNES.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening next, Mr. J. Le Breton Ham- mond is announced to lecture on the" Edueatiou Qued- don," in the Liberal Club Regent-street. BRUNSWICK IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above erociety was held on Wednes- day in the schoolroom under the presidency of Mr. T. &amp;mister. Mr. J. Littler read a paper on "An Ideal's Influence." A discussion followed.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The usual P.S.E. in connection with this place of wor- ship, which shoulol have been held on Sunday evening, did not take place owing to the indisposition of the pas- tor, the Rev. J Simpson. The eight new offertory bags, a thanks offering from Mrs. J. B. Chadwick, were used for the first time. They are made of satin lined with silk, and have brass bandies, with_filigree work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RIINCORN PRIZE CIZO-IR.</head>
        <p>RIINCORN PRIZE CIZO-IR.</p>
        <p>As will be seen in another column this choir will Rive its second concert ot the season in the Public Hall, on Wednesday evening next, when it will pertorm Handel's "Judas MaccatTeus," being assisted by a professional band, and a number of principals, all of whom, are new to this town. Tbs programme submitted is art attrac- tive one and should draw a large audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE.</p>
        <p>The report of the work of the district nurse shows there have been 19 cases (6 being new cases this mouth) on the books from October 25th to November 25th, viz. : —2 shronie, 1 typhoid, 7 medical, and 9 surgical. Of these 4 are convalescent and 1 is dead, which leaves 14 still on the books. Patients have received occasional visits. Number of visits paid to cases, 160 ; number of occasional visits, 61 ; total, 221. RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.</p>
        <p>The third lecture of the series is announced for this, Friday evening in the art room of the Technical Institute. The • subject to be introduced is " How our fathers played and sang," the lecturer being Mr. W. I. Argent, of Liverpool. Th syllabus gives promise that the question will be dealt with in a very interesting and instructive manner. A large attendance is expected as th subjnt is a very popular one. DRUNK AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday before Mr. P. Speakman and Mr. F. J. Norman, John Henry Green was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge- street the previous day. From the evidence it appeared that the prisoner was in the Egerton Inn occupied by Mrs. Waterworth. He was drunk and there refnsed to be served with drink.---P C. Williams was called to eject him,after which Green became very disorderly in the street.—A fine of 54. including costs was imposed. ST. JOHN'S BAND OF HOPE, RUNCORN. A meeting of the above Band of Hope was held in the Schoolroom, Victoria-road, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening. Mr. Jepson occupied the chair, and gave a very earnest address on the cause of temperance. Dur- ing the evening the following took part in the pro- gramme: Glees by the Band of Hope choir and glee party ; recitations by Emily Leach, Charles Bancroft, Cissy Moston, Miss E. Gatley, William Farrell, Albert Fox, Edward Gatley, Maggie Beswick, Maude Galley; songs by Miss Maggie Davies, and Mr. M. Thomas; selection by Misses Warburton. A collection was made on behalf of the temperance cause. SIR JOHN BRUNNER, M.P., AND EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner has just made another generous offer towards School Board educatioe in hi @ constitu- ency. The Runcorn Guardians are desirous of sending the workhouse children to Dutton Board School to be educated, but:the school authority refuses to receive them because there is no room. The Local Government Board decline to permit the guardians to pay a contribution to- wards theVnlargement of the school, and the Education Department say they cannot compel a school to receive additional children when there is no accommodation for them. Sir John Bruuner has solved the difficulty by offering several hundred pounds to Dutton School Board for the purpose of enlarging the school, but his offer has not yet been considered by the board. flour TRINITY CHURCH, RUNCORN.—SALE OF WORK. The annual sale of work in connection with Holy Trinity Church, for the benefit of the home and foreign missions, and for parochial objects, was held in the school on Thursday afternoon. There was a fair attendance. The Rev. Dr. Preston was prevented from being present through indisposition. The opening cere- mony was performed by the Rev. Canon Wood. The stalls and stallholders were as follows :—Fancy stall : Mrs. and the Misses Preston, Miss Fisher, and Miss A. Warner ; plain stall : Mrs. Fisher and Mrs. Barker ; re- freshment stall : Mrs. C. Timmins, the Misses Tyrie and Matthews ; toy stall : Misses Ivy Derbyshire, Hilda and Jennie Waterworth. There was also a Christmas tree in charge of Miss Miriam Derbyshire, and Mr. Sane Ra.ndles personified Father Christmas. In the evening there were musical entertainments and Professor Miller gave his amusing sketches, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of the order on Saturday night under the presidency of Mr. George Holt, a paper was read by Mr. James Johnson on "Loss of character in connection with drinking." 'rhe writer at the out- set referred to the crime caused by drinking habits of the people, and alluded to the terrible sufferings of the wife and children of the drunkard. He said that educa- tion of itself was not a safeguard against drunkenness, and stated that the people must be prepared for legisla- tion on the question, but it must not be in advance of public opinion. After the reading of the paper there was a discussion, in which the following took part : Messrs, George Holt, Edward Key, W. Bisbrown, Thomas Clarke, and James Fowles. A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Johnson for his paper. ST. PAUL'S MEN'S BIBLE CLASS, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the monthly open meeting was held in the Morning Chapel, under the presidency of Mr. B. Knive- ton. There was a crowled attendance. The following interesting programme was gone threugh. Two quartettes, " Calvary " and " Ora pro Noble," were sweetly sung by Messrs. K. Edwards aad L. Matthews, and Messrs. Thos. Johnson and Wns. Coventry. A violin solo was rendered by Miss Temperlay. "Nazareth" was well sung by Mr.rhos.Johnson.Miss Ed- wards and Miss Matthews sweetly sang the duet "Eter- nity." while Miss Temperlev elicted much praise for her rendering of" Daddy." Mr. Edgar Johnson did him- self credit with his solo " Only Tired." and Miss Et. Hill beautifully sang " The Lost Chord." Mr Geo. Bowyer ably accompanied, and also gave a very pleasing organ solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>This society, as most of our readers are aware started this season with an adverse balance of about £4O. At the annual meeting in September a discussion was raised as to whether the society should continue or not. Some were in favour of continuing, notwithstanding the position that the Society were in, but it was only after some debate decided that the Society should go on for another year. Several sums of money were advanced by members of the committee, and Miss Pierpoint who had given tableaux performances in previous years con- sented to arrange for another one to take place this sea- snn. The day appointed for this performance was Tues- day last, and a crowded audience was the result of the .xertions of Miss Pierpoint and friends, as well as the S.cretary, Mr. W. Crosby, in trying to make it a success.</p>
        <p>Just before the commencement of the evening's enter- tainment the Hon. Secretary, (Mr. Crosby) by a request from the performers, spoke on the loss which the Musical Society had sustained by the death of their President (Mr. J. Brundrit, J.P,) He stated that had it been possible to put the entertainment off, it would have been done. But expensive costumes had been ordered from Liverpool, and it would have been impos- alble to do such a thing. The proceedings then opened with an overture, "Masaniello," by the orchestra. "The Mistletoe Bough" 'ahleau was very beautiful, and the performers took heir respective parts in an admirable manner. Miss nlara Taylor rendered "The Promise of Life." The ableau "Freedom and Caught " took very well, as shown by the audience, who kept up the humour for a 'onsiderable time. "The Relief of Lucknow" tableau, ire ved very interestime, and represented the 93rd Sutherland Highlanders at the relief of Lucknow during -he Mutiny. The dresses worn in thiii scene were exactly he same as those worn by the 93rd Regiment. The or- •hestra rendered "Reminiscences of England," in which hey introduced several of the well-known national ,ngs. Mr.Doekrey sang "Come into the garden,Maud." Lod was loudly applauded. The tableaux "The Vil- age Wedding,"" Playing at ball," " The three Maids of 11,,e," were all produced in good part, and were --ry amusing. The evening's entertainment con- Wed with the singing of "God save the goeen." Mr. It. Burkett Musgrove, A.R.C.0.. hly filled the duties of accompanist. The performers ,ore Misses E. Bankes, Barker, M. Barker, F. Briscoe 1, Cameron, M. Deakin, K. Forrester, E. Gregor?. N. fohnson, I Kirkham, N. Lea, G. Morrison,M. Morrison, 1, Pritchard, (Farnworth) M. Pritchard, F. Pritchard, ' Price, G. Rayner, A. Riley, M. Smith, C. Stokes, C. 'otton, Timmins, F. Timmins, P. Timmins, Ivy Tate, (Formby), Messrs. J. Bankes, S. Bankes,Stanley Bankes, t. Bankes, C. Bond, E. Lea. J. Pritchard, (Farnworth), Pritchard, T. R. Pierpoint, R. Timmins, and Mrs, W. I. gay. Songs and recitations were given by Mr. Cyril</p>
        <p>'ha n tier, Miss Clara •Taylor VV. Dookery and Mr. W. K vhite.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Musical Society's rehearsal on Thurs- 'ay evening a resolution exuressing the great loss of the Qr,ciety through the death of Mr. Brundrit was moved v Mr. J. Littler and carried, the members standing hile the resolution was being read.</p>
        <p>The Commissicners who are charged with the admin tration of relief to the starving Armenians met on 'llesday at the British Embassy in Constantinople. It t 3 reported, officially, that no fewer than forty thou- children have been orphaned in Armenia by the iirkish massacres. L sum of least £lOO,OOO is required effectively aid the Armenian sufferers during the win-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EX A MINER F 11,1: Y, DECEMBER 4, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER F 11,1: Y, DECEMBER 4, 1896.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CIRCUIT W.ESLEYIN</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Runcorn District Council was held MI Thursday morning in the council chamber at the Town Hall. Mr. J. W. Lightburn J.P.,presided, and there were also present :—Messrs. S. Taylor J.P., C.C., F. Wiswall, H. J. Grice, G. H. Kirkham, J. Jones, W. Collier, J. Wilson, W. Handley, T. Dean, J. Horsefield, M. Sweetman, D. Bisbrown, E. Imison, F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (secretary), and J. Wildiog (sur- veyor.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Special sermons were preached on Sunday in St. Paul's Chapel, High-st., Runcorn,in furtherance of the cause of temperance. The Rev. 'l'. Brackenbury preached in the morning on "Daniel, an Old Testament abstainer." In the evening the Rev.R.J. Clifton preached on the charac- ter of the men wanted for temperance and all moral re- forms. On Monday evening the annual meeting was held in the chapel. There was a fair attendance. Coun- cillor D. Bisbrown occupied the chair,and was supported by the Revs. H. W. Pates (Widnes), R. J. Clifton, T. Brackenbury and W. George; and Messrs. J. D. Jones, A. R. Norman, E. Marshall, and T. Slushy er. The Secretary (Mr. J. D. Jones) submitted his annual report. It stated that the Temperance Committee of the Wesleyan Methodist Conference reported this year 4,393 Bands of Hope,with a membership of 433,027,an increase of 50 bands, with a decrease of 1,698 members. There were also 1,374 Temperance Societies, with 80,915 mem- bers, an increase of 48 societies and of 5,505 in member- ship. Towards these totals the Runcorn Circuit contri- buted :—Bands of Hope eight, with 1,274 members ; and one Temperance Society,with 466 members. The report went on to express the hope " That careful attention would be given in all their circuits to the work of train- ing the young in the principles of temperance. The terrible record of the ravages of drink in the columns of every day's newspapers was alone a sufficient indication of the need there was that the ehurch should put forth every possible effort in order to combat this evil, and especially to fortify their young people, in order that they might be prepared to resist the insidious and attrac- tive temptations by wnich they were beset on entering into life. The Rev. Sampson Weaver, the temperance secretary for the Liverpool district, reported " Every circuit in the Liverpool district has a Band of Hope with one exception, with 25,230 enrolled members. There are 46 temperance societies, with 3,624 members." These figures Mr. Weaver cannot regard as satisfactory. "Neither can we," he says, " consider them as a true indication of the interest, energy and attitude of the Methodist Church in the district in relation to temper- ance work." Proceeding, Mr. Jones said that in Runcorn we must have felt relieved at the decision of the local bench of magistrates in refusing the application to sell drink in Heath Ward. That " uncontaminated zone " ought to be preserved. One act of the prometers of the mischief will tend to make it more difficult than ever to get support in the tuture, viz. : the publishing of the names in open court of those who signed the memorial to the justices in favour of the licence. It must be stated that to the magistrates alone belongs the credit of pre- venting the evil from being planted in the district named, as no action whatever was taken by the temperance party to counteract the work of the brewers. " Fore-warned is fore-armed." et is te prevent as far as possible this fatal fac lity for getting drunk that all our efforts are being made, whether in the direction of moral suasion or by legal re- medies; and it only remains to inquire how far we :as a church, or circuit, are doing our duty in regard to this most pressing or all the questions of the hour. fhe committee urge the importance of every member of every society and congregation in the circuit himself abstain- ing,and where possible, see that his vote is cast for the prohibition of trade, which exists to spread disaster and ruin of the most horrible kind throughout the country. A. special appeal is being made this year for financial eels,. The Connexional Temperance Committee spent the modest sum of £650 last year, which was £lll more than was subscribed. The great enemy against which we are fighting can boast its millions. Or course we rely on the right arm of Almighty God, but it is as little as we can do to find paper,stamps,and a few sticks of seal- ing wax. A word to the wise is sufficient. (Llughter.) The Chairman said the temperance movement was one of the greatest moral institutions of the day. There were two planks in their movernent,one was teetotalism and the other Sunday closing. They had heard a great deal of late on this subject, and there was a strong pub- lic opinion to-day in favour of Sunday closing. (Ap- plause.) The Rey. R. J. Clifton addressed the meeting, and said the people whom they wanted there that night were not present.—They were elsewhere.—The Temperance people were present but not the intemperate. However their movement would prosper and attain access, by the help of God. He bad a resolution to submit to them that night which read "Sunday closing of Public-beuses. That this meeting records the satisfaction that, on May 6th, for the second time, the English Sunday Closing Bill, passed the second reading in the House of Commons. It further expresses as its strong conviction, that to stop the sale of intoxicating liquor on Sunday would be right hi principle, fair to other trades, and beneficial to the community at large ; and that it would be heartily wel- comed by those classes of society which would be most effected by such alteration in the law." After reading the resolution he (the speaker) was bound to put a big note of interrogation at the end of the resolution. He pointed out that the power was in the hands of the British public, and they alone could say what should be done and what should not be done. They wanted a spirit of conviction on this matter. They wanted enthu- siasm, as the cause for which they had met was a good one. (Applause.) The Rev. W. H. Pates,in seconding the resolution,said he had a strong feeling that all societies would welcome the Act for preventing the sale of drink on the Sabbath. He felt, however, that legislation would be of no avail whilst the nation itself was not ready for this great movement. He thought they were not at the present time—as a nation—completely ready for effective and satisfactory temperance legislation. They needed to try and show the people the benefits of such a movement. Then when their party was strong enough and when the nstion was educated in the principles of temperance, then they would come once for all and sweep away the evils of intemperance, and then their work would be done. (Applause.) The Chairman in putting the resolution to the meeting said, fifteen million pounds of money was spent every year on the Lord's Day for drink. (Shame.) Did it not stand to reason that the fifteen millions would go in to the pockets of the wives of the drunkards, if they had Sunday closing. (Hear, hear.) The motion was carried unanimously, and it was de- cided to send copies to the Prime Minister, the Right Hon. A. J. Balfour, M.P., and the Chairman of the Royal Commission on Liquor Licensing Laws (the Right Hon. Viscount Peel.) Mr. Marshall in moving a vote of thanks to the Chair- man and speakers, said the duty imposed on him that night was a double-barrelled performance. He had already occupied the chair at a temperance meeting that evening. (Hear, hear.) He did not know of a more useful position a man could occupy. The Chairman had done good work in connection with the Brunswick Band of Hope, and was one of its best supporters. (Ap- plause.) The speaker then referred to the practice of boys about the age of 8 and 9 years smoking. He con- sidered it was a growing scandal in their streets. Be had stood in a tobacconist's shop, and when he saw boys of the age above-mentioned,buying cigarettes then smoking them, he felt ashamed and abashed that shop- keepers for a few paltry pence would serve them. Smoking stunted their growth, bodily and intellectually, and it led them to habits of self-indulgence. He did not think it was out cf place, that night, to make aa. appeal to those guilty of the practice to cease doing so. (Applause.) Mr. T. Simister seconded the proposition, and said he was glad that the previous speaker had made retercnce to the habit of smoking by young people. He (the speaker) had travelled hundreds of miles with a cele- brated lecturer who chiefly lectured on three subjects. namely—tobacco, snuff, and drink. He contended that the drink was the devil, and snuff and tobacco his imps. He (the speaker) was proud to say that for the past 56 years he had been free from the devil and his imps. (Laughter and applause) The Rev. W. George pronounced the benediction and the meeting terminated. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CERMET CUM.—The adjourned annual meeting of the above club was held in the Trinity Wes- leyan Schoolroom,Halton, on Thursday last week. The Treasurer's and Secretary's ' reports were submitted. The reports were considered very satisfactory and were passed unanimously. The 4.ecrettry (Mr. J. Saunders) gave ap account ot the work of the club last season, in which'he said, they had played 14 matches, and had won 7, and lost 7. He considered that that was the best season the club had ever had since its formation three years ago. Votes ot thanks were accorded to the officers for their services during the put season. Mr. R. Hughes was highly complimented for his services to the club, he having played in every match since the club was organised. The election of officers for the coming season resulted as follows :—Captain, C.P. Brad- bury ; sub-captain. Mr. ft. Hughes ; treasurer, Mr. R. Hughes ; secretary, Mr. W. Looker ; committee, Messrs. W. Gregory, A. Hughes, T. Hanksy, P. Rawlinson, and %V. Tomiinson.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD—The weekly meeting of the above was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening. There was a large attendance, Mr. James Looker presided. A number of papers were introduced during the evening. The first was by Mr. Tomlinson on " temperance." He spoke at length on intemperence as a stumbling block in the way of Christian work. Mr. Albert Rowe con- tributed a paper on" Moderate drinking, as being in- jurious to health." Mr. Fred Hankey and Mr. Cyril Baguley gave rapers on "Money invested in brewery companies and money invested in other companies." Mrs. Boys also contributed a paper on reacue work. This p(per proved very interesting. Miss Boys presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>THE SECRET OF BEAUTY is found in CIITICURA SOAP, the most effective skin purifying and beautifying soap in the world, as well as the purest and sweetest for toilet, bath and nursery. It is the only preventive of pimples, blackheads, red„, rough, and oily skin, red, rough hands with shapeleis nails, dry, thin, and falling hair, and simple baby blemishes, because the cnly pre- ventive of inflammation and clogging of the pores, the cause of minor affections of the skin, scalp, and hair. Sale greater than the combined sales of all other skin and complexion soaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. McDeu,gall, in his report, said that the number of deaths during the past month amounted to 30, of these 5 were inquest cases and of the remaining 8 were due to convulsions, 4to bronchitis, 2to phthisis, 2to pneu- monia, 3 to heart disease, and one each to senile decay, hernia disease, liver, kidney disease, paralysis and misenteric disease. The 30 deaths showed an average death rate for the 1.2 months of 1904,4 and, exclusive of the inquest cases, 1602 of the estimated population. There were 51 births registeted during the month. For Use corresponding mouth of List year 32 deaths were registered (three sif them being inquest cases) and 42 births.</p>
        <p>SUMMATE AND BUILDING COMMITTEB.</p>
        <p>This committee recommended that a notice be served on the Rural District Council of Runcorn to terminate the agreement of Bth January,lBBs,in respect of the con- nection of .heir sewer with the Folly Brook sewer; that tide tender of Messrs. Brook and Brooks to supply about 1,500 superficial yards of 3in. Haslingden flags be ac- cepted; that an amended plan of Mr. W. C.ddick with regard to the new theatre, showing enlargement of the stage and the lengthening of the auditorium, be ap- proved ; that, in reply to a letter received from Mr. Orrell,the Runcorn pOstmaster,requeating to know if the Couocil would approve of the omission of the second de- livery of letters on Saturdays (2.15 p.m.), so as to give a half-day holiday to one or two postmen, he lael informed that the Council saw no objection to this particular de- livery being dispensed with for such a purpose.—These minutes were passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This committee recommenied that the tender of Mr. William Moore for the supp:y ef a set of harness for the ambulance be accepted; that the tender of Mr. J.Dobson for the supply of provender for the ensuing six months be accepted. The Sanitary Inspector having reported that there is insufficient or imnroper privy accommoda- tion at premises situated No. 31 Brook-street, and at No. 5 Egerton-street, it is recommended that a notice be 9ervel on the owner or owners under Section 36 of the Public Health Act,1875,t0 make good the deficiency or to provide proper accornmedarion within two months of the service of the notice, and for that purpose to substi- tute water closets for the existing privies, and ash-boxes for the existing ashpits.—Mr. Handley proposed the adoption of the above minutes, and said the health of the town was very satisfactory.—Mr. Dean seconded and they were carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MARKETS AND CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>MARKETS AND CEMETERY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>- - This committ-e decided that the tender of Mr. H. H. fthodes for the winding and reeulating of the cemetery clock for one year, be aceepted.—These minutes were adopted on the motion of Mr. Kirkham,seconded by Mr. Grace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TIIR LATE INIR..BRUNDRIT, J.P.</head>
        <p>TIIR LATE INIR..BRUNDRIT, J.P.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had now a painful, but in his opinion a proper duty to perform, and that duty was to move a vote of condolence with the family of the late Mr. John Brundrit in their sad bereavement. Apart from any official connection with that authority, Mr. 13rundrit was much esteemed in the town. He took a deep interest in all local affairs. At the time of his death he was chairman of the magisterial bench, chairman of the Saving's Bank, and president of the Runcorn Musical Society. He also held the position of trustee to several of the building societies in the town. He was a large and liberal employer of labour, and resided with them to the last. Much could be said of the deceased gentleman and he could say that those who knew him best, esteemed him must. He fortunately left three sons behind him, and he (the sp(aker) was sure they were worthy to fill the vacant place of their father.—Mr. Bisbrown asked if it was the custom to pass such votes of condolences from the Council Chamber. Although he asked the question he was quite in favour of it.—Mr. Imison said he felt certain that they would have incurred a considerable amount of blame had they allowed the death of the chief magistrate and so promin- ent a citizen to pass by without making any remark. He felt certain that Mr. Bisbrown and all the members of that Council would join in expressing sympathy with the family in the loss they had sustained. The deceased gentleman played a very prominent part in obtaining water for the town, and if there was no other reason why they should Condole with the family it would be due for his support on that important work.Mr.Brundrit and others set themselves to work and for many years provided toe town of Runcorn with water at a very great loss to themselves. He also played a prominent part in connection with the organisation of t• e volunteers. Mr. Brundrit actually moved a resolu- tion which resulted in the local corps being organised.— Mr. Handley, in supporting the vote of condolence, re- ferred to the name of " Brundrit " in connection with the town of Runcorn. He thought they should honour the memorylof Mr.Brondrit.Mr. Denis Brundrit played a very prominent part in the welfare of the town, and he was always looked up to as one of the fathers of Run- corn. The late Mr. John chose another way of serving the town by acting in the capacity of chief magistrate. He considered that they would have been wanting in- deed, if they (lid not pass the vote of condolence. Mr. Brundrit was very much respected.—Mr. Taylor said he I was sure they would all agree with him in saying that no other man had done more in various works than Mr. Brundrit had done. W,,en any good work for the benefit of the people was on. it had always the de- ceased's gentleman's sympathy and help.—The vote was then passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN BRIDGE FARES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BRIDGE FARES</p>
        <p>Mr. Bisbrown inquired whether any reply had been received to the memorial praying for a reduction in the Runcorn Bridge fares.—The Chairman said no reply had yet been reccived,and he was not at all surprised at it.— Mr. Bisbrown considered it very strange that they had not yet received a reply. He thought some steps ought to be taken in the matter. The memorial was unani- mously carried by the Council, and a considerable num- ber of people in the town felt deeply interested in the matter. He moved that as a Council they instruct their Secretary to remind the London and North-Western Railway Company of the memorial, and ask them to give their favourable consideration to it.—The Chair- man said he made the remark that when comments were circulated in reference to the conduct of public bodies,and they then asked them for a favour,they were then treated with scant courtesy.—Mr. Jones said he would be very glad to push the matter forward.—Mr. Collier suggested that they co-operate with the Widnes people in the matter.—Mr. Wiswall didn't believe in these matters being gone into openly. He quite agreed with the members in endeavouring to get a reductim in the fare for passing Inver the bridge by foot. He also thought that they should get a reduction in cross- ing the bridge by the train. If they were to get redress they must go about it in proper, systetnatic, and sensible fashion. The way they had gone about it hitherto was not the way to get it. Mr. Wilson seconded the proposition of Mr. Bisbrown, and said they should itry and obtain quarterly tickets for : 23, 6d. for the working classes who crossed by the toot bridge. Mr. Wiswall moved as an amendment that the matter be referred to the General Purposes Committee.—Mr. Taylor seconded the amendyient.—Mr. Collier said that Mr. Bisbrown had never spoken disrespectfully of any Corporation. He (Mr. Bisbrown) ought to be congratulated on the way he had stuck to the question of the reduction of the fares.—Mr. Handley said that he had noticeel that when subjects were referred to the General Purpsses Com- mittee they practically ware last on the shelf.—The amendment was then put before the meeting, and seven voted for it whilst five voted for the original proposi- tion. The amendment was therefore carried.</p>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER SHIP CANAL CoMPANY AND THE RECENT ACCIDENT AT THE RUNCORN PIERHEAD DOCKS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman read a letter which he had received that morning, dated Runcorn, 28th November, and signed by 'Mr. J. Meadowcroft. It stated : "The remarks of your- self and Mr. Speakman at the last meeting of the Cou- ncil respecting the circulation of reports detrimental to the interests of the town have just received a striking confirmation and it is perhaps right that I should relate to you as an illustration of many mis-representations which have been sent forth in respect to the Port. On the 19th inst. the steamer " Val-De-Travers" arrived here with a cargo of flints, &amp;c. The same evening there appeared in one of the evening papers, a paragraph to the effect that shf collided with the pierhead at Runcorn Pocks when entering the tidal basin ; that the force of the impact was very greatos the ship was coming up the the Ship Canal on a high flood tide, but that she mar- vellously excaped serious damage, and the steamer after discharging was proceeding t. Manchester to be over- hauled. Not having heard anything of this, lat once asked the pier-master for an explanation, and he re- ported that she broached the south pier as she made for the entrance and barely scratched paint off her. Since this the captain has written as follows :—Dear Sir, as you are fully aware that the report of the Val-De-1 ravers being in collision with the Runcorn pier is false, I trust</p>
        <p>you will assist me in finding out and bringing to justice the author of these malicious reports detrimental to yours and my character. I have seen the Editor of one of the Manchester papers to day about it and intend taking action at law to find out and put a stop to these malicious reporte. The Val-15e-Travers had not a scratch on her. I got a ship-master to look at her in Manchester also. The writer continuing stated that the letter of the captain needs no comment, and I may add that although the steamer went up to Manchester she proceeded there expressly to discharge goods and reload cargo,a,nd with regard to the high flood title there would only be about one foot of tidal water in the canal at high water. After what has already passed I am not sure that you will be surprised at this case, but I can truly say that .such a matter would not have been noticed before the canal was constructed but such reports are now evi- dently well received. I think I may here appropriately refer very briefly to the complaints made to your trade committee a short time ago respecting the congestion of traffic at the docks. I explained to the deputation</p>
        <p>which waited upon me that we had had a heavy arrival 'of vessels, quite a rush latterly, and that owing to de- pression of trade abroad, and also in the Potteries,little material had been going forward, and that in conse- quence our storage room was blocked; this statement has since been disputed by some who assume superior knowledge, but I here furnish you with an old and important shipper in a letter received within the last fortnight:—"lt would be very much better if we could regulate the shipping so as to avoida rush in August and September. This year has been very unfortunate for us so far as freights are concerned." This state of things has occurred over and over again and will occur in the future—nothing can prevent it. Outsiders seem unable to recognise that the owners of the estate have by far the greatest interests at stake. As to whether Runcorn has suffered, I can confidently assert that she has—both tradesmen and workmen have suffered directly and indirectly from mis- representations that have been made. The sooner this is appreciated, both inside and outside of your chamber, the sooner may you look for returning prosperity."— There was no discussion on Mr. Meadowcroft's letter. The Council rose and the members dispersed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. J. BRUNDRIT, J.P., OF</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. J. BRUNDRIT, J.P., OF</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mr. John Bruadrit, J.P., which took place at big residence, The Highlands, Higher Runcorn, on Tuesday morning about 4 o'clock. The deceased gentleman had not been in very excellent health for some time, although he followed his business up to about a fortnight ago, whea he had to take to his bed. Dr. McDougall and his assistant (Dr. Wright) were his medical advisers, but nothing serious in Mr. Brun- drit's condition was noticed until lest week. Two Liver- pool doctors, Dr. Banks and Dr. Hamilton, were then summoned, and they along with the other two medical gentlemen were in constant attendance. Deceased was well-known and much respected in the town, and as sena as the receipt of the news of his death - got alit it spread rapidly all over the district. Early on Tuesday morning flags were lying half-mast at the late gentleman's office in High-street,the Leeds and Liverpool Canal Office(Messrs. Simpson and Davies agents), the Town Hall, the Sea- men's Mission, Parr's Bank,and at most of the canal and steamship companies offices at Top Locks. Mr. Brun- drit was in his 63rd year and was the senior partner of the old-established firm of Messrs. Brundrit and Com- pany,quarry proprietors and ship owners, Runcorn and Perinsaenniawr. Same years ago he took a very active part in connection with church work,occupied the posi- tion of churchwarden, treasurer of several institutions, was connected with the Parish Church,and was also one of the trustees of the National Szhool. He was closely associated with various charities. He qualified as a magistrate for the county of Chester in February, 1873, and was the oldest member of the Bench in the Runcorn Petty Sessional Division,of which he had been chairman for abut ten years. He was president of the Rune,orn Savings Bank,of which he was the oldest depositor. He was also chairman of the old Runcorn,Halton, and Wes- ton Water Works previous to their being taken over by the late Board of Commissioners. He was also president of the Conservative Association, and took an active in- terest in the furtherance of the Unionist cause, and pre- sided at almost all their political meetings. He was chairman of the Runcorn Musical Seciety,and at one time a member of the Runcorn Improvement Commissioners. He also took a very prominent part in connection with the organisatien of the Runcorn Volunteers, and it was through him the local cerps were organise I. He was an enthusiastic sportsman, and took a very keen interest in coursing. He leaves three sons Reginald, Percy, and Cecil, to mourn his death, which is very much re- gretted throughout the town and district by all classes of society. The remains of -the deceased gentleman were interred to-day at noon, in the Runcorn cemetery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Runcern School Board was held in the Board-rnom at the Town Hall on Tues- day evening. Mr. J. Pritchard presided and hero were also pre-ent,the Bev. Canon Wood,Mr. Imison, C.C., Mr. J. Norman, J.P., and Mr. G. Moss (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>The minutes of meetings of this committee held on the 10th and 24th ult. showed that 61 parents were sum- moned.of which 52 appeared, and it was decided that proceedings be taken against 12 parents, who had failed to send their chinlren regularly to school. It was also recommended that a sum not exceeding £5O be contri- buted during the current, and during each of the next two years towards the maintenance of children at certi- fied Industrial schools. The visitors reports for the month ending November 20th were read. They showed that the numbers on the registers were 3,414,a falling off of 26 compared with the corresponding period of last year, but the actual attendance was better, the average being 2,842 against 2,757,an increase of 85. This was con- sidered satisfactory.—The Chairman in moving the con- firmation of the minutes referred to the proposed £5O for maintenance of children at industrial schools, and said that the sum might or might not be required. They had not sent a boy to an industrial school for some years and in his opinion the Board had taken the best view of the question. The general body of ratepayers were not bound to educate children except under excep- tional circumstances. There were two or three cases of boys who had passed from the control of parents and were under the observation of the visiters. It might be necessary to use their powers in regard to one or two of these cases, and thus set an example to other boys.— Mr. Norman secended.—On the proposition of the chair- man, seconded by Canon Wood, it was resolved that Mary Ellen Neish, daughter of Mr. John Neish. of 18, St. John-street, be sent te the school for the blind at Liverpool, the Board to pay £2l per annum for her maintenance and £3 per annum for her outfit. It was also proposed that the Board hereby agree to accept is. 61. per week from the father of the child as a contribution towards the child's mainten- ance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The above Committee met on Tuesday, November 24th. They recemmended that Lily Hill be appointed monitress at Mill Brow Infant School. It was also re- commended that a clock be purchased for Granville-et. school. It was further recommended that three desks be obtained for Granville-et. girls' school. A letter was read from Mr. J. Simpson agreeing to allow the Board the use of the piano at the Brunswick-st. boys' school. the Board to pay for tuning.—Mr. Norman moved, and Mr. Imison seconded, and the above minutes were carried.</p>
        <p>EXTENSION OF VICTORIA-ROAD SCHOOLS</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved the following resolution: "That the plans and specifications of the extension of the Victoria-road school as received from the architect be, and are hereby approved, and the Clerk is hereby in- structed to submit them for the approval of the Educa- tion Department."—The Chairman said the plans had b.en amended by the architect to meet the views of the Education Dtpartment.-11r. Norman seconded, and it was agreed to. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PROPOSED COTTAGE HOSPITAL FOR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED COTTAGE HOSPITAL FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>I NOVEL FOO 'MALL MATCH.</p>
        <p>An interesting and very amusing football match was played on Wednesday afternoon on the Football Ground, Irwell•lane, between the Runcorn Cycle Club and the Co-operative Stores. The proceeds were in aid of the Runcorn Cottage Hospital Fund. Had the atmospheric conditions been more favourable a parade formed by the teams in comic costumes would have been made through the town to the football field, but this had to be aban- doned owing to the rainy weather. The teams tank up their positions on the field and were composed as follows: —Cycle Club : W. H. Munday, captain, full back ; Houghton (Runcorn R.F.C.), Farmer (R R.F.C.), R.y (Runcorn Recreation) and Wainwright (a former mem- ber oi Runcorn R.F.C.), three-quarter backs; Whinyates and Stratton, half backs; T. Cannon, C. Sayles, J. Charleswnrth, H. Smith, J. Mack. S. Miles, J. Tomlin and W.Moores,forwar's.Co-onerative Stores: Gavter,full ' back; W. Gayter(R.R.F.C.).T.Richardeon (R.R.F.C.),w„ Rushton. captain, A. Abel, three-quarter backs; J. Nick- son and J. Nickson, balf backs ; Withington, Douglas, Gresty, Cowan. Hughes, Matthews, Nicholas and A. N. Other forwards. Co-operative touch judge, W. Lawton. The description of the dresses wcrn were :—Cycle Club : Munday wore the gown of a clown. Ray figured in a schoolboy's suit, and represented a big school boy. Stretton appeared as a Volunteer. Charlesworth repre- sented a Harlequin. Swale was dressed in a convict ' garb and numbered "99." Whinyates and Mack were attired as Tramps. Cannon appeared as a Frenchman Miles as a Gentleman,and wore a tall hat, long coat,and white kid gloves. Smith represented the Maid of the Mill, whilst the remaining five players were attired in their ordinary foothail suits. Webster dressed in white with blackened face, appeared as a nigger, aid repre- sented the Cycle Club touch-judge. The Co-cperative team were dressed in suits of white.bearing the name of a local soap company. Mr. J. Ellison acted as referee with a fog-horn. The game proved very amusing, but nevertheless a good game of football wan witnessed. The referee caused much amusement by his frequent blasts on the fog-horn, as also did the tramps and the gentle- man "masher." At half time nothing was scored. In the second half the men in the popular costumes played a rushmg game and ran out winners by two tries to one.</p>
        <p>Natalians have a rival to the sea-serpent in the shape of a "lamp-snake," which resides in a pool near the Inanda Fails,aud according to the report of a party from Durban, lights un its habitat with two large illuminants equal to thirty-two candle electric power. The local newspapers say that " the story is confirmed by several credible witnesses."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>CHRTSTMA.B CARDS AND</head>
        <p>CHRTSTMA.B CARDS AND</p>
        <p>MAGAZINES.</p>
        <p>RAPHAEL TUCK AND SONS, LIMITED.—We are once more pleasantly reminded that Christmas ap- proaches by the arrival of a box of sample cards, booklets, etc., from the above art publishers. Year after year rolls on and we still find that Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons,Limited,are to the fore with beautiful designs in Christmas and New Year's i cards. Last year there was a noticeable feature n this firm's stock of novelties,and that was the intro- duction of the "Black and White" series of cards which at once attained wide popularity. This year we notice that the "Black and white" series is again predominant. In giving it premier place the publishers display accurate discernment of the public taste, for no Christmas cards yet introduced are so chaste and artistic. The series embraces nearly 300 sets of cards, and the work of over 50 of the most prominent artists of to-day is embodied in them. En addition to the single "black and white" cards there are platino panels of most artistic design. Framed and hung on the wall they make a charm- ing picture. Calendars also occupy a prominent position in Messrs. Tuck and Sons' catalogue. .i.here are nearly 100 distinct designs in exqui- site colour printing and black and white work, wnich the calenders claim as their own. All sizes and all . shapes are found and the mechanical contrivances in some for hiding and disclosing the dates are highly ingenious. There is plainly no falling off in this department. Another series of card which claims special atten- tion is the "Lace "series, a novel and exceedingly pretty card which is calculated to draw the deepest admiration from the gentler sex. The younger generation is, as usual, large:y catered for. Book- lets with illustrated Bible stories, fables, and nur- sery rhymes, are put up in glowing colours which only the infantile mind can fully appreciate. The Royal " Art Novelty," series of stand relief cards are also elegant and ingenious. There are "Young Riders," regiments, private theatricals, merry-go- rounds, &amp;c.. which can be arranged by the child, as the cards are made to fit one another for the re- quisite figures and designs. Of miscellaneous cards, Remembrance,Good-wishes, and Forget-me-not, &amp;c. there is no end. The style and tinting of each .differs, yet all are beautiful, while the verse inside is simple and appropriate. Though Messrs. Raphrel Tuck and Sons do not, this year,show many original designs in cares there is a universal excellence in their productions which defies criticism. Every- thing is of the highest finish and commands the eAlogy of all who have a taste for or appreciation of art in its most attractive form. MACMILLAN &amp; Co.—With the Christmas month now here all magazines are visibly enlarged and improved. The December "Century"magazine is in- deed a splendid number containing an unusual quantity of reading matter and fine illustrations. The cover too has a Christmas look about it and in- dicates its special character.—" Macmillan's Maga- zine "presents its readers with some excellent Christmas stories which well sustain its high repus tation.—" St. Nicnolas„' Magazine for the current month is likewise replete in stories, poems, and riddles, which will assuredly brighten many a Yule- tide fireside.—A book which is eminently suited for children is" Little Wenderlin," and other fairy stories by Annie and E Keary. There are altogether 15 charming little stories in the volume, which would make a most suitable Christmas present. The price is 23. 6d. WARD, LOCK AND BOWDEN.—This month the " Windsor " magazine takes the palm among all its numerous compeers. Not only is it double the size but a novel entitled "In Strange Company," is given away as a supplement. Guy Boothby, author of Dr. Nikola, is the ;writer of the novel, and we must say at once that it is a reaily splendid one. The story is one of stirring adventure in Chili and tfie South Seas, and covers 80 pages the size of the " Windsor." Not an uninteresting paragraph is to be found from beginning to end,and tho publishers must be congratulated on their enterprise in pre- senting it. Stanley L. Wood supplies four graphic, full-page illustrations with the novel. ihe "Wind- sor" itself is double its ordinary size,and the price is is. We cannot particularise all it contains but we are compelled to state that a brighter or more interesting budget of stories, interviews, and pictures, does not exist at the price. The most important feature in connection with it is the commencement of "The Christian," Hall Caine's new story. The author of the" Manxman," "Deemster," and other works, is too well-known for his last novel to need com- menting on. It opens well and judging by the first few chapters it is more than likely to fully I uphold the author's reputation. Good illustrations from the pen of J. Barnard "Davies accompany the novel.</p>
        <p>CASSELL AND CO.—The December number of " Cassell's Family Magazine" sees its permanent enlargement. Not only is it bigger,but a new and most attractive design adorns the cover. The magazine now looks more modern and in keeping with present-day style. The size of this new num- ber is quarto,as before, but there are now 112 pages and 120 illustrations. The latter are particularly goad. A new serial story by Flora Annie Steel com- mences and gives promise of being exceedingly en- tertaining and clever. D. H. Parry contributes an exciting complete story entitled " At the Third Pass ; " Clark Russell writes on "The Pirate in Fact and Fiction" and among numerous other excellent items are"An interview with Blondin,"and "A Chat about the Sultan" by G. B. Burgin. Every pur- chaser of this number receives as a souvenir of the enlargement, an album of Frederick Barnard's cele- brated character sketches from Dickens. The latter, 16 in number, are beautiful pieces of work, and themselves worth the 61. charged for the mar.a- lzine.—" Christmas Arrows," the extra number of the "Quiver" issued each Christmas is also to bani. The principal story in it is "A Clerk of Oxenford, by M. A. Balliol, but there are also several short sketches and stories at the end. Mr. BallioPs story is cleverly written, embodies pathos and humour, and finally ends up to the satisfaction of all con- cerned.—ln the current number of the " Magazine of Art" is given a special supplement of the works of Mr. E. J. Poynter, P.R.A., including reproduc- tions of many of his representative paintings. The text of the article is contributed by Mr. M. H. Spielmann.—During the last month or two Messrs. Cassell and Co., have issued several editions of valu- able works at a cheap rate, but none should meet with more favour than " Cassell's Technical Educa- tor," the first weekly. part of which is just out. Altogether there will be 24 numbers at 6d. each, so that the purchaser gets a library of the most useful technical instruction for 12s. The work is uniform with the" Popular Educator " and each part will contain 96 pages of text,illustrattons, and diagrams. The whole range of tour industries will be covered by the different sublects, and the writers are well- known experts in their respective departments. To working man, and all kinds of artificers the " Tech- nical Educator " should be an inestimable boon,and greatly helpful to proficiency in labour. Part I. contains a presentation plate of Lord Leighton's "Flie Industrial Arts applied to Peace,''and a brilli- ant coloured illustration of the Bessemer process,as frontispiece. lion.AcE Cox.—One of the biggest budgets of literature published every yule-tide is the Christ- mas number of "The Queen," the premier ladies' paper. This month's edition is no exception and fully comes up to the high standard oi previous years. A number of first class short stories, sketches, reviews:, articles, and all manner of diver- sifiesl contributions fill the huge pages. Fashions are Illustrated and talked of 'very skilfully, and are indeed a feature of the paper. A prize of .£l5 15s. is offered for the completed lines of a number of rhymes, Each line has a word or two missing and the task of filling them up correctly is by no means an easy one, though the attraction of £l5 153. is great enough to ensure perseverance. The best way of investing a is. is to buy the Queen for Dezember. Not only is reading and other matter of the best kind procured in quantity, but a couple of hand- some presentation plates accompanying each num- ber. The chief is supplement to tbe Queen and bears the title of "Mimosa," a copy of a tastefully tinted picture of a girl carrying mimosa flowers and painted by Edith Scannell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
