        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE 1'.5.1. sociErY. The attendance at the meeting of the above named society on Sunday afternoon was one of the largest in its history. It was expected that Mr. and Mrs. Evans of Liverpool would conduct the Ktrvice.but in consequence of the costinded illness of Mts. Evans they were unable to be present. The meeting was an open one and a large number of the wives and,friends of the members was present: Mr. r. Hawthorn was chairman and de- livered a brief addresa en" Cross bearing." Mr. James Evans, of Warrisgton, was the speaker, who based a very intereating and practical address ea " Organisa- tion ;" his trenchent criticisms of some of the methods adopted to reach the rna.sses Ad to rescue the perishing VlSTO.much appreciated. The speaker related several iv' cideats in his owe experience te illustrate various poir,;.B in hi: address. Mr. Richard Atkinson and Mr. Beswick also took part in the service</p>
        <p>AItI4IeNIAN RZFITGE.I; LOCAL FUND. We have been supplied with a balaac,! Ogee, of this fund which shows that the 811111 of £:.)(t) 2s. 10d. has been raised in Earlesown and neigtipourlood. Atter deducting the expenses, which have, ‘evltleatlY been kept down to the lowest -point., .rtls 2d. was forwarded to Lady Henry Somerset. It la4aY be mentioned that this is the second patolic effort, made in the immediate vicinity to hein tlie oppressed and persecuted Armenians. The first Ci,irlectloni amounted to £l5. and was made in respG.:3hie to the Stitring , and pathetic lecture given by Mi4(lattil4ouulaine in the Earle:l:own Town Bail somio time ago.</p>
