      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NATION'S HEALTH.</head>
        <p>THE NATION'S HEALTH.</p>
        <p>It is becoming mere and more evident year by year to those who minutely observe the current of internal events and esnecielly the health of the British nation that we are fast becoming a nation of Dyspeptics. The malady of dyspepsia.has spread its effects not only over our own nation, but it is the prevailing malady of civi- lised life. There is no disease more prevalent or more difficult to cure. It is a disease of the stomach and as- sumes a variety of different forms in parts of the body far removed from the seat of the disease. Dyspepsia is only another name for the effects of indigestion and the different forms it assumes as its effects are known by various names, according to the different symptens it presents, as dyspepsia flatulence, heartburn, sick head- ache. pains in the stomach, palpitation of tilt heart, heart diseases,colic. dysentery, diarrhcea. &amp;c. All these and even apoplext and paralysis and nine tenths of the ' canes of convulsions in children have their source in that defective nutrition to which the term indigestion has been applied. Other causes are worry, anxiety,over work, emotion, stimulants. narcotics. Many persons overload and over- the digestive organs which impairs their powers and renders them incapable of performing their proper func- tions. Constant overworking of the stomach must pro- duce disease of that organ. More nutriment is furnished to the tissues of the body by a small quantity of food well digested, than by a large quantity half digested. The only trustworthy and permanent remedy is Gwilym Evans's Quinine Bitters. The Vegetable Tonic, which, acting upon the digestive organs, strengthens them and stimulates them to healthy action, and gives tone to the whole stomach imparting new life, hope, strength and happiness. It is the most successful remedy ever dis- covered, and has never failed to gain high approval and praise whenever it has been properly and fairly tried. It contains a suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose together with the Medicinal Virtues of the following well-known herbs :--Sarsaparilla, Gkitain. Burdock, Saffron, Lavender, and Dandelion, combined in most happy proportions, and concentrated in a pure state, as well as being scientifically prepared to be suitable to all ages, at all seasons of the year. It is Sold in Betties. at 23. 91. and 4s. ed. each, by all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors. or Carriage Free by Parcels Post, direct from the Proprietors. Beware of imitations, see the name GWILYM EVANS, on Label, Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING</p>
        <p>Co., LTD., LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICR COURT. MONDAY —Before the Mani' (in the chair), Mr. F. Monks, Alderman Harrison. Mr. T. Birchen, and Mr J. W.</p>
        <p>Hayward. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Thomas Flannery, 17 Friars' Gre.n, was find ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-st. on Saturday night.—P.C. Sankey gave evidence in support of the charge. It w,s defendant's 29th appearance. A RNCKLESR DRIVER. Joseph Miller. butcher, Whitey, was charged with being drunk in charge of a horse and c,rt in Horsetnarket-st on November 25.—P.C. Bell said that de- fendant was very drunk and driving about 10 miles an hour He almost collided with an omnibus.—Herbert Kendrick, 'bus driver, stated that he saw defendant drunk, and the latter almost drove into witness's 'bus.—Defendant was fined 10s. and costs. OBSCENITY.—MichaeI Murray. 3 Berry's-yard, was com- mitted to gaol for 14 days' herd labour for using obscene lan- guage on Pinner's Brew OR the 21st November. He made no appearance in answer to the charge. THE MAYOR PaYs A vnfE.—Thomas Walsh, I.ndlord of the " Crown and Sceptre" inn was fined a shilling for allowing his chimney to be on fire on the 24th November.— Alderman Harrison: The full penalty is 40s. yen know.— The Mayor: Well Wait till you're chimney is on fire then. (Laughter.)—Rdward Hughes, 35, Hopwood-street. was similarly &amp;lett with, for the same offence.—He did not appear and Constable Frank Cooper said he was working.— The Mayor: Where does he work P—P.C.Cooper : At Messrs. Ifairelough's Mills. (Laughter.)—The Mayor: I'll pay the shilling then. (Laughter.) And he did. WEDNESDAY.—Beforeyr. John.Geddes (in the chair) and ,</p>
        <p>the eart was jammed in the tree guard. After waiting for about twenty minutes he fouad the defendant in a beer house. On being spoken to with reference to the dam ag- done, the d.f.ndent became very insolent, and walked back again to the beerhouse. He stayed there for another ten minutes.—The maTistrates imposed a fln. of 5s- and costs:.</p>
        <p>AS.A.IILTIVG THE LANDLADY.—Levi Bradshaw. a collier was summoned for having on the 14th November been drunk and further with Rasa...Ming Elizabeth Hibbert, of the Blue Bell Hotel. Newtoa.—P.O. Williamson stated that at 7.30 on the night of the 14th inst. he received a communication that Mrs. Hibbert had hem assaulted. He went to the Blue Bell Hotel and ejected the defendant —Elizabeth H. bbert. of ths Blue Bell Hotel, &amp;posed that the defendant came into her house intoxicated and commenced quarrelling. She went to him and told him that if he did not quit the premises she would bring a policeman. He deliberately kicked her and got hold of her arm and pulled her. She sent for assistance, and the defendant was ejected. —John lieaft residing at. 3 Vul^an-st • spoke to seeing the defendant kick the last wit- ness.—The defendant denied the offeace. and stated that the 'witnesses were telling lies. He contended that he was as- saulted.—Tbe magistrates said they Qbnsidered the offences proved, and fined him ss. and costs for being drunk. and 10s. and costs, or one month's imprisonment, for the assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Mr S. Wright.</head>
        <p>Mr S. Wright.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Thiimas Bmney was charged with belug drunk and disorderly in Foundry-st. at 9.15 on Tuesday night.—lnspector Taylor proved the case and de- fendant was fined ss. and costs, or seven days in default. TRURSDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman). and Lieutenant Colonel Ridgway. - - -</p>
        <p>THEFT or A Wair.—Michael Rowan, son of Patrick Rowan, of 28 Charles-street, was charged with stealing a whip from a cart in Market-street, Warrington, the previous day.— The defendant, who is 14 years of age, pleaded guilty. —The Chief Constable in his statement to the bench said that a man named John Littler,employed by Mr. Howton, of Newton-by-Daresbury, came to Warrington on Wednesday to deliver some rabbits. He left his cart standing near the Market-place and on his return later on, he missed his whip. Information was given to the police, and the defendant was apprehended with a whip in his possession. His parents were communicated with,and they stated that the lad had got beyond their control.—The magistrates ordered him to receive twelve arokes of the birch rod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESIONS. MONDAY.—Before Megan. F. J. Norman, S. TAylor and J. Handley.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESIONS. MONDAY.—Before Megan. F. J. Norman, S. TAylor and J. Handley.</p>
        <p>A SHOPKEEPER IN TROUBLE:— William Boulton, 103 Percival-lane, grocer, was summoned at the instance of Alfred Timmins. inspector of weights and measures for the Cheshire County Council for selling butter as margarine on October 27th.—Mr. Timmis said thsst on the date named he visited the defendant's shop and asked for a pound of buuter and was zharged Is. He informed defendant's wife that it was for the purposes of analysis. It was accordingly sent to the analyst. who certified that the saropl3 sent was not butter, as it contained 100 percent. of margarine. Defend nt said he purchased it as butter and paid for it as such.—The Bench imposed a fine of 2s. 6.1 and 15s. 6d. costs. (jBSCENE LA:iGUAGE.—Ann Phi bin of Surrey-A ,was fined ss. tor using obscene language on November 23rd. VAGRANCY. —William Woodier, of no fixed abode, was com- mitted to prison for three weeks for sleepirg in the Silt Works. Weston Point that morning. P.C. Bancroft proved the cue A FISHERMAN'S Row.—William Antrobus. Halton-road, fisherman, was fined ss. 6d. for assaulting Henry Hambton, of 3 Rock Mount. Heath-road, on November 22nd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS,</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—fore Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman), Colanel Wood, Mr. R. Stone, and Mr. C. 8.. F. Borron,</p>
        <p>LICENSE TRANSFERS.— nu Parr, Gelb rue, applied for the transfer of an off-beerhouse license lately held by John Onsworth —Superintendent Brassington statail that there had been some little alterations of the premises, but they were an advantage rather than otherwise, and he did not oppose the transfer. The magistrates transferred the license. The following were also transferred —Joseph Goulden to the Railway Hotel, Golborne, in lieu or John Parr; Emma Ryder to the .Bowling Green Inn, Rixton (on the application ot Mr. Grang- r. solicitor) ; William Beatty to the Grilfinitlotel, Earle-st., from Th )mas Hamer; Thomas Rooinson Heath to the Swan Inn. Winwiek. from J B. Cooper . William Whit ey to the Millstone Inn, Newton, fron John Webster ; J hn Naylor to the Swan Inn, Newton Common from Harriet Naylor ; Peter Nicholas to the Black Swan Inn, Rixton. from Arthur Kiernan. A CYCLIST IN TROIIBLE.—Harry Arden was fined ls. and costs tor having on the 20th November at Winwick ridden a bicycle at 6.10 p.m., without a lighted lamp THE DE-NEEN LisT.—iPatrick Kenny for being drunk in Jamea-street, Golborne, on the 20th November. was fined 2s. 61. and costs. The following were dealt with for bring drunk and disorderly :—Michael Callaghan (15th appearance) en the 14th November at Barlestown, 10s. and costs ; Samuel Ryder (31st appearance), on the 27th November at Winwick. 10s. and costs; Patrick Glynn, on ti.r 18th November at Gelberne. ss. and costs; Martin Callagian, on the 13th November at Golborne, 2s. 65. and costs ; John William Greenish and Stephen Gleave, on che 17th November at the 410 ear, Haydock, ss. and tosts. liReNKEN DRIVER.—Owen Gallagher was charged with bar gbr ei, drunk whilst, in charge of a horse and ctrt on .9th November.—P.O. Addison stated that at 7.30 on the dat • n .med he saw a horse and cart travelling towards Queen-street, Baelestown. He saw that the animal was not under coatrol, and stopped it. He f3und the defendant lying at the bottom of the cart drunk.—The magistrates severely censured the defendant, and fined him Ns. and, -costs. A CHARGE OF STEALING CO/L.—Ellen Brown, a married wom n was char ed with having on the 25th November stolen 2jllss of coal, value one penny, the property of Messrs. Richard Mans and Company. Limited. She pleaded not guilty.—P 0 Adsiisen stated that at 10.45 on the previous Thursday morning he saw the defendant coming away from the Wood Pit, Newton, with a bag of coal in her possession. There was a truck on the highway for the purpose of carrying coal away. He stopped the defendant to as. her Actin the coal ,nd she replied "Joe is IlOt well and I have come for some coal." She admitted that she had taken the coal in the bag but denied that the treck was hers.—ln answer to the Chairman witness admitted that the coal had been taken from a w ate heap at the pit. People had been summoned before tor taking it. The dtfendant had been driven away from tir: eeap r, pettedly. No one was allowed to pick th.! coal fr m it.—The defendant said the coal she picked was r.,11i ig into the lane.—The Chairman said the Bench eons d, red that the defendant had been under the misappre- hension that she was at liberty to take the coal. and they had decided to discharge her with a caution. She must not take the coal ',gain. A Weafterireerust CARTER IN TROUBLE.—Richard Barer. of Vliinteick-road, Warrington, was summoned for having on the 18•11 Noveml,er at i.arleistown, not been in a ntrol of a horse and cart of which hp was in charge.—P.O. Bullen gave ,evidene. as to receiving a complaint from the Assistant Nuisance Inspector a ith reference to damage being done to a tree guard in the street by the defendant's cart. lie found that ea, one was in ch tree of the vehicle ; the di fendant ting in A eeighbouring public-house. Witness asked h m lsr hie name acid adereies and he became very abusive.—Mr. Geigery. assistant nui,ance inspector, stated that about 2 33 OIL tbe ilate nemed, ho saw a horse end cart in H!gh-streat, with int aievne ia charge. One of the wheels of</p>
