      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Captain Withington (In the chair), Mr. W. Houghton, Mr. L. Tinsley and Mr. J. White. DRISNIC AND DISORDERLY.—For being drunk and disorderly in Croft on the 2.3n4 November, Edward Sharpies and William Collier, were fined, the former 2s. 61.. and costs, and the latter ss. and costs; Thomas Fisher and John Cunning- ham were fined, the former 2s. 61. and costs and the latter 51 and costs for beim, drunk and disorderly in Poulton ; Edward Murphy, same offence in Rixron. fined 2s. 6d. and costs; William Bolton, Penk-th, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs; John Porkin, drunk and disorderly in Burtonwood. fined 2s. 6d. and costs.</p>
        <p>CYCLING lON THE FOOTPATEL—ThOtHAS Thompson. 40th Reglment,was charged with riding on the footpath in Sankey on November 22nd .—P.C. Thorley proved the caseand defend- ant was discharged on paying costs. TeEsPaseiNG.—ateorge Gleeye was charged with trespass- ing on land In the o -'cupation of Mrs. Warburton in search of game, on the 21st November.—Mr. E. A. White prosecuted and stated that on Saturday. the 21st Novernher.John Thorn- ley, a gamekeeper in the employ ef Mr. Tobin was on du'y in Burtonwood, and heard several shots in the direction of Mrs. James Warburton's farm. He afterwards saw Gleave and three other men on a field occupied by Mrs. Warburton and over which Mr. Tobin had the right of shooting. They were breastin.f. the field in line. Thernley concealed himself and saw that all had guns. He saw several shots fired in an upward direction and linable with partridges, but he could not say who fired. He intercepted them and caught Gleave and got his name and address. Gleave offered Thornley half a •• quid" to say nothine about the case. The land was situated between Barlestown and Warrington and it was over-run with poachers. It was a regular business of poaching and he (Mr. White) asked tbe bench to inflict a penalty which would make it unprofitable.—Thornley corroborated and defendaat was fined 10s. and costs. Mr. White was allowed an advo- cate's fee of 10s. 6d.—Evans Roberts, William Evans. and Edgar Critchley were charged with having on the 14th November, in Burtonwood trespassed on land in possession of Jeremiah Hayes in search of garne. —Mr Browne defended. Mr. White prosecuted and said that on the day in question three of Mr. Tobin's gamekeepers were on duty in Old Hall- lane, Burtenwoed. They saw the three defendants coming along the road from the direction of the Vulcan. Critchley and Roberts had guns but Evans had not. The keepers concealed theme -Ives behind some buildings and watched the defendants They heard reports and saw defendants coming back along the canal towing path. They entered a meadow and commenced to breast the field. One gamekeeper saw them put up a covey of 7or 8 partridges, and one of the desndant's shot at them. They afterwards put up another covey but it was out of range and at the end of the meadow the keepers made for the de- fendants. They caught Evans who gave them his name and address. The other defendants were afterwards summoned— Jonathan Shopley, and John Thornley, gamekeepers, corro- borated.—Mr. Browne raised objection to the case as no deeds showing Mr. Tobin's shooting rights were produced. The prosecution too must be tak n by someone in the employ of the owner. Shapley was not in the employ of the ow iers as the land belonged to the Railway Company —Mr. White said that anyone was entitled to prosecute in a game case.— The magistrates decided against the objection, and Mr. Browne then urged that Evans had no gun. He was not a companiou o the others and only met them accidentally. They all denied :eaving the towing path or entering the meadow. They had guns because they had been to a pigeon shoot at Wargraye. Critchley and Roberts also denied haying fired a shot.—The Bench fined Roberts and Critchley . and costs. each, and Evans ss. and costs</p>
        <p>AN ALIBI PnovEn.--Jonn Atherton was charged with hay ing on the 9th Noyem ler in Botrtonwood fired off a gun in Hall-lane.—He pleaded not guilty.—John Thornbiy said that be heard defendant the day in question ordered off some land ,belonging to lir Bond. When in the road he fired his gun and again opposite Jeremiah Hayes' place. Witness stopped him and said he should summon him.—Defendant denied haying seen Thornley in his life before November 25th. and said h was working on the afternoon in question. —A. neighbour his deposed that he went to his work at the colliery and came home black. He stopped in the house and Bad his tea. had no gun,—The bench dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC HIGHWAYEAUTHORISED.—Mr. It. A. White applied on behalf of the Highway Botrd, with respect to a private occupation road in the parish of Winwick leading in a northerly dit,ction towards Vulcan foundry, and another commencing at the boundary of tne borough of Warringt near Orford Hall, and running in a northerly direction to Orford Post Office. The owners and oclupiers of every nart of the roads had consented to their being made public high- ways and repairable by the district. He therefore asked for orders authorising the roads as such. It was the intention of the Highway Board to widen each road to 30 feet.—Mr. J. E. Worsley appeared on behalf of the Parish Council of Winwick and produced a resolution si.zn,d by Canon Penrhyn on behalf of the members present. instituting these proceedings. —There was no objection to the proposal an.t the magistrates made the orders.</p>
        <p>DRUNK IN CHARGE.—loseph Potter, Poulton, was charged with being drunk in Warrington-road, Poulton, while in charge of a horse and trap on November 19th —P.O. Jehnson proved the case and defendant was fined 10s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WORLD MOVES.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD MOVES.</p>
        <p>Not so very many years ago the man who might have suggested the possibility of sending a message over a wire to Australia would have been thought insane. Twenty-five years ago to have perscribed cod-liver oil in any form for dyspeptic symptoms would have been re- ceived in about the same way. But the world moves. We are not doing things as our parents did at our age. We are discovering many new uses of cod-liver oil, thanks to Scott's Emulsion. A gentleman writes : "Some three or four years ago I had a severe attack of influenza, leaving behind it as a legacy a recurrence, often from day to day, of most excruciating spasms in the region of my stomach, frequently after meals. Iso suffered for three years, trying every remedy under the sun, but all proved of no avail. lat list bethought me that I would try Scott's Emulsion, which at that time a very delicate girl was taking to her great benefit. After taking, say, half-a-dozen battles or so, I seemed to gain more strength and put more flesh on to my bones, which before were appreciably getting more exposed. The pain seemed to gradually lessen as I gained tlesn and strength ; my food seemed to digest better, and the pains.became only of two or three months' recurrence. Persisting still in taking Scott's Emulsion up to the present date, I may say that I am now perfectly cured of these pains, and am well and healthy in every respect. I verily believe, had I not taken in hand your valuable tonic, I should not now have been alive to bear testimon% to the valuable properties of Scott's Emulsion, which I am now constantly recommending (and with marvel- lous effects in many cases) to all suffering similarly as I have for years suffered. My friends can all bear testimony to the wonderful changes it has effected on my constitution, and with every feeling of gratitude to you, I beg to subscribe myself, yours faithfelly, (signed) F. S. COLQUEIOUN, The Owston Ferry, Bawtry, Esgland, January 20th, 1899." Having read what precedes this letter, you may desire an explanation. Did it ever occur to you that a weak stomach may be the result of a weak condition of the whole system, a low state of vitality ? You probably know that when vitality is low the weakest part of you will give way, and in spite of all you do, this part will remain weak until the aggravating cause behind it is taken away.</p>
        <p>Scott's Emulsion is cod-liver oil, but not plain cod- liver oil alone. It is palatable, sweet, and free from decay and rancidity. It is also partially digested and gives nourishment to the system without taxing the digestive organs like ordinary food. It contains the hypophosphites of lime and soda, which aid digestion, stimulate the nerves, and sustain Ithe brain. It is the most rational way of taking cod-liver oil. If you are weak, emaciated, thin, coughing, anmmic, or sufferiug in some vital spot, try Scott's Emulsion. We venture to say you will be surprised at its beneficial effects.</p>
        <p>T.he Local Government Board have given a quali- fied approval to the Sheffield system of dealing with poor law children, the main feature of which is the placing of the children in cottage homes in the town, instead of in the country. It has been taken too much for greeted by those who have tried similar experiments in the past that cottage homes must be placed in the rural districts. It may be desirable.in principle to choose the most healthy surroundings, and to return as many people as pos- sible " back to the land." But better air does not always compensate for increased difficulty in super- vision, and if, as is often the case, children in the manufacturing districts are not fitted for an agricul- tural career, and only drift out of it ill-fitted for anything else, it is far better that they should be brought up in town streets and fitted for an urban artisan career. Sheffield has given a lead which ex- perienced men think may well be followed in other manufacturing districts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 4, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, DECEMBER 4, 1896.</p>
        <p>FOOTBA.LL: CI HO, G. BUSSBY &amp; CO.. are makers of High Class appil- ances for Football. Golf, Hockey, Lacrosse, and al iports and Games, also for Gymnasia and Physical Rxercises. Illustrated Catalogue may be obtained front local Dealers or he Sports Manufactory, London, S.H. Trade Mark G.G.B.</p>
        <p>kick into touch. Johnson was prominent by a good kick past midfield, and a moment later by a good run, being fetched down near the Oldham line within an ace of scoring. Oldham were now playing on the defensive. A free kick to Runcorn now saw Lightfoot make a good attempt at goal, the ball going outside. On resumption Runcorn again attacked, and Bradbury in replying sent up the field. Gregory rushed forward, and getting pos- session ran over near the corner and scored. Antrobus failed to improve the point. Johnson made his mark, but nothing resulted. Midfield was now the venue of play. A free kick to Oldham was teturned by Wilkin- son into touch. Johnson put in a good punt well into the Oldham half, and Bradbury replied into toueh. Again Johnson broke away, but was collared. The home forwards now dribbled over the line, forcing the visitors to kick dead. Nothing further was scored. Final re- sult :—Runcorn A. two tries; Oldham A. nil. RUNCORN RECREATION V. FRODSHAIL—This return match was played at Frodsham on Saturday, a good number of spectators witnessing the game, Sutton won the toss for the visitors, and the home captain kicked off against a slight breeze. The Recreation at once began to press, and the game was played in the home quarter during the greater part of the first half.but the tackling of the Frodshant men was most vigorous, and they pre- vented the visitors from making much score. Hughes, following up well, managed to get over, but the referee ruled him off-side, and after several unsuccessful at- tempt' Basnett succeeded in placing a goal from a mark, this being the only score up to the interval. The play in the second half was much better, the visitors' passing at times being splendid. The Frodsham men continued to play a heavy forward game, and gave their opponents no quarter in that respect, Tomlinson and Atherton both being off the fi:.ld together from injuries received, the former not being able to resume play. A Froclsham half-back had the misfortune to break one of his fingers, and also had to retire. Basnett tried a drop at goal from a favourable position, but the ball fell a little short, and the Frodsham full-back mulling. Melia, dashed up and scored for the visitors, the kick at goal failing. Later on a scrimmage was formed on the Runcorn line, and A scramble ensuing the referee awarded the home side a try, against which the Recreation made a strong appeal on the ground that the ball was played into touch in goal hut without avail, and shortly afterwards the game was finished with the following score :—Runcorn Recreation, one goal one try; Frodsham, one try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. OLDHAM V. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. OLDHAM V. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Much excitement existed both in'Oldham and Run- corn on the first meeting during the present season. Last year the teams met three times, the last being at Broughton-to settle the right of holding the fine cup be- longing to the Lancashire and Cheshire Section of the Northern Rugby Union. The Cestrians, after a hard fight, claimed the right of holding the cup for the first year, much to the satisfaction of all Cheshire men. Starting off again in the competition for the present sea- son the Oldham Club have been very successful, not having lost a single match, and having the record of nine matches played and nine won, with a score of 13.4 points for, and only 12 points against them. Rnneorn have' in the early part not quite so good a record. They unfortunately lost the matches with Widnes and More- cambe. The former was associated with a regrettable incident, when Houghton was Bert off from no fault of his own. The defeat at Morecambe was quite unex- pected. Runcorn being the cup-holders are awaited everywhere they go by the teams going in special train- ing to take them down. The record of the past few weeks has been excellent. At..Helens,Broughton Rangers, and Stockport all being defeati;d'its the result of sterling Play by the Runcorn team, who pride themselves that with one exception they play Runcorn men. Runcorn team: Houghton, back; Warder, Butterworth, Robin- son, and Myers. three-quarter backs; Faulkner and Robinson. half backs • W. Faulkner, Langley, Farmer, Walker, Reece, Lightfoot, Darlington, and Gayter. for- wards. Oldham: Eagland, back; Hurst, Taylor,Lees, and Martin, three-quarter backs; Varlet'? and Lees, half backs; Borsor, Furness, Hughes, Edwards, Wylie, Frater, Lees, and Murrell, forwards. Beyond a rather stiff breeze, the atmospheric conditions were excellent, and the ground was in first class condition. It was ten minutes past time when the hornesters appeared on the scene, and two minutes later the Runcernians, who had a very cerdial reception, en- tered the field. Oldham won the toss, and W. Faulkner started for Runcorn, Hurst making a bad return. A scrummage was formed in the 25, and a free kick was immediately awarded Oldham. Varley kicked, and there was an excellent return by Houghton. The Old- ham backs next showed some fine passing, but their efforts were neutralised,S.Lees being tackled by Warder. Runcorn gained an advantage by some smart passing, supplemented by a grand kick into touch by Butter- worth. A bit of rough play ensuedecreditable to neither lot of forwards. A smart kick into touch brought relief to Oldham, whose forwards followed -with a beneficial dribble. Runcorn retaliated with a kick into touch,and the fast play created the greatest enthusiasm. Harry Eagland was prominent with a long kick, and from a scrummage Runcorn showed up well, Eagland saving smartly. The Runcorn forwards exhibited some magni- ficent efforts,play settling close on the Oldham goal line, Faulkner being held just in time. A. free kick shortly afterwards proved a blessing to Oldham, and a moment afterwards Runcorn were also awarded a free kick for an infringement. Play settled for a brief period in midfield, and Arthur Lees made a sad mistake by sticking to the -ball too long. Butterworth was prominent with a neat kick into touch, and from a scrummage Robinson sent the ball into the Oldham 25. The game just now was fast and furious, bet h lots of forwards evidently being on their mettle,and leaving little for the backs to do. Tarley made a goad run, but was smartly tackled. Eagland immediately afterwards let Oldham down by misjudging the ball,and a free kick to Runcorn was the next noteworthy episode. Butterworth in the line-up, showed well, but as he was getting dangerous S. Lees came to the rescue. The referee gave another free kick right in front of the Old- ham goal, but Warder failed, his failure provoking immense cheering, which showed the feeling with which the contest was being regarded. Seldom has such an exhibition of enthusiasm been displayed on the Water- sheddings ground at an opponent's downfall. Runcorn still continued to press. Warder by a tricky run elicited the appreciation of the spectators, who could not refrain from cheering the effort. Butterworth attempted to drop a goal, and Hurst shortly afterwards found touch. A free kick was awarded to Oldham fcir an infringement in the scrummage, and Eagland kicking, Houghton replied beautifully. Arthur Lees found touch at centre, after which the forwards on both sides showed up again, and Hurst made his mark near the centre. Houghton soon after followed suit. A smart bout of passing by thp Oldham backs was spoiled by too hard kicking, Martin failing to ground the ball before it went dead. After some rough and tumble play, there was another good show of passing by Oldham, and Hurst was brought down in the Runcorn 25. Runcorn gained a big advan- tage by Eagland stepping in touch near his own line. He, however, shortly afterwards retrieved himself by a grand kick, and half-time arrived after a most stubborn contest with no score registered by either side. Ou re- suming, one of the Runcorn forwards made a smart run, and was within measurable distance of the line when he spoiled a grand effort by passing forward. Eagland had all his work cut out, but Oldham, gave one of their famous exhibitions of passing, the ball being transferred nearly the whole length of the field, Taylor grounding behind the posts, S. Lees converting. Warder again was conspicuous with grard relief, and the Runcorn for- wards followed up,taking the ball close to the homesters' line, where some hot scrummaging took place. Oldham had several narrow squeaks. A free kick to Ruricorn,in a good position, was token poor advantage of by Warder. Oldham, on good terms with themselves,acted on the defensive. Runeorn played for all they were worth, and prayed well, hut could not make headway, though they frequently had hard lines. The last ten minutes were piayed in Kank'Firitness, sad it was diffi- cult to distinguish the players. One of the best games witnessed at Water-sheddin,ge ended—Oldham one goal;' Runcorn nil. , WARRINGTON " A " v. ROCHDALE HORNETS "'A ". This return match was played at Warrington on Satur- day before 3,000 spectators. The afternoon was fine and well suited for football. Rochdale were represented by Ferris, full back; Sutcliffe, Collier, Isherwood, and Howarth. three-quarters; Fletcher and Mason, half- backs ; Higgin, Trevor, Tarr, P. Leach, J. Leach, A., Kershaw, Sixsmith, and Fletcher forwards. Warrington were represented by Boscow,full back; Lawles, Barber, O'Brien, and Radford, three-quaeters ; Houghton and Foden, half-backs ; Higham, Scholtz-;, Dakin,Fairhurst, Andrews, Wilcock, Meacock, awl Thornton, forwards.— l'he Hornets started and play settled in War- rington quarters. Lawless over-ran the ball and Radford only just prevented the visitors scor- ing, by kicking dead. By a tremendous kick aided by the wind Houghton transferred play to the Hornets 25. Warrington soon assumed the aggressive and Fletcher being penalised, Barber placed a splendid goal,. After this the homesters held the upper hand and several smart passing mevements were wit- nessed, but for some time they came to nothing. The Hornets looked like relieving when Barber, who had stumbled, threw the ball to Boscow who promptly put it in touch near the corner. Another penalty kick was taken by Barber, but the attempt failed and the Hornets pressed for a time. Warrington again got near the visi- tors' line where Wilcock almost scored. Boscow had hard lines - in dropping at goal, but a moment later , Houghton transferred to Foden who passed to Bar- ber. The latter dcdeed his opponents and raced over the line, converting his try very neatls. Half time came with Warrington leading by 8 points to nil. Dakin re- started, and Wilcock initiated a good passing move- ment between Lawless, Radford, and Barber, but the latter knocked on. From a line out J. Leach got away and was only just pulled up on the line. Mason almost dropped a goal, and a particularly fine run and kick by Boscow gave immediate relief. The Hornets, however, got on the home line again and commenced a long siege. Man after man attempted to dodge over, only to get into the hands of the Warrington backs. Barber and Boscow tackled grandly, and eventually the visitors were forced back. The whistle sounded for time, leav- ing Warrington winners by two goals 1 penalty), eight points, to Rochdale nil. WARRINGTON ST• MART'S V. LOSTOCK GRALkM.—Played on St Mary's ground on Saturday Warburton winning the toss, the visitors started against the wind, Berry making a good return. By a free kick the Saints got into the Lostock quarter, and Buckley made a good run to the line, Bennett eventually scoring a good try. On resuming the aisitora were again penalized, and no return being made Bennett again scered,the kick failing. The game continued to !se one-sided, Gregory and War- buton scoring tries, Siddall improving the first one. At half-time the Saints were leading by one goal and three tries to nil. On resuming Lostock pressed, but tipe home team soon transferred play to half-way, and after a rush by the forwards Edgar scored. From the kick-off the play was at the centre, where Bennett got possession, and passing to Buckley, the latter handed to Dakin, who tricked his opponents and scored, Berry improving. Near the close.the visitors obtained a try after a loose rush, May converting. The whistle was sounded for time shortly afterwards, the Saints winning somewhat easily by two goals, four tries (22 points) to Lostock Gralam, one goal (five points). RUNCORN " A " v. OLDHAM "A."—Played at Runcorn, before a moderate gate. Woods started for Runcorn, but his men being off side, a seri -omage was ordered, in midfield. After some give-and-take play, Runcorn gra- dually v orked up to the Oldham line. where Betley at- tempted a drop at goal, but the ball failed to rise, and rolled dead. Runcorn pressed, and the visitors were awarded a free kick on the goal line. Sellers took it,and Poole put a neat return into touch. Wilkinson imme- diately after made his mark and sent into touch. Half- time arrived with no score,. After the interval Cunning- ham started for Oldham, and Johnson made his mark, and put in a good reply. Sellers returned to Lightfoot, and the last-named sent into touch. The home forwards lot away with a rush, Johnson being conspicuous with a good run, and play was soon at the Oldham line. Reece got possession a moment later and scored. Gre- gory made a good attempt at goal from a difficult po- sition, the tall just falling outside. On resumption Runcorn attacked, but Bradbury cleared his side with,</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA is both a stimulating and refresh- ing drink, and a nutritious food, probably more nourishing than any other beverage. It is absolutely pure, containing uo foreign adatiatura.—Adv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>A.SSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>A.SSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Sr. ELPHIN'S V. CELESTES- COLLEGE.—At Dalton sank on Saturday. A. Duckworth resumed his position as full-back and Baillie was given a trial in the forwards for the Saints. The College had also a good team up, and as they had not been defeated last season nor this season so far, a difficult straggle was expected. Chester kicked off, and the Saints assisted by the wine com- menced to press. The Chester backs were hard to pass, and the Saints' forwards were held in check for a time. At last the ball was worked up to goal. and Baillie being given a chance scored with p. splendid shot. After some mid-field play Peadlebury received, and running down to the line sent in a grand centre, and Baillie shot hard in, the ball cannoning off a Chester back through the goal. After the interval Chester had the best of the game, until towards the close, when a long shot was put in and, Baillie tackling the goal-keeper before he could clear, Taylor received the ball in the goal mouth and scored. Final :—St. Elphin's, 3 goals ; Chester Col- late, 0. BARLESTOWN V. SHELMFIRSDALE. —The above teams played their return fixture in the Alliaece league, at Earlestown on Saturday afternoon. When the teams met at Shelmersdale, Earlestown succeeded in olefcating the home eleven by three goals to one. This victory was turned into a defeat on Saturday, when Skelmers- dale managed to lower the home colours by. rese goal to nothing. Skelmersdale kicked off down hill 25 minutes late, owing to the non arrival of the referee, and in con- sequence of this, the game had te be conducted towards the close in semi-darkness. A quick return by Limond brought the visitors' goal in danger, which Gibson re- lieved. Play was transferred to Midfield, and Skelmers- dale secured a corner, which was badly placed. EdMonson and Harrison got up and a nicely Placed kick by the backs just grazed the bar, of Hughes's head. Another good run by Banks brought up Earlestown, but Gibson relieved, and the visitors again got down, final shot going wide. A foul close in goal gave Skelmersdale a chance, but Mather cleared, and Eitnonson running the length of the field caused Keyon to handle. The visitors again got down, but their shooting was erratic. The brothers Hughes got well up, and some long passing enabled Banks to make an attempt, his effort just going outside. Play was of an even character, and both goals were visited alternately, but the defence on each side could not be pierced, and half-time arrived with a clean sheet. With the hill in their favour it was expected Earles- town would:open the scoring,but althoueh they severely pressed the visitors and secured a number rf corners, they were unable to break the defence. A break-away by Blake for Skelmersdale enabled that player to put the goal at his mercy, and he was successful in piercing the Earlestown citadel. The Earlestown players lacked combination, and unless some improvement is made in the forward division, it is evident Earlestown's posses- sion of the Alliance championship will be short lived. The goal scored by Skelmersdale was the only point registered during the game, and the home team had to retire beaten for the first time in two seasons on their own grou</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>FROM THE " BIRMING-HANI TIMES.". REMARKABLE AFFAIR.</head>
        <p>FROM THE " BIRMING-HANI TIMES.". REMARKABLE AFFAIR.</p>
        <p>The " Birminrrham Titllos " sent a representative to Aiss N. Malin, 245, Pershore-road,Edgbaston, Birming- ham, the other day, who verified an inteiesting and inal.:nitant narrative. Miss 'Malins, said the replrter, was the piczure of perfect healtb, but her story began with particulars of a serious illness. She said : "I had been ailing for more than eighteen months, suffering from anmmia. My blood was going thin, and very watery. I could not get upstairs only with the greatest trouble, and sometimes had to rest to recover my breath. When I went out of doors it seemed too much for me, and I returned weak and low spirited. My doctors did not succeed in curing me. I naturally began to despair and left off talricr.; all medicine,until one day Enw an acc:th: ct of the wonderful cures effected by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and determined to try one box. The first few doses wrought a marvellous change in my appearance, and when I had finished the box I felt a new woman.</p>
        <p>"And you really believe that your health is due to these pills ?" " I do, indeed. I have the greatest faith in them,and shall be pleased to recommend them to any of my friends who may suffer in a similar way. lam quite sure it was Dr. Williams' Pink Pills that did it, bocause I load given up taking anything else. Some time before I had been tryina so-called blood purifiers, but all to no purpose." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Miss Malios added. " Perhaps you would, like to hear about my motiser. She was fifty years of age, and was suffering excruciating pains from swellings in the legs, arid other symptoms attending what is called " change of life : "she could only get about with assist- ance. She tried various remedies without any benefit, and at last I procured her a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. The effect was magical. The swellings immedi- ately began to disapp4ar,and before she had finished the box he was quite well again. That is also why I have such faith in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. My mother lives at 156, Barrows Hill, Chesterfield, Derbyshire, and she has writteu me about her wonderful cure." Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cure anieuia and other disorders arising from impoverished blood, as well as rheumatism, sciatica, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, palpitations, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, shortness of breath, pains in the back, early decay, all forms of female weakness, hysteria, low vitality, and wasting strength from any cause. These Pills are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and are sold by chemists,and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn. viaduct, London, E.C., at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 91. Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars are not genuine ; do not accept them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>EXAMINATION FOR LABOUR CERTIFICATES</head>
        <p>EXAMINATION FOR LABOUR CERTIFICATES</p>
        <p>tier Majesty's Inspector, Mr. J. Sinkins, examined a numoer of children from the several elementary schools in Warrington for labour certificates at St. Mary's Schools, Butterma.rket-street on Monday. There were three third standard candidates, 52 from the fourth staadard. 256 from the fifth standard, and 31 from the sixth standard. GOODS TRAIN ON FIRE AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>At half-past four on Tuesday morning, as an express goods train was passing through Latchford, the signalman in the Latchford station box noticed that one of the trucks was ablaze. He at once stopped the train, and the burning truck which was loaded with cotton, was placed in the siding. A breakdown gang was soon at work putting the fire out, but it was not until noon that this was efffr.cted. The truck was burnt out altogether, only the metal frame-work being left. It is not known how the fire originated but it is probably due to sparks falling from the engine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>AROUND THE M DITERRANEAN.</head>
        <p>AROUND THE M DITER- RANEAN.</p>
        <p>BY DORA M. JONES.</p>
        <p>SS. Midnight Sun.</p>
        <p>I never in my life saw anything to approach for beauty the colouring of the coast as we entered the Bay of Tunis this morning. The sea was a rich and brilliant green-blue, like the colouring on the neck of a peacock, .and the cliffs along which we were coesting glowed like the heart of a dusky rose. Soon we had a good view of the Promontory of Carthage, the white houses of La Marsa, the Bey's residence on the headland, then the Acropolis,onee girdled with fortresses and crowned with temples, now a bare mound, with the white cathedral, dedicated to St. Louis, standing out conspicuously against the sky. We were landeJ at La Goulette, formerly the port of Tunis. The French have now canalised the Lake, so that vessels can go right up to the town, but as we were bound for Carthage, we did not follow that plan. We received a good deal of attention from the inhabitants of La Goulette. chiefly Maltese and Jea s, who lined the road ,and stood en their doorsteps and in their balconies, staring at us with the most flattering curiosity. The attire of the Jewish ladies was too wonderful for des- cription : baggy white trousers, a silk girdle round the hips' a full muslin tunic and short gay eilk jacket,and a high conical structure on the head, from which floated a long white veil. As they were nearly all immensely stout, the effect was more curious than graceful. Some of the younger girls, in simple white or yellow cotton dresses, with their dark curly hair knctted carelessly at the back of the head, were distinctly pleasing. The other concomitants of the drive were heat, smells, flies, dust, market-gardens with prickly pear hedges, and mean little white houses at intervals.</p>
        <p>As we drew near the Cathedral, I thought how per- sistently the Cross returns to every position it has once occupied. It was built by Cardinal Lavigerie—whose name will be mentioned in future days with that of Manning and Gibbons, as one of the great champions of humanity whom the modern Roman Church has pro- duced—in memory cf the capture of Carthage by St. Leuis during the sixth crusade, and his death here in 1270. I thought the building somewhat pretentious without and tawdry within, but ills impossible to stand unmoved on the spot where one of the noblest models of Christian knighthood gave up "his pure soul unto his Captain Christ, under whose 'olours he had fought so long," and where the good Lavigerie, a comrade in the same warfare, though parted by so many centuries from him, after his long warfare sleeps also. Behind the Basilica is the Carmelite Convent and Museum, also founded by the late Cardinal,and contain- ing the results of recent exploration en the site of old Carthage. In the museum gardens is a small decayed chapel of St. Louis,built by Louis Philippe, and contain- ing a marble statue of the soldier-saint. Here is also the monument of Count Mathieu de Lesseps, father of the engineer of Suez Canal, with an inscription recording his share in the conclusion ef the treaty with the Bey of Tunis in 1850, by which Christian slavery was abolished. All these buildings are on the Byrsa or Acropolis of Carthae,e. Beneath our feet, piled into mounds of indis- tinguishable dust, were the ruins of three great civilisa- tions—the Carthage of the Pheeeicians, the Carthage of the Roman Lair ire, and lastly, Christian Carthage, with its memories of Cyprian and Augustine. .)n our drive to Tunis, across the flat plain, bounded by a semi-circular range of hills, we had the Lake on our left and beyond us the Za.eruan Mountains. The road seemed almost deserted. Once a suite of carriagr rolled past, containing the Bey • and the ladies of the harem on their way back to La Marsa. Then we passed two fresh-faced, pleasant-looking nuns from the Carme- lite convent, or again a solitary Arab, pacing in solemn state upon his mule. The deep red of a gorgeous sun- set flushed the whole sky, and was reflected with the purple hills in the waters of the Lake. It seemed a fit close vs all our musing on dead heroism and glories. We had arrived too late at Tunis the day before to see anything cf the town. The more enterprising spirits among us, therefore, acting on information obtained for Us by Mr. Perowne, left the ho it about half-past seven this morning and caught the 8 a.m. train from La Gou- lette for Tunis. This little,rail way is an Italian enter- prise; ticket, time-tables, and everything of the kind are printed in Italian. On alighting at Tunis station you find yourself eppar i ently n a French town; a fine wide street, lined with good shops and hotels and planted with trees, leads up to the massive Moorish gateway known as the Bab-el-Bahr, and here the tramways branch off in every direction. We went scraight up the Rue de Kasbah, the chief street of the Arab quarter. It is narrow, but beautifully clean and well-paved, with a little gutter running down the middle and lined with Arab shoes. The women we saw here wear masks of black stuff, with slits for the eyes, which give them a most uncanny and ghoul-like appear- ance.</p>
        <p>At tee top of the street we found the Dar-A-Bey, or Bay's palace. The titular ruler of Tunis resides at La Marsa, but comes here about once a week to transact Government business. The large central court, decor- ated with black and white tiles, is vaulted over with a glass dome. Out of it open on one side small sleeping- rooms, decorated in the same style, and on the other the state apartments, which are spoiled by hangings and " chair-backs " of gaudy yellow silk and muslin, enor- mous cheval glasses,and cheap woodcuts in common gilt frames.</p>
        <p>We climbed innumerable stairs to the top of the palace where our perseverance was rewarded by the curious spectacle spreai out before us. Acres of flat roofs ex- tended almost without a break, for nearly all the stretts and bazaars of the Arab quarter are covered over on account of the heat. They are sacred to the women in the daytime ; also, it appeared, to geese, chickens, and dogs. Flowers grew along the terraces, and mats and rugs'were spread about. In the hot nights the Moorish population ascends to sleep here under the stars. The few streets that cut through this dead level seemed like narrow, deep canals, and slender minarets rose here and there from the housetops, the largest quite close to the palace. This belonged to the Grand MoFque, and from its gilded balcony every morning the Muezzin gives the signal to the other mosques of the city, and the hoarse chant goes up with one accord from every quarter, call inr, the faithful to prayer in the name of Allah the only gr"eat and Mohammed his prophet. Beyond the flat roofs of the city we had a magnificent view of the Lake and the lofty ridge of mountains beyond, with the Bay in the distance. We had still to do our duty by the Tunisian bazaars, which are really the most unique things of the kind out of Cairo. They are narrow lanes entirely covered, and each devoted to one class of merchandise. The goods and the Arab salesmen together made a perfect kaleido• scope of colour. Some of the vendors of haiks,fezes,and Oriental silks would have been invaluable at s charity bazaar. They were so amiable, so ingenious, above all so persistent. " Spik English," exclaims a gentleman in a lovely blue hark, rushing up and extendine his hand with the ardour of an old friend to the leader of our natty. " Touencz tal" and he sweeps the whole party off irresistibly into his particular den, spreading out scarees,mantles, embroidery, and chattering nineteen to the dozen. He flings a silk scarf round the shoulders of one of the ladies. " Buy this for madame. Yes, mon- sieur, very pretty,very good." Madame and monsieur happen never to have ex .hanged a word; she looks stony and he embarrassed. Next the indefatigable one fixes on the most timid and girlish-looking of the party. "Buy it, mademoiselle. Very pretty. You will have a rich husband. Lots of money. Yes, only trente francs." In a few minutes he is down at fifteen. Some one suggests eight. He moves off with a gesture of supreme contempt. The party turn to leave; he flies after them. " Combien, monsieur, cernbien." The eight francs is offered again and accepted ; the salesman shakes hands again exclaiming, "Good-night, good- night," with great fervour (it being about 11 a.m.), and we go a few steps down the bazaar and begin the same thing all over again. We took the tram round part of the town without perceiving anything of interest. wiles were everywhere, and the provision dealers sat on their counters flourish- ing long switches. The coinage gave us a little trouble —it is practically French, with the value in Arabic en the reverse side; but French money is not accepted b, tram conductors and railway officials, though the shop- keepers take it willingly enough. French is everywhere spoken and understood, and seems, in fact, the official language of North Africa.</p>
      </div>
