      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>GRAPHIC ACCOUNT OF A VISIT TO A</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER PALACE OF INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>The Press is the most powerful lever of modern civilisation. The pulsations of thought. words, and actions cabled each day, flashei from every quarter of the globe, are conveyed to the human brain by mean. of the Press. This constitutes the Press the most interesting the most, instructive, and the most fascinating invention and production of the march of in- tellect. Kings, Emperors. and floverrnments have been swept from power by means of the Press. Both gend and evil ewe much to the influence of the Press. The newapaner forms the chief source of delight of interest. and of instruction at the morning breakfast-tahle. If the newspaper surl-lenlv ceased to exist then half the enjoyment of the world would cease also Even the advertisement columns form a source of pleasure to many readers. The ladies carefully examine their Society papers for fashions : sales of milinery and dress- goods have special iuterest for the ladies. It was whilst earr fully noting the heads of different advertisements that I was attracted by the unique announcement of a G,gantie Fur- aishing Establishment at GAYTHORN, Manchester.</p>
        <p>I d.termined, after consultation with my wife, to proceed to " '7 he Pantheon." ct, Gaythorn, in order to test the acm- racy of this advertisement. Accordingly we set cut, and eventually reacher] Piccadilly. We made straight for the. " Royal Hotel," at the corner of Mosley-street, and after in- quiry we then took a ear bound for Alexandra Park, which' we were informed. passed the very door of "The Panthi an ' at Gaythorn, which is close to the Central Stotion. We reqmested the guard to stop the car t " Woralev's." On arriving there we fouryl that " The Pantheon "was a palatial structure, being nothing less than an Immense Warehouse, with huge niate-glasa windows forming a frontage of over 100 feet. We had hardly patience to examine the windows, so great was our anxiety to examine the interior. Antic:nation is often more enjoyable than realisation, but in this else rea- lisation altogether overwhelmed our anticipation. Our visit to "The Pantheon" proved to be most enjoyable. Upon examining the windows our attention was riveted upon a magnificent display of Clippendale Furniture, beautifully earved in</p>
        <p>BAS-RELIEF. It is the Finest Exhibition of Furniture of this class that I have ever seen. The Cabinets are original in design, and from what we learned afterwards are the very latest— specially drawn and manufactured for Worslev's. I also noticed some very high class Pollard Oak Furniture for the dinincr-room. magnificent specimens of bedroom furniture, besides a miscellaneous collection of household requirements. There were many other specimens of furniture on show ; but we shall now proceed inside the premises and attempt to give a brief description of what we saw there. Upon Passim( through the entrance we were immediately struck with amazement by the</p>
        <p>GORGEOUS DISPLAY. The ground floor of this establishment,, we were in- formed had just been re-modelled and re-furnished. My wife immediately expressed astonishment and admiration at the very unique display of the most beautiful and exquisite furnitnre we have ever seen. Magnificent speeimens of bed- room fu niture of the very highest class. at 75, 85, 100, and 150 guineas. We saw the most perfect. the most charming. the most delightful bcdroom (mites in Chippendale mahogany • that it has ever teen our pleasure to inspect. Prices rule from the very lowest for Chippendale furniture from 10 to 60 guineas. We have paid visits to the best establishments in anal around Manchester, but certainly no establishment in my experiences has such a collection of high-class furnitnae RS Worsley's have We. therefore, concluded that we were In az establishment for the rich only, hut we soon disabused our minds of that idea upon inspecting the tremendous stock which is here displayed throughout the various gieantie showrooms. Worsley'a ,cster for the working man, the tradesman, and the merchant with the long purse. Their stock is so Immense that you can buy a bedroom suite for £3 155.. or you can pur. chase one for 150 guineas. Continuing our inspection of the ground floor. I must mention that the place is beautifully lighted by electricity. You can, therefore, see everything distinctly. The powerful light would soon lay bare any defects. if any existed. Specimen suites of Dining-room Fueniitire in pollard oak, sp cimen suites of Dining-room Furniture in Chippendale mahogany of the most delightful design were mark's"' in plain figures at extremely low prices. Our next attraction was the Carpet Showroom upon the same too-. The display did credit to the gentleman who is in cheeee of this denertment. and was remarkable in some Instances for the delicate and artistic prism' tic effects, while in other eases they present a gorgeous embellishment of delf7htful hue and brilliance I noticci that the best productions of the newest patterns for next spring have actually been secured already, and we were given to understand that the next spring's patterns have not yet been shown to any competitore in the ordin- ary way. We noticed the following prices • Five-frame Brussela,2s 2ad per yard: special Brussels at 28 41,1 per yard ; extra Cut line Brussels, 2s Thd per yard ; superior 13-nesels at 2s lilul per yard ; best Five-frame Prussels at 3s 6d per yard • British-made taxminaters at 3e 3d and 7s Ad per yard; best Royal Axminsters at 3s 110 per yard (we have netiped these at 5s 14,1 retail in other establishments) ; best all- Wool Arid°. at 5s 6d e'er yard ; the Cromnton Axmiester at 7a lid per yard. The Crompton Axminater is specially recommended as the finest carpet extant, the most beautiful designs, and the loveliest colours procurable. The Princess Seamless Axminster Squares. 12ft by 9ft, at £4 19s 91. We afterwards took the trouble of comparing the prices RS ehewn In this department with the prices on other carpet displays in Manchester and district, and the result of our investiga- tions nroved to us ereeebisively that Worsley's carnets are quite 30 per cent blow the ordinary retail prices. Wersley's -Daces are wholesale prices.</p>
        <p>WE PROCEEDED UPSTAIRS. The first showroom is the Dining-room Furniture Showroom Sideboards are planed in corridors, with the suites in the centre. We were astonished at the immense variety and the tremendous stock of furniture Sideboards. with plate-Rims hacks, are here exhibited at 355.. £2 5s , 3 guineas, and 4 guineas ; the most extraordinary value, in walnut. mahogany.and oak. Magnificent produ-tions in pol- lard oak at 30 guineas 75 guineas 40 guineas. and 60 guineas will show at once the immense variety of the stock Dining- mem Suites, of whien there are over 150 on show, start from £4l'.. and go on step by step up to 75 guineas ; they are up olstered in all kinds of materials—velvets, pegamoiel.aed morocco., Overmentels to match the Sideboards are also on show. Dining if aMes from 255. to £25. We have paid visits to msnv furnishing establishments, but the prices fixed to each article at Worselev's simply defy bewilcier,snd pulverise any possible competition We understand every Suite is guar- anteed.and the money will be returned if any reasonable ob- jection be found with them after deliverv.worslev's eschew the jerry furniture makers.and will only trado with manufactures who will guarantee them against loss if it is to be found fanitv. We were next conducted to the new Pisno Saloon. which is one of the latest additions to "The Pantheon." and has been reconstructed during the month of Octobsr. It has been doubled in size and forms one of the most attractive features of The Pantheon " lere we found Ft magnificent collection of Musimil instrnments, both new and second- hand. by all the best makers, at womclerfully low prices. Second-hand Pianos to Bret the working class from the very low price of 30s £2 l's £3 10s. £5, up to £lO. We next saw a eollecidon of a better chef, of seeond-hand Pianos ranging from £lO to £2O; any quantity to select from. From this point we nnw inspected a most. wonderful collection of new Instruments, 11 marked at whnle,ale prices. We were con- vinced upon examination that these Instruments are quite 25 per cent less than the retail mnsic-seller's price Pianos bY Ki-kman lkirceitnever. Bechstein. Tlopkinson, may he pur- ennsed for 28 g.,ninetts. We should strongly advise anyone requiring a good pianofore to visit Worslev's before pur- •chasing.</p>
        <p>DkEZLINC4 A PPE AR. ANCE. The Bedstead Showroom at Wmrsley's presents an ap- pearance of a sea of cold. illatmificent specimens of the brasaworkere art are here dis,laved to perfection Brass Bed- ater,?s may be purchased from 35s to £35. Brass and iron Bedsteeds from Es 6d. to 50a. Of course Worsley's ester for all classes in this department. just as we have described in the other departments. There is the common Iron Bedstead for half a cuinea. There is the macnificent and latest, pro- ducti n of Hoskins and Sewell for 40 cmineas. A Persian Bed- stead. enamelled all brass, complete with beaatiful set, of Oran,rr and Shetland wool overlay. c.., he se^nred for £3 17a 6d. Woraley's have ore, 2000. ty,lateads in stack, nearly the whale of which we rind -rstand.were sec rail before thn advance in nrices. We understand ,Iso that Worslev's e.• for Tioaritais and Institutions at, a specially reduced sc,l ContinninT, our round *4 lnsnention. we were next con'-e.tecl to the showronsn speeially devoted to R 1-I,•nam Fur* ilure. We can confidently asse t that the display of furniture for bedrooms at WOralAV'R Is the moo, wonderful eolleetion to be seen in the whole North of Bncland The yi•itor is struck by wh,t annears to be miles of c,rrilors of Bero im Suttee. The wardrobes of each suite stand shoulder to shoulder like a</p>
        <p>N T OP SOLDERS. AA/ A ccess to each of the Suites is gift. a simple matter, stifficirnt space beim allowed in corri,lnrs for examinstion an inspeetion. There are Suites at £3 15... PI 15,.. £5 lin.. £6lss £7 151., 11 guineas. 12 guineas, 1; guineas. up to 50 guineas. The working man, to whom economy ia of the most vital imonrtence, can he suited he-e quits as well as the merchant. Suites in solil walnut Tlressing-table, Washstand Chsira Pedestal, an I Towel-rail, are supplied as 8 and 9 guineas per Suite. Money returned not, satisfactory, and guaranteed for 1C years. For £2B von can obtain a wonder- fully cheap Suit of Furniture in the very atest style, beauti- fully carved, sol:ithron,hout in either walnut or Clinpendals mahorrany. /1' 90 noticed an immence coliection of Wardrobes in 11 kinds of wood, from the painted wardrobe of 35s lip to the hug, Snanish mehogany Wardrnhe. 71t. long, at 18 rrnine-ls 21 eninoqq, and 25 guineas Dressiwy-tables and Washstands painted at 12s 64 18s 64, 455, and -23 ss. The next, Showroom we ‘serP conducted to was one im- mediately next to "The Pantheon," over the" Albion Mills." It is crammed jam-full of second-hand Furniture of a miscel- laneous character. where if von want a cheap Int, von will have no difficulty in finding it. The first- lot, of Furniture to attraet our attention was the Difice Furniture. There Is an immense stock of all kinds rf Deiks—Flat-ton Desks with four-legs from 20s. Slat ton Desks on pedest .1 drawers from £7 15s. Register Desks from 5 guilleas. Cylinder Desks 6 guineas, Stand-up Office. Desks from ' guineas ; but. the most important of all is the sp'endid stock of Ameerm Desks. The prices attached to thee Desks would alrnos', make one hink that they are being</p>
