      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>tetters ta the etittor,</head>
        <p>tetters ta the etittor,</p>
        <p>REGINA T. HAMPSON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have been asked so often since the trial of Mr. Hampson as to whether the conviction and sentence will interfere with his pension, that I shall be glad if you will permit me through your columns to inform those desirous of knowing, that his pension will not be affected ; inasmuch as by Section 2of "The Forfeiture Act 1870 " a pension payable to any person shall only be forfeited in cases of conviction for treason or felony,and not misdemeanour, under which latter description Mr. Hampson's case comes.—Yours truly, ARTHUR BROWNE. Warrington, 27th Nov., 1096.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>"BRABAZON" EMPLOYMENT SCHEME AT OUR WORKHOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE "BRABAZON" EMPLOYMENT SCHEME AT OUR WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln common with nearly all your correspondents upon the above subject, I greatly deplore the result of the Board's unwise decision to have nothing to do with the above movement. Although it was distinctly pointed out when under discussion by the Board,that the scheme had been, and was, at that very time, being worked at various Union Workhouses, and notably at Chester,with the happiest results, and adding greatly to the comfort and delight of the poor inmates, whilst the adoption of it would not impose upon the ratepayers any additional expense whatever, and if found in the working of it, in any degree impracticable or objectionable it could at once, it was urged, to discontinued without injury or inconvegience to any human being ; yet, after the deliv- ery of unanswerable spec:hes supporting this beneficent scheme, and other speeches against it, in which there was not a shred of argument, it was lost! lost ! by a majority of one 1 " Against eLapidity," Schiller says, "The Gods fight in vain." Although it may be said, that stupidity is a misfortune and not a fault, there is a cranky, cantankerous, pragmatical stupidity, which sets itself up as superior to all plain and obvious considerations and claims the gift of seeing through stone walls, and of, proving that two and two make four only for common folk, an offence surely, against decency and common sense, and particularly when directed against a commit- tee of ladies, whose sole object in urging forward this benevolent scheme was a laudable desire to brighten the monotonous lives of the most helple..-3 of our Work- house inmates. Speaking generally, I may say Bumble- dom has often, and deservedly, been subjected to scath- ing criticism, both before and since the days of Dickens. It would almost appear, that the appointment to the official guardianship of the poor acts like a forcing frame in bringing out all the littleness of mind, hard- ness of heart, and obtuseness of judgment, that may lurk in a man's character, and otherwise remain unseen and undeveloped. " Bigotry" and" Bumbledom "seem well nigh convertible terms, with a dash of mental nat.- rowness,and workhouse officialism thrown in as a make, weight. But I would say to these active-minded good ladies, to whom the ratepayers of the Union owe so much for their unselfish and humane labours, and par- ticularly in the regeneration they have worked at the Padgate Schools, be not d;scouraged ladies ! You mu st and will ultimately succeed in your philanthropic la- bours on behalf of th o whom, we are told, we shall always have with us, even to the end of the world.— Yours truly, SENEX.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>-0,-. STEA.XLBOA.T SERVICE BETWEEN LIVERPOOL AND WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>-0,-. STEA.XLBOA.T SERVICE BETWEEN LIVER- POOL AND WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington may now claim to have a daily steam service to Liverpool via the river and Ship Canal. This important development was inaugur- ated on Thursday morning la ,z when the Bishop's Wharf and Shipping Co., Limited, docked their first dircet steamer from Liverpool at their Warrington wharf, which it may be stated is near to the Castle Rubber Works. In close contiguity to the wharf a commodious warehouse has been erected by the Company, the entire work having been placed in the hands of Mr. J.Mounfield, builder. In the base- ment a gas engine las been fitted up and this sup- plies motive power for a Temperley transporter. This transporter is a recent invention for discharging and loading cargo The one in use at Warrington will lift any material up to fifteen cwts direct from the hold of the vessel and convey it along an iron beam into the warehouse or place it on carts which may be drawn alongside the wharf. Mr. Alexander Carson, of Liverpool, has been appointed managing dire or of the company, and there will be offices at both Liverpool and Warrington. The Liverpool address is Ba, Rumford-place, and the loading berth, Southwest side Prince's Dock. The com- pany intend to carry on the business of warehouse- men,shipping and forwarding agents. For the pre- sent, the service will be confined to one steamer per day from both Liverpool and Warrington. Their two steamers are the " Hope "and "Samson," and they have a carrying capacity of about 100 tons each. The fist one to reach Warrington was the Samson, which arrived about eight o'clock on Thursday morning with a general cargo of merchandise. The consignment included seventy bales of cotton for Messrs. Armitage and Rigby, Limited,and a quantity of tanners' material for the Tanners'-lane Tanning Company, Limited. The vessel was speedily discharged and afterwards loaded with borax, iron, &amp;c., and departed in the evening for Liverpool. This new development at- tracted considerable attention, and there is every prospect of the venture being a great success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WARRINGTON CONDEMNED MAN.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON CONDEMNED MAN.</p>
        <p>VISIT OF RELATIVES TO WALTON GAOL. PAINFUL INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, the condemned man James Bate, of Warrington, who is under sentence of death for the murder of his wife, was visited at Walton Gaol by several relatives, namely, Bate's mother, who lives at Warrington, his twin children, Elizabeth and John Willie Bate, aged six years, and a married cousin, Mrs. Ann Naylor. They reached Liverpool at 12.15, the railway journey berng evidently one of great delight to the two children, who had never he3n to Liverpool or in a train before. Their childish glee was in marked contrast to the grief-stricken feeling of their grandmother, who fully realised the painful ordeal before her. The first obligation of the party was to secure the necessary order for admittance to the prison signed by a magistrate, and after the usual routine this was obtained, and they proceeded by train to Walton. The anticipation of the children seemed to rise as the destination of their journey neared, and their frequent expression that "We are going to SAC daddy flow" was very touching. The permit acted as an open sesame to the ponderous outer gates of the prison, but, it being visiting day, the party were subjected to a delay of an hour, which was spent in a cold, cheerless, fireless ante-room. The tedium of wait- ing was relieved by the motley stream of visitors that kept passing to and fre, and the Liverpool "Black Maria," with her involuntary crew of in-comers, passed through to the inner quadrangle where they were duly deposited. Many of the visitors, it may be mentioned, were apparently lady misaionaees. When four o'clock sounded no further visitors were admitted.</p>
        <p>The credentials of the party having meantime been closely scrutinised in the Governer's department, and everything been found in order, at twenty minutes to five, amidst the gathering gloom of the prilon, the massive inner iron gates were opened, and the party passed through and were conducted to the iron-barred chamber. They were ushered in by a kindly warder,and found the prisoner James Bate in the adjoining room, anxiously awaiting them. The mutual recognition be- tween the mother and her only son—her only child—was of a very painful character. This was intensified when the children, and particularly the boy, who seemed to be his father's special favourite, exclaimed,"Why wan't you let me come nearer you, daddy ; I can't see you ?" A narrow passage, closely barred, separates the visitors from the visited. In answer to his child's entreaty, the prisoner stood beneath the gas and said, " Can you see me now ?" The warder kindly lifted the one child in his arms, and then the other, so that they might have a better view. "Why, daddy,' exclaimed John, the boy. "vou are growing whiskers,and are you a scldier now?" Then turning to the warder, he said, " Open that door, and let me in to daddy; you have enno' o' keys. The impossible request was gently refused,and the conversa- tion turned upon the incidents of the case, and more es- pecially as to the efforts for a reprieve. When informed that his petition had been signed by the Mayor and the leading people of the town, the hopes of the condemned man were greatly raised. Through the courtesy of tke officials the interview was allowed to last from twenty minutes to five to twenty minutes past. The parting, as it may he expected, was of an even more pathetic nature, the children utterly failing to comprehend why their father was not to return with them, and sadly and sorrowfully date had to put them 6ff with the promise that he would come home next week and fetch something with him. The mother and the cousin were almost equally affected. It was not until seven o'clock that the party reached Warrington, after a never-to•he-forgotten experience. Two coaling of the condemned man visited him last Saturday and found him bearing up well. He made a special request to see his daughter Maggie, who gave evidence against him at the trial, but so far it has not been decided whether she will be taken to the prison. Mrs. Bate has received two letters from her son, in which he also refers hopefully to the prospect of a reprieve. The petition is now being considered by the Home Secretary, whose decision may be expected almost immediately, as the execution is fixed to take place next Tuesday.—Liverpool "Daily Post."</p>
        <p>The order for Dr. Jam release was received at Holloway Prison on We.ineso ay, and at nine o'clock at night he was quietly removed in an ambulance by some of bis friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY. DECEMBER 4, 1996</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY. DECEMBER 4, 1996</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN A.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Police Station, Edrlestown, on Monday morning,before Mr. Borron, Elizabeth Russell of no fixed abodetwas charged with being drunk at New- ton, and in default of paying a fine of 28. 6d. and costs,she was sent to gaot for seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ACCIDRNT ON THE MARKET GROUND.</head>
        <p>ACCIDRNT ON THE MARKET GROUND.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon a youth named Job Watkinson, living in Wellington-street, was enjoy ing a ride on some hobby horses which ware stationed on the Market ground, when by some means he fell eff, and when examined by Dr. Valen- tine was found to have sustained a simple fracture of the leg. LECTURE AT VIADUCT INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>in another column an announcement is made tltat the lecture on "Oliver Cromwell" which should have been given in the Viaduct Institute on Tues- day evening next, is postponed indefinitely in con- sequence of the illness of the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones, the lecturer. We understand that Mr. Jones has had to cancel all engagamentA for two months hence, awl is now confined to his bed through a severe ifinoss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>1.0.G.T. EARLESTOWN ESENEZER LODGE.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. EARLESTOWN ESENEZER LODGE.</p>
        <p>This lodge held its usual weekly session on Tues- day. There was a very good attendance of friends and members, who were presided over by Bro. Morris, C.T. The roll of officer was called and the minutes of the last session read and adopted. Ttie Secretary read the digest of No. 1 Convention which is to be held at Wigan on the 12th, also the report of the district lodge which reporte 1 a substantial increase on the quarter. There were several motions of great interest to the lodge passed. A programme was afterwards submitted, all taking part. A vote of thanks to Bro. Price for his admirable programme brought a very enjoyable evening to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good lemplars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. The digest of the business for No. 1 Convention, to be held at Wigan, was read by the Secretary. Bro. E. Newman gave report of the lodge visit to St. Helens Great heart Lodge, and of the S.W. Lancashire District Lodge held at St. Helens on Saturday. The reports were adopted. A social evening was afterwards spent, refreshments being handed round.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>P.S.A. AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>P.S.A. AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The weekly gathering in connection with the Pleasant Sunday Afternoon Society, was held in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday. There was a crowded audience, and the meeting was presided over by Mr. Clough. After a brief address by the Chairman, an interesting sketch from the "Life of Nelsen," was given by Mr. Jackson which appeared to be greatly enjoyed. Solos by Mr. W. Monks contributed to a very pleasant afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ACCIDENT AT THE NEWTON BOTTLE WORKS,</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT THE NEWTON BOTTLE WORKS,</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon a lad named Frederick Wil- liams employed at the Bottle Works of Messrs. Borron and Co., Newton, met with an acciaent which might have been attended with very, serious results. He was wheeling a barrow full of broken glass, when he slipped, and falling forwards on the barrow, cut himself very badly on the neck. He bled profusely, and was sent in charge Df a lad younger than himself to Dr. Valentine who ex- pressed surprise at the lad having walked so far in such a condition, the wound being one requiring prompt attention. He dressed the wound, and al- though the lad suffered greatly he is now out of danger. DEATH OF THE TOWN HALL KEEPER.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon, Mr. John Parry, keeper of the Earlestown Town Hall, after a lingering ill- ness, from consumption, passed away. Mr. Parry was in his 48th year, and for 18 years had been a resident of Earlestown, having for two years been in the position he held at the time of his decease,he having previhmsly been employed by the Collins Green Colliery Co. Ile leaves a widow and four children. The funeral took place on Monday at the Cemetery,amongst those present being :—Mr. R, Brierley, Mr. W. Shirley, Mr. H. Houghton, Mr. Gregory and Mr. E. T. Evan.. He was a deacon of the Welsh Primitive Methodists, Earlestown, and most of the congregation attended the funeral, and the deceased's favourite hymn was sung at the •Srave side RATEPAYERS' MEETING LT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The adjourned meeting of ratepayers, which was called last week, was held in the Town Hall, New- ton, on Monday evening, but it was no better at- tended than the original meeting. Mr. James Sankey presided, and there were present—Mr. J. Simpson, Mr. J. Singleton, Mr. A. Anderton,and Hr. H. Houghton (assistant overseer). The meeting was called to take into consideratiol the applica- tion of the Newton-in-Alakerfield Urban District Council for an order under Section 33 of the Local Government Act of 1894, conferring upon them the powers of the vestry of the parish under se.ction 3 and 4of the Poor Rate Assessment and Collection Act, 1869. Without any preliminary. Mr. J. Single- ton moved that that meeting of ratepayers accede to the resolution passed by the District Council. This was seconded by Mr. Andenon, and carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>THE L. AND N.-W. AMBULANCE COMPETITION. The silver medals which were gained by the Earlestown Wagon Department team in the recent competitions in ambulance work amongst teams drawn from the whole of the L. and N.-W. system were presented to the members of the winning team—Messrs. Jolley, Waterman, Green, and Teece, by Mr. J. W. Emmett on Tuesday. Mr. Emmett in handing the men the medals briefly addressed them and they individually replied. The challenge shield, which was won by the team, is also on exhi- bition at the works. It is a fine piece of workman- ship, and mounted upon a suitable stand. Round the outer edge are 18 small plates, upon which the names of the teams by whom it will be won will be inscribed,the topmost and central plate bearing the name of the Earlestown team,who hold the honour- able position of first winners. In the centre of the shield is a plate,supported by a cherub representing the Angel of Mercy, and inscribed upon it is a train and station platform, upon which is an ambu- lance stretcher bearing a patient,with three men in attendance. It is beautifully embellished by scroll work, and below all are the arms of the L. and N.-W. Railway Co.,the design altogether being very fine and of massive appearance. POLICE COURT AND PRISON GATE MISSION, LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>In support of the above the Rev. J. Hirst, of Liverpool, preached in Wargrave Church, Earles- town, at the Sunday morning service. In the after- noon a short musical service was given by the choir when the following programme was submitted to a very fair audience : anthem," Oh, for a closer walk with God," (choir) organ solo " Communioi, in F" Mr. E. Leyland; solo "The Heavenly Song" Miss L. Gorse ; Irwin solo" 0 Sanctissima " with varia- tions and finale, Mr. H. Leyland • solo " A dream of Paradise" Mr. James Nichols ; anthem "Plead thou my cause," choir. An address was afterwards given by the Rev. J. arB gave some interesting, but pitiful de- tailsioftt' h who esocenes which come under the notice of the missionaries. On Monday night in connection with the same cause, an exhibition of lime light views was given in the Wargrave schools, the lantern being worked, and under the control of Mr. J. Lawson. The subject displayed on the sheet was the pathetic story of " Her Benny," the narrative being read by Mr. J. Taylor, who also gave one or two recitations. The Rev. J. Carson addressed a few words to those present in explanation of the cause which they were assembled to support. The total amount collec'ed was £4 16 s. CONCERT AND SUPPER IT PRESTON BROOK.</p>
        <p>The choir and friends connected with the Wes- leyan Chapel, on Friday evening, gave a succesQful concert, in the Chapel School, the room being filled. —The Rev. William George made a few remarks respecting the object for which the concert was held, and invited Captain Rodger to take the chair, which he did. The programme,which was a lengthy one, containing songs, duets, glee., instrumental pieces. &amp;c., was then proceeded with, the following ladies and gentlemen efficiently taking part :—The Misses Jones, Ground., F. Clare, Bertha Webb, and Messrs. R oughsedge,W ebb, Rosbottom, Miller,Ath er- ton, Okell. and Maddock. Mr. R. Johnson accom- panied on the piano, and at the conclusion of the concert a hearty vote was passed to the chairman The majority of the audience adjourned to another roona.where a substantial supper was awaiting them the Rev. W. George accompanying.—The evening's enjoyment was brought to a close by hearty ap- plause being given to the ladies and gentlemen who had Provided the entertainment and to those who had prepared the supper.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Wakefield received in one thy last week three anonymous donations, viz., £5OO for the Wakefield Spiritual Aid. £5OO for increasing the patronage of the see, and £lO,OOO for the formation of a new parish in any part of the diocese his lordship thinks desirable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>"WARRINGTON AT THE</head>
        <p>"WARRINGTON AT THE</p>
        <p>ASSIZES."</p>
        <p>SERMON BY THE REV. JOHN YONGE.</p>
        <p>Attention was drawn to the recent cases at the Liver- pool Assizes by the Rev. John Yonge, in Wycliffe Con- gregational Church on Sunday evening, when there was a crowded congregation. Ile chose as his text "They are out of the way through strong drink," Isaiah xxviii, part of the 4 v. He said it was a terribly sad thing that these words written seven centuries before Christ came should still be true of multitudes of men nearly nineteen centuries after His coming. Looked at in one way the fact was almost enough to kill the cause of temperance right out. It might be asked what hope could they have to-day of chocking,cripplingeand casting out the demon of drink which had tyrannized over mankind for those aireost innumerable generations ? That despairing ques- tion, however, might be still mare appropriately asked respecting the long and triumphant prevalence of evil in all its myriad forms. They might ask, " What use is it to combat the foe that is so deeply and strongly, entrenched, and so impregnably fortified, in individual men and society at large ? The answer to all such desperate questions was the answer of a living faith in the living God. We lived in the inspiring faith of the good time com- ing when all enemies should be destroyed, and as Chris- tian men and women we were called upon to do what we could to hasten on the good time coming. It was to be earnestly hoped that that day would have much to do in rallying the Christian forces of the country against the enemy of strong drink. It was Temperance Sunday. Hundreds of appeals would be made from the pulpits of nearly all Christian denominations upon that great national question. All such appeals would be' backed by cegent arguments and convincing facts that would go to prove the fierceness of this terrific evil, and the para- mount duty of Christ's followers to combine for its over- throw. Hero in Warrington they were presented with fresh and even appalling evidence of the greatness of the curse in their own community. It seemed to him that they ought to pause in front of the recent illustrations of the evil of drink,and seriously consider the testimony Whether they like it or not, the people of Warrineton had peculiar cause to study the question of drink. The drink factories of the town, from which a great many people besides those directly concerned in them derived pecuniary prefit, were always sending forth through Lancashire, Cheshire and North Walesethe drink that was the deadly enemy of humanity. They could not walk many yards in Lie,. cpeol, or St. Helens, or other towns without being reminded of Warrington, for they saw at almost every street corner the sign, " Warrington Ales." Moreover, complicity in this trade was not cen- fined to Warrington brewers or distillers, and they were constantly meetieg with even Warrington teetotallers who would tell them that they did not like to be too much in the front in temperance matters, as they were dependent for considerable custom on the brewing or distilling firms. Therefore he was justified in laying that the people in the:town ought to be made specially thought ful by the recent revelations at the Liverpool Assiz ,s. It is an indisputable fact that the criminal court of her Majesty's Assizes would become largely unnecessary if the drinking habits of the people could be overcome. The very olening, of that court wee a proclamation that , yet more victims of strong drink needed to be dealt with ' as a punishment for their offences and as a protection to society. If they questioned that assertion let them first take the trouble to read the reports of any assizes and they would question it no more. But in the last assizes at Liverpool the direful evil of drink had been for War- rington most painfully illustrated and emphasized. Three trials had occurred of Warrington men all of them for acts of violence smaller or greater, and not one of those three trials would have taken place had it not been for strong drink. What had been charitably designated a" drunken frolic," but still came perilously near a pos- itively tragic result, had been followed by a sentence of nine months' imprisonment. A second case, in which a husband maddened by drink threw a lighted paraffin lamp at his wife, by which she suffered fearful burns, received the sentence of three years' penal servitude. There they had two cases in which not only had. lives been imperilled, serious injuries inflicted, great pain and suffering caused, but homes and families had been sad- dened and humiliated, and had afirced to thorn for years to come the shame and stigma of public _disgrace. They were sorry for the men who, under the influence of that deaslef enemy of humanity, were driven to these evil deeds. They might say, perhaps, that they had merited the punishment that had been assigned them. And they might say so truly. But that was no reason why they should refuse them their compassion. Indeed, they deserved their compassion as much as they deserved the punishment the law had dealt them. " They were out of the way through strong drink." If that strong drink had not been se much and so easily in their way, probably it would never have turned them taut of the way. They had no right to be vindictive against their fellows who fell by strong drink,while they did so little to keep it out of their way, or even while they connived at so much beiug done to nut it in their way. They were their "ibrothers' keepers" in the mat- ter infinitely more than they thought. They had no right as citizens to allow the traps of the deadly foe of humanity to be at every street corner, to waylay a man at every hundred yards,to surround him,as was the case in the centre of the townoes completely thanon the testi mony of the Mayor himself, the centre of the town fre- quently became" a perfect hell." The community could alter this if it chose. So long as it suffered it to remain unaltered, so long must it expect " drunkea frolics" to more or less frequent loading to drunken scrimmages and fig,hts,wnich might imperil life and limb. No ! they ought both to be sorry for these men and to share the shame and humiliatioa they were called to endure. They had only fallen into the pit dug for them by the hands of the community. They had only fallen where, with similar tendencies, they themselves might have fallen. Let them, therefore, compassionate these men and feel for their families, who were mortified by their shame. These two cases,however, would probably have receives far less notoriety but for the other terribly tragic occur- rence that happened in Oliver-street. In that case War- rington was put face to face with the dread evil of drink in ghastlieet form and in a whole variety of its hideous aspects. For example, they were confronted with the chronic condition of some of the people, and of the houses and streets of the town. The first fact which came out was the habit,not only of the woman herself, but of a number of women living near, to meet in a neighbour's house to drink themselves drunk. It seemed not to have been an isolated case, but a weekly custom. What a revelation of the habit of a number of working- men's wives ! %V at a vision it gave,not only of drunken women but of wretched homes and neglected families,and of husbands driven to the public-house to get the food, warmth, and comfort denied them in their homes by the drunken habits of their wives ! Those who lived in decent surroundings had little con ception of the misery that dwelt in some of their streets, or of the fearful provocation that many husbands gave their wives, and many wives gave their husbands, through the habit of drink. It was not easy to say. which was the most evil, a drunken husband or a drunken wife. Perhaps of the two the latter was the Nernst ; for if the wife be drunken the children would be neglected, the home would go to wreck and ruin, and do as the husband 'might, if he be a workingman, obliged to be abroad at his work during the day. he could not do much to stem the stream of m'sery. Undoubtedly it must be a fearful trouble for a woman to have a drunken husband, to be left without money to carry on the house, teed and clothe the family ; and to have as well to contend with the maudlin besottodness or the brutal violence of the husband in his drink. No wonder the wife's heart broke under such a sorrow. What, how- ever, he had sometimes wondered at with utter amaze- ment had been a wife so situated still clinging to her own glass of beer, though she knew the sight and smell of it on her table was enough to send her husband off on a drunken spree! He had known Warrington women do that, and defend themselves for doing it. "She could drink her glass and keep sober; why couldn't he?" He contented that such selfishness was unwifely aid un- womanly. A question, however, that had run in his mind about the female drinking in Oliver-street was this —What about the people and the places where the liquor came from ? What about serving the children with drink who probably were sent to fetch it? What about serving women already drunk who wont to fetch it? These drunken orgies in houses such as Oliver-street could hardly be possible if the provisions of the Licens- ing Acts were always faithfully regarded and fulfilled. There could be no question that drink was often sup- plied in wrong methods, and that care was by no means always taken to guard against supplying it to drunken people. The whole of this part of the traffic needed care- ful revision and supervision ; for as long as a public- house manager was judged and esteemed by his em- ployers by the quantity of liquor he sold, so lone would he be tempted to get rid of it at every chance. Did not this ease preoent an urgent call to all Christian women who wished their sisters well, not only to abstain from drink themselves, but to use their every effort to promote temperance amongs women ? The British Women's Temperance Association should undertake some aggressive work, if possible. in the very locality of the tragedy to try and reclaim these womereFlow terrible was the revelation of the very wretchedness of a drunkard's home. Referring to the tragedy itself on the night in question, he said it was a scene he did not know how to paint. The man in the condemned cell, and the woman in her grave were the outcome of drink. The tragedy never would have occurred but for Veen He wanted the Warrington people to realise it. If ever an objsct lesson on the evils of drink were given it was given now. He had been told that a large number of drink sellers of the town attended the trial They were looking on at the fruits of their own busi- ness, Some of them surely must have felt inward com- punctions, and he was confident that many a publican wished he was out of the business. Not only was James Bate on his trial, but the whole drink traffic was on its trial, of which Bate, his wife and children were victims. Let those present that night beware lest they should fall from steadfastness, and be led captive by the demon at us will. Let them not say it was impossible. It was</p>
        <p>mpossible to become a victim of strong drink on one condition only and that was by never taking any of it Iu conclusion, the rev. gentleman made an urgent ap- peal to his hearers to keep out of the way of strong drink. At the conclusion of the service, a number of persona signed the pledge-book which was placed in the vestibule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>"CHILDREN AND PUBLIC-HOUSES."</head>
        <p>"CHILDREN AND PUBLIC-HOUSES."</p>
        <p>LECTURE AT SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Sankey Bricl-g-W- Wesleyan Band of Hope, the Rev. W. H. Drummond, president of the Warrington Temperance Federation, delivered a lec- tine, illustrated by lantern views, on the subject of "Children and Public -houses," in the Wesleyan School- room, Liverpool-road,on Wednesday evening. The chair was occupied by Councillor Thomas Burton. In the course of a few introductory remarks the Chair- man said lantern lectures were coming more and more to the front. By such, people had a better chance of re- taining in their memory what the lecturer was speaking about. He was pleased to see that the temperance movement had so many friends in the district. The Band of Hope movement was a step in the right direc- tion, and he was sure those who attended the meetings could not do so without profiting thereby. (Hear, hear.) There was plenty of room for aggressive temperance work in the Sankey Bridges district, and he was glad to see Mr. Drummend present that night. (Applause.) The Lecturer, who was cordially received, said he wished to speak of temperance legislation in relation to child life. It was a vastly important subject ; it was a practical subject. When he lett college some years ago i it was his (the lecturer's) great good fortune to be n- vited, not to the pulpit of one of those comfortable con- gregations which were supposed (but in many cases how falsely) to be the first ambition of budding ecclesiastics, but to the charge of a mission in one of the most neg- lected spots of squalid Liverpool. It was a neighbour- hood with every facility for the manufacture of drunkards and criminals. The pitifulness of child life struck him forcibly. How was it possible, he kept ask- ing himself, for children to grow up with a child's innocence and simplicity,to say nothing of a child's joy- ousness, in such surroundings? The most debasing influence was the public houseeieliberately setting itself to train the child into a good customer almost as soon as it could toddle. It was a familiar sight to see little ones, so small that they could hardly clasp the bottle in their arms, going to and from the public-house and if you watched them closely you might see them taking stolen sips of the liquor on their way home. This went on all week and at almost every hour of the day, but on Sunday afternoon just before closing time, the street seemed to swarm with these juvenile liquor car- riers. As he passed along the street, and peered into the half-opened doors, he could see the steps provided so that quite a tiny child could reach up to the counter.and not infrequently he heard of presents given as a bonne bouche to encourage the small customer to return on a similar errand as soon as possible. Did they wonder that he came to the conclusion that this frequenting of public-houses by children was a fertile source of juvenile disease and depravity, that it was one of toe best methods of creating drunkards that could possibly be devised; that it was a habit which demandel the most determined opposition on the part of these who be- lieved that the soul of a little child was one of the most precious gifts of God ? The evidence of statis- tics showed a still more terrible tale. A census taken in regard to 200 public-houses in London showed that in three hours on a Saturday evening, 7,019 chil- dren ent,ered as customers. That all the drink thus sold was not for adult consumption at home was proved by appalling facts. In 169 C, the Head Constable of Liver- pool reported that during the preceding twelve months five males were locked up for being drunk and disorderly of from 14 to 16 years of age and three females. Of per- sons from 16 to 18, there had been 833 males, and 408 females, or 1,438 arrested under the age of 21 for drun• kermess. In London, in the same year, 500 children under ten'years of age were taken up in a state of intoxi- cation; 1,500 under :4 ; 2,000 under 21, total 4,000. He knew of no cure for this evil but legislative interfer- ence. Society must step in to protect the children from their parents, their surroundings, and their inherited cravings. To permit this wholesale manufacture of drunkards was bad economy, and it was worse Chris- tianity. In the season of 1893 a bill to prohibit the sale of intoxicating liquors to children was introduced into the House of Commons. It met with the usual fate of bills framed in order to stem the tide of great secial evils. It was not passed. He knew that an Act of Parliament with the severest penalties would not work a complete cure, but it would afford an important measure of relief and protection ; and he wanted to urge them to support a bill for the protection of children, by whatever influence they pos- sessed. To be kindly sensitive to the evil was in itself something. No reform was ever carried till it was backed by earnest men and women. For the rest they must use whatever educational influence they could. Plain talks to young-people, especially to the future mothers might be of great service. They would probably have to wait a good while longer before they got adequate temperance teaching in the elementary school. Let them see to it that it was given with all earnestness in the Sunday school, and that from time to time a word in season was spoken of that subtle and widely ramifying danger—this vast social conspiracy against the sweet simplicity of English childhood-. (Applause.) The lecture was aptly illustrated by views taken in Liverpool showing the children's connection with public houses. The pictures were shown by Mr. Wilkinson. At the close of the lecture, a vote of thanks to the lecturer and chairman was unanimously passed, on the motion of Mr. W. Brough, seconded by Mr. Blythe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>PAAR HALL CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>THE PAAR HALL CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>Another of the popular evening concerts was held in Parr Hall, Warrington, on Saturday. The attendance was not quite so large as usual but the concert itself was the best which had been given this season, and included the following artistes :—Soprano. Miss Edith Williams, Liverpool ; contralto, Miss May Pierpoint, Warrington ; Mandoliniste. Mille. Marienne Tostea,, Paris; the " Tostea " ladies mandoline orchestra ; baritone, Mr. Alec Wood, of the northern concerts ; and humorist. Mr. Leslie Harris. The programme of songs &amp;c., proved to be well chosen and the various items were well suited to the artistes. La Tostea's mandoline orchestra of eight performers opened the concert by playing a pretty musical selection. The twang ef the mandolinea in con- cert was quite a novelty and as such did not fail to please. On their second appearance the performance of the mandolinists met with such appreciation as to be encored. Their playing is 'very combined and shows a precision only acquired by. practice and individual skill. Miss Edith Williams signalised her appearance by a ren- I dition of 'Lane's " Carrnencita," but the song suited neither the singer nor the audience. " The Carnival," (Molloy), was much prettier and Miss Williams sang it tastefully and well. She was called upon to repeat the last verse, and completed her share of the pro- gramme, leaving a good impression upon her hearers. Mr. Leslie Harris was the draw of the evening, and his refined and subtle wit gave greater satisfaction than that of any of his predecessors this season. Mr. Barris instantly calla up recollections of Gressmith and is the nearest and best approach to that inimitable humorist that Warrineton has ever seen. "It does go " (SPurr) was the title of his first item, a Song, and it did go, well indeed that nothing would satisfy but a re-appear- ance. Altogether Mr. Harris occupied the platform four times and en one occasion he so delighted his hearers that he was cheered and cheered again until he made three responses. A musical sketch " The Choral Society" (Grossmith) was a delightful piece of mimi- cry. "Her golden hair was hanging down her back," ala Mendellssehn, Chopin, Mozart and Liszt, bn the piano,showed another of the talented composer's accom- lishments. Other items were" Unmusical Music," "The fashion is Recitation," " A Drama in a Nutshell," "Laughing Song," and "The Dead Dolly." Screams of laughter greeted Mr. Harries recital of " The Dead Dolly," showing how '6 poor Hildegarde died of a broken heart and a great big whack on the head." It was the funniest apeing of a child's troubles that any humorist has attempted since these concerts began. No one will be sorry to know how soon Mr. Plarris is back again. Miss May Pierpoint, eldest daughter of Councillor Pierpoint, J.P., also met with a very cordial reception. Her deeo and clear contralto sounded well in "The Clang of the Wooden Shoon," (Molloy), and "The Silver Rine," (Chaminade), more especially in the latter. Both songs were heartily en- cored, but "The Silver Ring" was undoubtedly the prettiest and most sweetly rendered song of the evening. Since last season Mdlle. Marienne Tostea has become a prime favourite in Warrington, and she received most flattering appreciation of everything she attempted on Saturday. De Beriet's "Premier Concerto," and " Fan- tasie Trovatere," (Verdi-Allard) were finished perform- ances and it was all the more creditable to Mdlle.Testea's skill on the mandoline that she never needed a line of printed music. The orchestral selection in the second half of the programme owed much of its finish to her masterly leading. The only male singer was Mr. Alec Wood,and it caneot be said that he added much to the attractive- ness of the concert. His voice is not a good baritene and "The Vagabond" (Molloy) did not meat with much applause at his hands. However he gave a creditable rendition of" Off to Philadelphia," as his second song. Mr. W. H. Payton (Warrington) accompanied Miss Pier- peint,hut upon Miss Sophie Roberts (Liverpool) fell the arduous task of playing the piano music to all the other artistes. It can fairly be said that she was fully equal to it and evinced natural talent in accompanying even I such difEcult pieces as Mdlle. Tostea's trying concertos. etc. Miss Roberts merits her engagement for the same work at to-night's concert.</p>
        <p>WARRINGrON.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO THE WARRINGTON FEVER VAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon as a spring cart belonging to a hawker named William Riley was turning out of Pinners'-brow by the Rodney public house, it collided with the van used for conveying patients to the Warrington Fever Hospital, and which was coming along Winvvick-road. Inside the van were a woman and her child. One of the shafts of Riley's cart crashed into the driver's seat and right through the van. Fortunately both occupants were lying down' and the shaft passed over their heads, otherwise they would have been seriously injured. Riley's harness was damaged, but not his cart, and the van was able to proceed on its journey,although the worse for the collision. ST. GEORVE'S DAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A very successful concert was given on Monday evening by the infants attending this school. The Rev. G. B.Thurston presided. The programme con- sisted of musical drill ; with dumb-bells and coloured scarves, action songs, and recitations, all of which were given in a manner that reflected great credit on the teachers, and elicited most hearty applause from the large audience. The children when arranged on the platform presented a pleasing appearance. Miss Bertha Smith, of St. Helens, who is a brilliant young pianist°, gave two pianoforte solos; and Miss Winnie Pigot, of War- rington, delighted the audience by contributing two solos, violin and moadoline, in her usual feel- ing and artistic manner. CONCERT AT ST. PAUL'S SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The third of the weekly entertainments was given last Saturday in the Infant School, Kendrick-street, before a good audience. The concert opened with a piano solo, " Tarantelle," by Mr. H. Everett ; and was followed by the Rev. H. Brierley singing " The Bell Ringee " (Wallace). Miss G. Finney then played an intermezzo on the violin, and Miss Ash urst read with her usual excellence " The one. legged goose." The Orph-us Glee Party sang" Oh fly with me " (Meudelssohn), for which they were loudly applauded and recalled. Miss May Jones gave a brillant rendering of Paderewski's 'Minuet," and was encored. Miss Codrington, sang with her usual charming style Cowen's " Swallows "; and Mr. Everett followed with another pianoforte solo. After the interval Miss May Jones played on the piano a pas redouble. The Orpheus Glee Party sang "Over her grave" (Mendelssohn) ; and Miss J. Finney next gave a violin solo." Elyn Gavotte," for which she was warmly applauded. Miss Codrington, who had another good reception, sang "All Souls' Day " (Ed. Lassen), and the Rev. H. Priestly received an encore for singing " The Postillion." The programme closed with a song and chorus " The interfering Parrot," Miss, Arm- strong singing the • solo. The audience demanded an encore, which waq given, thus bringing to a finish a most enjoyable evening. CHESHIRE AND LANCASHIRE VOLUNTEER INFAN- TRY BRIGADIC.—SERGEANTS' ANNUAL DINNER. The fourth annual dinner in connection with the above brigade was held on Saturday at the Arm- oury, Stockport, Quartermaster Sergeant Rough ton (4th Cheshire), of Cheadle, in the chair, and Quar- termaster Sergeant Ogden (2nd Fusiliers) and Hospital Sergeant Swift in the vice-chairs. The following is a list of those who attended from the Warrington Corps :—Captain Appleton, Captain and Quartermaster Hutchinson, Sergeant-Major Sulli- van, Sergeant-Instructor Barker, Sergeant-Instruc- to. Killen, Quartermaster-Sergeant Potter, Bugle- Major Plinston, Orderlyroom-Sergeant Bennion, Bandmaster Thomas Armstrong, Colour-Sergeants Wood, Jolley, Spencer, Sergeants Connor, Thomas, Rooks, Dutton, J. W. Plinston, Kermode, Russell. Ashworth, Devonport, Jones, Whitham, Hunt,Bram- hall, Whitfield, Cliffs, Colour-Sergeant W. Plinston (retired), and Sergeant W. Hudson (retired). Great praise is due to Quartermaster-Sergeant Potter, of Warrington, hon. secretary to the brigade, for mak- ing the necessary arrangements for the annual din- ner. It may be interesting to note that the brigade is made up of battalions from Stockport, Bury, Rochdale, Salford, St. Helens, Knutsford, Warring- ton, Macclesfield, and other places, and that the strength is from 5,000 to 6,000 officers and men. An extensive toast list was gone through and interest- ing speeches given. Songs were also rendered, and a happy evening spent.</p>
        <p>"OUR INDUSTRIAL SYSTRIII.-HOW IT WORKS AND WHO WORKS IT."</p>
        <p>The above was the subject of a lecture by Mr. James Sexton, of Liverpool, delivered under the auspices of the I.L.P. in the Hruid's Hall, Sankey- street, on Sunday evening. Mr. A. Appleton occu- pied the chair. The lecturer in answering com- ments on holding Sunday meetings remarked that the Socialists worked for humanity, and he con- sidered that was the only religion worth working for. He contrasted the life and practice of Jesus with the respectable church going of professing christians who all the week through were, he ar- gued, engaged in a cutting competition with their fellows which resulted in the crushing of the mass of the people. Coming to the immediate subject of the lecture, he showed that the private monopoly of the land was the root cause of all monopoly. The lecturer proceeded to give many practical examples showing the condition of the people and the Effect of the monopolies of land and the means of produc- tion on the work,health, and morality of the nation. He contended that this monopoly and the competi- tion system under which industry was carried on was responsible for the problems which surround us to-day, and advocated the collective ownership of the means of production for the benefit of the community. Ile contended that the laws which upheld the present state of things could be altered by the use of the ballot box,and expressed the hope and belief that the people would see to it that they used their votes to bring about such a condition of things. As a trade unionist,he demonstrated how the trade unions at present had it in their power as a body to send to Parliament a sufficient number of members to completely alter the conditions under which they existed. There was a good attendance and the lecturer answered several questions. A LOCAL FIRM'S COMING OF AGE.</p>
        <p>A gathering of more than usual interest, was held at Hull a few days ago, when the well-known Public Benefit Boot Company, of which Mr. W. H. Franklin is the founder, celebrated its 21st anniver- sary. The celebrations took the form of a dinner and dance, to which upwards of 120 ladies and gentlemen were invited. Amongst the gentlemen present representing the chief departments through- out the kingdom, was Mr. G. Kirby, of Warrinwton, whose place of business is in Bridge-street. Some interesting information with respect to the firm was given by several speakers during the evening. The founder (Mr. W. H. Franklin) said nothing gave him greater pleasure than to look back on their small shop opposite the Hull Infirmary in 1875, which had culminated in the erection of their large premises at Albion-street corner. The small seed had not only taken root in Hull,but had spread out its branches in other large towns, such as Derby, Nottingham, Sheffield, Birmingham, Coven- try, Warrington, Leeds, etc. He was delighted to see the heads of all those establishments present that night. They had given him the appellation of father of the Company, whilst he considered they were the vigorous sons, and from their energy they had buiP,-, up extensive establishments in other towns.—Mr. G. E. Franklin mentioned that the firm now employed 2,000 hands altogether in the manufacturing,retailing and managing departments. No less than twelve million pairs of boots had been sold by them,and these if stretched out in a straight line, would reach 3,409 miles, viz., ten times the length of England. If they continued in the same way during the next 21 years. the hoots sold would stretch from the North to South Pole. (Laughter and applause).</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION: LANCASHIRE SECTION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held in Manchester on Thursday night the following referees and touch judges were appointed for matches on December 12 : —Competition matches: Broughton Rangers v. St. Helens, referee J. H. Smi:h (Widnes), touch judges F. Hancock (Runcorn) and G. Tinge (Salford) ; Widnes v. Tyldeeley, referee H. Court (Oldham), touch jiidges E Beswick (Swinton) and G. Skidmore (Broughton Rangers) ; Wigan v. Warrington, referee J. Priestley (Broughton Rangers), touch judges T, Mellidew (Rochdale) and J. Graham (St. Helens) ; Leigh v. Oldham, referee J. VV. Griffiths (Rochdale), touch judges C. Hardman (Tyldesley) and J. M'Gowan (Broughton Rangers) ; Rochdale Hornets v. Stockport, referee G. Wainwright (Runcorn). touch judges R. S. Davies (Broughton Rangers) and A. Hope (Swinton) ; Swinton v. Morecambe, referee J. Underwood (Wigan), touch judges J. Edwards (St. Helens) and E. Buckley (Rochdale Hornets) ; Salford v. Runcorn, referee F. furner (Warrington),teuch judges A. Critchley (Widnes) and R. Moore (Oldham). Non-competition matches: Crompton v. Werneth, referee W. Dillon (Warrington) ; St. Helens Recreation v. Walkden, referee T. Kent (Radcliffe) ; Dukinfied v. Rochdale St. Clement's.referee A. Smith (St. Hel-ns Recreation) ; Radcliffe v. Swinton A, referee R. Nuttall (Crompton).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-12-04_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT.—On Wednesday morning, a sad accident betel William Hope, of Heath-road, Weston, whilst fol- lowing his employment as regulator of the acid vats at the Weston Chemical Works. He was regulating thm acids in the vats, when some of the boiling liquid splashed in his face, and badly burned him about the face and neck. Fortunately his eyes escaped injury.</p>
