        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY LECTURES.- The Third Lecture will be delivered on FRIDAY, V.ecember 4th. in the Technical Institute. by W. I. ARGENT, !sq., of Liverpool. Subject: "HOW OUR FATHERS rLAYED AND SANG." Chair to be taken at Eight o'clock, by SAMUEL POPE, Esq.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PRIZE CHOIR-SEASON 1896-7. GRAND PERFORMANCE OF HANDEL'S "JUDAS MACCABAEUS," IN THE PUBLIC HALL, RUNCORN, on WEDNESDAY NEXT, DECEMBER 9rn, 1896. Doors open at 7 to commence at 7.38. PraNcipsis:—Miss ANNI NORLEDGIR (of the London Concerts). Miss MAUDE ROBERTSON (the noted Scottish Vontralto). Mr. GWILYM RICHARDS (of the London Concerts). Mr. WILLIAM ROBERTS (the famous Yorkshire tasso). Full Band and Chorus. Leader of the Band, Mr. J. W. Coninson ; organist, Mr. William Cooke, A.R.C.O. ; conductor, Mr. John Holford, L.T.S.C. Admission 35.. 25.. and 6d.</p>
        <p>NTATIONAL ,:-OCIETY FOR TILE PREVENTION OF CRUELTY TO CHILDREN. (Warrington and District Brands.) A PUBLIC BALL, Will be held at the LION HOTEL, On THURSDAY, 10T DECEMBER. 1896, in aid of the above Society. TICKETS 7s. 6d. each. be obtained from MR. it. H. PEARSON, Bold-street, Warrington. DANCING 8-30 to 1 o'clock. It is hoped that all will support the Ball to help the funds of the Society.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION. A PUBLIC MEETING will be held on MONDAY EVENING, DECEMBER 7TH, 1896, IN THE CO-OPERATIVE HALL, SANKEY-STRTET, when COUNTESS ALICE KEARNEY, ME. ARTHUR H. CROSFIELD, and others will speak. °hair to be taken at 7-45 p.m. by Miss BESSIE DUTTON. The proceedings will be interspersed with music. The first four rows of seats will be reserved at a charge of Sixpence each. Tickets to be had from Mr. Mitchell, at the Liberal Club or from any member of the Committee. Admission free. A DANCE under the auspioes of the above Association will be held on JANUARY 19TH, 1897.</p>
        <p>DOMESTIC SCHOLARSHIPS. The COUNTY COUN- CIL offers for Competition, a number of SCHOLARSHIPS providing instruction in Dairy-work, Dressmaking, Cookery, Laundry-work, and tenable at the Dairy Institute, Worleston for nine weeks, commenc- ing January 4th, 1897. Particulars aad forms of applica- tion can be obtained from the under signed R. P. WARD, Organizing Secretary. Crewe. RUNCORN UNION SCHOOLNIISTRES.—The Guardians require a Schoolmistress for tke Girls' School at the Workhouse, Dutton. Salary £33 per annum With Board, washing, and Furnished Apartments in the Workhouse, together with such further sum as may be %Warded by the Certificate of the Local Government Board. Candidates must be unmarried or widows without children dependent upon them. The officer appointed will be required to take the entire Charge of the children out of school hours, superint,nd their industrial training, and give instruction in knitting and eeedlework. The qmointment will be subject to the approval of the Dee,l Government Board. Applications in the handwriting of the candidates stating age, class of certificite, and present and p evious occupations eceompanied by copies of te.timonials of recent date (net exceeding three in number) to be sent to me not later than WEDNESDAY the 16th December 1896. The appointment will be made on TUBSDAY the 22nd December, 1896, and selected candidates will receive notice to attend on that day, and thizd class return fares will be paid. By Order, JOHN ASHTON, Clerk to the Guardians. Runcorn, Ist December, 1896. DANCING.—MASONIC HALL, WARRINGTON. Mrs. DOLE'S PRIVATE CLASSES have resumed. Ralf-term commences Wednesday, November 25th. Private lessons by appointment. PRODSHAM visited —Address bURWENT HOUSE, Brougham-terrace, Liverpool.</p>
